INSERT OR REPLACE INTO scene_synopses (work_abbrev, div1, div2, synopsis) VALUES
('1H4', 1, 1, 'King Henry IV opens his court expressing his wish to lead a crusade to the Holy Land, but news arrives of trouble on two fronts: Mortimer has been captured by the Welsh rebel Glendower, and Hotspur (Henry Percy) refuses to hand over his Scottish prisoners. Henry summons Hotspur to court to explain himself. Henry laments that his own son, Prince Hal, is an irresponsible wastrel compared to the heroic Hotspur.'),
('1H4', 1, 2, 'Prince Hal and his fat, witty companion Sir John Falstaff joke together in a London tavern. Falstaff grumbles about his dissolute life and asks what Hal plans to do when he becomes king. Their friend Poins arrives with a plan to rob some travelers at Gadshill; Falstaff eagerly agrees, but Hal privately tells Poins he plans to abandon Falstaff at the robbery and watch what happens. Hal closes the scene with a soliloquy revealing that he intends eventually to reform and surprise everyone with his true kingly nature.'),
('1H4', 1, 3, 'King Henry confronts Hotspur and his father Northumberland and uncle Worcester about the withheld prisoners. Hotspur argues he was provoked by a foppish royal messenger and did not refuse outright. Henry demands the prisoners and leaves. Worcester reveals a plan: they will ally with the disgraced Mortimer, Glendower in Wales, and the Douglas in Scotland to overthrow Henry. Hotspur, fired by ambition, agrees enthusiastically.'),
('1H4', 2, 1, 'A group of carriers prepare to leave an inn at Rochester before dawn, grumbling about the poor conditions. The thief Gadshill meets the Chamberlain and gets intelligence about wealthy travelers on the road. The carriers depart, and Gadshill sets off to join the robbery crew.'),
('1H4', 2, 2, 'Falstaff, Gadshill, Bardolph, and Peto wait on the road at Gadshill to rob the travelers. Prince Hal and Poins hide nearby. The robbery goes smoothly, but then Hal and Poins, disguised and masked, attack and rob Falstaff and the others. Falstaff and his companions run away in panic, leaving the money behind.'),
('1H4', 2, 3, 'Hotspur reads a letter from a lord who refuses to join the rebellion, and he dismisses the man as a coward. His wife Kate notices that Hotspur has been distracted and sleepless and begs to know what secret plan he is pursuing. Hotspur affectionately but firmly refuses to tell her, saying only that she must travel separately to join him in Wales.'),
('1H4', 2, 4, 'In the Boar''s Head Tavern, Prince Hal and Poins tease the tapster Francis. When Falstaff arrives, he boasts of his bravery during the robbery, wildly exaggerating the number of men he fought. Hal exposes Falstaff''s lies by revealing that it was he and Poins who robbed him. Falstaff cleverly claims he recognized Hal and didn''t want to harm the prince. News arrives that a rebellion has broken out and Hal must answer to his father; they play-act a mock interview between father and son, with Falstaff defending himself as Hal''s companion. The sheriff comes looking for Falstaff, and Hal hides him behind the arras.'),
('1H4', 3, 1, 'The rebel leaders Hotspur, Glendower, and Mortimer meet in Wales to plan the rebellion and divide England among themselves after the expected victory. Hotspur quarrels with Glendower over the division of the map and mocks Glendower''s belief in prophecy and magic. The mood softens as Mortimer and his Welsh wife say a tender farewell, and Hotspur and Lady Percy share a playful exchange before departing for battle.'),
('1H4', 3, 2, 'King Henry privately rebukes Prince Hal, saying that his reckless behavior has squandered his princely reputation and made him look like Richard II before his fall. Hal sincerely apologizes and vows to redeem himself by defeating Hotspur in battle. Henry is moved and gives Hal command of a portion of the royal army. News arrives that the rebels have assembled a powerful force.'),
('1H4', 3, 3, 'Back at the Boar''s Head Tavern, Falstaff jokes with Bardolph and accuses the Hostess of picking his pocket; Hal arrives and reveals the charges are false. Hal tells Falstaff that he has secured him a command of foot soldiers. Falstaff boasts that he will prove himself in battle.'),
('1H4', 4, 1, 'At the rebel camp near Shrewsbury, Hotspur and Douglas receive a letter from Northumberland saying he is ill and cannot join them. Worcester fears this weakens their cause, but Hotspur defiantly insists they press on. A messenger reports that Glendower also cannot come with his forces. Hotspur remains determined, though their army is now significantly smaller than expected.'),
('1H4', 4, 2, 'On the road near Coventry, Falstaff reveals that he has abused his authority to recruit soldiers: he took bribes from able-bodied men to let them off and conscripted only the most pitiful and ragged recruits. Hal and Westmorland ride by and Hal scolds Falstaff for his disreputable soldiers. Falstaff shrugs it off, saying they are good enough to die in battle.'),
('1H4', 4, 3, 'Near the rebel camp, Hotspur, Worcester, and Douglas debate whether to attack the king''s forces immediately or wait for reinforcements. Sir Walter Blunt arrives as an envoy from King Henry offering to hear their grievances and grant pardons. Hotspur recounts a long list of wrongs Henry has done to the Percy family, but says he will send Worcester to the king with their answer in the morning.'),
('1H4', 4, 4, 'The Archbishop of York, fearing the rebellion may fail, sends letters to his allies urging them to prepare defenses. He acknowledges that the rebel army is not at full strength and worries about what will happen if Hotspur is defeated.'),
('1H4', 5, 1, 'On the morning of the battle at Shrewsbury, Worcester and Vernon meet with King Henry, who offers full pardon if the rebels stand down. Prince Hal honors Hotspur by offering to settle the dispute in single combat, sparing both armies the bloodshed. Worcester and Vernon return to the rebel camp. Falstaff and Hal exchange words about honor; Falstaff delivers his famous cynical speech arguing that honor is merely a word and not worth dying for.'),
('1H4', 5, 2, 'Worcester decides not to tell Hotspur about the king''s offer of pardon, fearing Henry will not keep his word and that their own positions will be insecure even if pardoned. He lies to Hotspur, saying the king was contemptuous and Prince Hal''s offer of combat was mocking. Hotspur is roused to fury and calls his men to battle.'),
('1H4', 5, 3, 'The battle of Shrewsbury begins. King Henry has dressed several knights in his royal armor, and Douglas kills more than one of them. Falstaff enters the field armed only with a bottle of sack instead of a pistol. The battle is chaotic and bloody. Blunt is killed by Douglas, who mistakes him for the king.'),
('1H4', 5, 4, 'Prince Hal fights heroically on the battlefield, even rescuing his father from Douglas. Hal and Hotspur finally meet in single combat and Hal kills Hotspur. Falstaff, who had been lying on the ground feigning death to avoid Douglas, rises and stabs Hotspur''s corpse, then claims the kill as his own. Hal is amused and allows Falstaff his lie.'),
('1H4', 5, 5, 'After the battle, Worcester and Vernon are captured and sentenced to death for starting the rebellion by lying about the king''s pardon. The Douglas is captured by Prince John and, at Hal''s request, is released without ransom as a mark of honor. Henry sends his forces in different directions to deal with the remaining rebel threats: Northumberland and the Archbishop of York.'),
('1H6', 1, 1, 'The great lords of England gather at the funeral of King Henry V. News arrives in rapid succession that English territories in France have been lost and that the dauphin Charles has been crowned king of France. Winchester and Gloucester quarrel bitterly over who holds more power. A messenger arrives with word that Talbot, England''s greatest warrior, has been captured at Patay due to cowardly retreat by Sir John Fastolf.'),
('1H6', 1, 2, 'Outside Orléans, the French leaders Charles, Alençon, and Reignier discuss the siege. A messenger announces that a young woman from Lorraine, Joan la Pucelle (Joan of Arc), claims divine inspiration and wishes to speak with Charles. Joan meets Charles, defeats him in single combat, and convinces him she is heaven-sent and will drive out the English. The French leaders are captivated by her confidence and agree to follow her.'),
('1H6', 1, 3, 'In London, Gloucester''s servants try to enter the Tower of London but are refused entry by Winchester''s men. Gloucester and Winchester arrive and trade insults; their followers brawl in the streets. The Mayor of London intervenes and orders them to stop fighting. The quarrel between Gloucester and Winchester continues to simmer.'),
('1H6', 1, 4, 'Before Orléans, the aged English hero Talbot recounts to the Earl of Salisbury how he escaped captivity through a prisoner exchange. They climb a tower to survey the city below. Salisbury and Sir Thomas Gargrave are struck by a French cannon shot; Salisbury dies from his wound. A scout reports the French are advancing, led by the Dauphin and Joan of Arc.'),
('1H6', 1, 5, 'Talbot fights Joan of Arc before the walls of Orléans. Joan holds her own against him and he is astonished by her skill, but eventually she and the French forces drive the English back. Joan leads the French in recapturing Orléans. The French celebrate their victory.'),
('1H6', 1, 6, 'The French leaders, elated by their victory, celebrate the recapture of Orléans and praise Joan''s heroism. Charles declares Joan will be honored above all women in France. The mood is triumphant as the French savor their success.'),
('1H6', 2, 1, 'Talbot and the English forces mount a surprise night attack on Orléans, catching the French off guard. Charles, Joan, and the other French leaders flee from their beds in panic and disarray. Talbot recaptures the city and orders the trumpets to sound in victory. Salisbury''s body is brought in, and Talbot vows to have a monument built in his honor.'),
('1H6', 2, 2, 'Inside Orléans, Talbot and the English lords pay tribute to the fallen Salisbury. A messenger brings news that the Countess of Auvergne wishes to meet Talbot, whom she admires as a great warrior. Talbot agrees to visit her, whispering instructions to a captain before he leaves.'),
('1H6', 2, 3, 'The Countess of Auvergne has plotted to trap Talbot: she has him seized the moment he arrives, mocking him for being smaller than she imagined. Talbot laughs and blows his horn; his soldiers burst in, surrounding the castle. The Countess realizes she has only captured his shadow and not his true strength, and she graciously admits defeat and entertains him hospitably.'),
('1H6', 2, 4, 'In the garden of the Temple in London, a quarrel over a legal question escalates between Richard Plantagenet and the Earl of Somerset. Unable to resolve it with words, they invite their peers to declare sides by plucking either a white or red rose — white for Plantagenet, red for Somerset. Warwick, Suffolk, and others take sides. Vernon and a lawyer choose the white rose. The quarrel marks the birth of the faction that will become the Wars of the Roses.'),
('1H6', 2, 5, 'Richard Plantagenet visits his dying great-uncle Mortimer in the Tower, where Mortimer has been imprisoned for years. Mortimer recounts the long history of his claim to the throne through the Yorkist line, explaining how Richard''s father was executed for treason. Mortimer urges Richard to be cautious and reclaim the family''s honor carefully. Mortimer dies, and Richard resolves to restore the Plantagenet name.'),
('1H6', 3, 1, 'Parliament is in session in London. Gloucester and Winchester continue their feud; their followers fight in the streets with stones and clubs until the king orders the city gates closed. Young Henry VI pleads for peace between the lords. Winchester grudgingly accepts a superficial reconciliation but aside reveals his continuing hatred of Gloucester. Henry restores Richard Plantagenet''s title as Duke of York. News arrives that all of France is nearly lost.'),
('1H6', 3, 2, 'Joan of Arc devises a ruse: she and French soldiers disguise themselves as merchants and gain entry to the walled city of Rouen. They signal for the French army, which then storms and takes the city. Talbot challenges the French to open battle, but Joan refuses to come out. Bedford, the English regent, insists on watching the battle from his sickchair and dies during the fighting. The French mock the English from the walls but then are themselves driven out; the English retake Rouen.'),
('1H6', 3, 3, 'Joan speaks privately with the Duke of Burgundy, who has long been England''s most important ally in France. Through emotional appeals about France''s suffering, she persuades Burgundy to abandon his English alliance and rejoin the French cause. Burgundy agrees and switches sides. Talbot, learning of the defection, curses Burgundy as a traitor.'),
('1H6', 3, 4, 'King Henry VI has come to France and is in Paris. Talbot is brought before him and given the title Earl of Shrewsbury as reward for his services. Vernon and Basset quarrel again over the red and white roses; Henry puts the dispute off and picks a red rose to show his impartiality, not understanding the faction he has inadvertently fueled.'),
('1H6', 4, 1, 'In Paris, Henry VI is crowned King of France. Fastolf arrives with a letter from Burgundy formally announcing his defection to the French side. Talbot tears the Order of the Garter from Fastolf''s leg for his past cowardice at Patay and Fastolf is banished. York and Somerset argue bitterly about who must supply Talbot with reinforcements, each blaming the other and refusing to cooperate. Henry pleads for unity, but the factional hatred is too deep.'),
('1H6', 4, 2, 'Talbot is besieging Bordeaux when the French general on the walls announces that he is surrounded: Joan and the French army are advancing from behind, and French forces inside the city will sally forth. Talbot is trapped. He sends for help from York and Somerset, but the reinforcements are delayed because of the rivalry between those lords.'),
('1H6', 4, 3, 'York blames Somerset for not sending cavalry to support Talbot. A messenger reports Talbot is in desperate need. York furiously accuses Somerset of letting Talbot die to spite him.'),
('1H6', 4, 4, 'Lucy appeals to Somerset for reinforcements for Talbot. Somerset blames York for the failure to help and refuses to move quickly. Lucy is dismayed, fearing Talbot and all the English forces will be destroyed by the delay.'),
('1H6', 4, 5, 'On the battlefield near Bordeaux, Talbot urges his young son John Talbot to flee and save himself. Young Talbot refuses to abandon his father and insists on staying to fight, saying flight would bring shame on both of them. Old Talbot is proud of his son''s courage even as the situation grows desperate.'),
('1H6', 4, 6, 'Old Talbot rescues his son from French captivity during the battle. He again urges young John to flee, but John again refuses, saying his father''s life is worth more than his own. The two fight on together.'),
('1H6', 4, 7, 'Young John Talbot is slain in battle. Old Talbot holds his son''s body in his arms and then dies of his wounds, his son in his arms. Lucy arrives and asks the French about the fallen Talbots; Joan and the French lords mock their titles and names dismissively. Burgundy rebukes Joan for her mockery of great men.'),
('1H6', 5, 1, 'In London, the Pope''s legate and the Emperor''s ambassador urge peace between England and France, and Cardinal Winchester (formerly Bishop) agrees to negotiate. York receives letters ordering him to arrange a truce and bring Joan of Arc prisoner to London if she is captured. In France, York captures Joan after defeating her supernatural helpers (spirits who abandon her). Joan is taken prisoner.'),
('1H6', 5, 2, 'The French forces under Charles discuss the truce with Winchester. Charles initially refuses but eventually agrees to accept peace terms under which France acknowledges English overlordship in exchange for keeping his French titles.'),
('1H6', 5, 3, 'Suffolk captures Margaret of Anjou during the fighting and is immediately captivated by her beauty. He wants to woo her for himself but he is married; instead he decides to arrange her marriage to King Henry VI so he can remain close to her and rule England through her. He persuades Margaret and her father Reignier to agree.'),
('1H6', 5, 4, 'Joan of Arc is brought before York and Warwick for trial. She first claims her father is a shepherd; when he arrives she disowns him as too lowly, claiming instead a divine heritage. When told she will be burned, she claims to be pregnant to gain a stay of execution, naming several different fathers. York and Warwick condemn her claims as lies and order her execution. She curses England and the English lords as she is led off.'),
('1H6', 5, 5, 'Suffolk returns to England and presents Margaret of Anjou to King Henry as a proposed bride. Henry is enchanted by Suffolk''s description of her and agrees to the marriage, setting aside an earlier betrothal to the Earl of Armagnac''s daughter. Gloucester warns that the treaty gives away English territorial rights without a dowry. Henry overrules all objections and Suffolk is sent back to France to bring Margaret to England.'),
('2H4', -1, 0, 'Rumor, personified as a character covered in tongues, speaks the prologue. Rumor explains that it spreads false news through the world, and declares it has already spread a false report that Hotspur defeated King Henry at Shrewsbury, when in fact the opposite is true. This sets the stage for the misinformation that will drive the opening scenes.'),
('2H4', 1, 1, 'Lord Bardolph brings the Earl of Northumberland false news that the rebels won at Shrewsbury and that Hotspur is alive. When Travers and then Morton arrive with correct news — Hotspur is dead and the rebels were crushed — Northumberland is devastated. Morton urges him not to despair: the Archbishop of York is raising a new rebellion. Northumberland resolves to join the new uprising.'),
('2H4', 1, 2, 'Falstaff walks through London with his page, exchanging witty banter and complaining about his gout and the difficulty of getting his tailor paid. The Lord Chief Justice stops Falstaff and reprimands him for his disreputable behavior and his involvement in the Gadshill robbery. Falstaff deflects all criticism with humor and cheek. Falstaff requests a loan from the Chief Justice, who refuses.'),
('2H4', 1, 3, 'The Archbishop of York, Lord Mowbray, Hastings, and Bardolph (not Falstaff''s companion) meet to plan the new rebellion. They debate whether their forces are strong enough without Northumberland''s support. Hastings argues that the king''s forces are divided among three fronts — France, Glendower''s Wales, and their own — and that their army is sufficient. They decide to proceed.'),
('2H4', 2, 1, 'Mistress Quickly, the Hostess of the Boar''s Head Tavern, has had Falstaff arrested for debt. Falstaff smooth-talks her out of prosecuting him, convinces her to loan him more money, and invites her to dinner. The Lord Chief Justice arrives and hears the dispute, but Falstaff manages to escape the legal consequences. News arrives that Falstaff must report for duty — the rebellion has begun in earnest.'),
('2H4', 2, 2, 'Prince Hal and his friend Poins discuss the news of the rebellion and the expected death of Falstaff''s companion Bardolph''s nose (a jest). Hal reflects somewhat guiltily on his own dissolute behavior. He and Poins plan to disguise themselves as tapsters and eavesdrop on Falstaff at the tavern. Hal reads a letter from Falstaff that mocks Poins but is also revealing about Falstaff''s self-regard.'),
('2H4', 2, 3, 'In Northumberland''s castle, Northumberland prepares to join the rebellion against King Henry. His wife and daughter-in-law Lady Percy plead with him not to go, urging him to wait for a better moment and reminding him that he failed to support Hotspur when it mattered most. Northumberland listens and decides to withdraw to Scotland rather than immediately join the rebels.'),
('2H4', 2, 4, 'In the Boar''s Head Tavern, Doll Tearsheet and Mistress Quickly enjoy an evening with Falstaff. Pistol arrives and causes a brawl; Falstaff drives him out. Hal and Poins enter disguised as tapsters and overhear Falstaff speaking disparagingly of them; Hal reveals himself and Falstaff quickly covers himself with his usual wit. A messenger arrives summoning Falstaff to court on urgent military business. The scene ends with tender and melancholy farewells between Falstaff and Doll.'),
('2H4', 3, 1, 'King Henry IV, ill and sleepless, meditates alone in the early hours of the morning. He reflects bitterly on the paradox that the poorest laborer can sleep peacefully while the king lies awake under the burden of responsibility and worry. Warwick and Surrey arrive; Henry speaks of his fears for the kingdom and reflects on how he had long ago foreseen the treachery of those now rebelling against him. He resolves to lead a campaign to the Holy Land once peace is restored.'),
('2H4', 3, 2, 'Justice Shallow and Justice Silence, old country justices in Gloucestershire, reminisce fondly and comically about their wild youth with Falstaff at Clement''s Inn. Falstaff arrives to recruit soldiers. He reviews a list of country men, most of whom bribe their way out of service with the help of Shallow. Falstaff privately reflects that Shallow''s memories of their shared youth are completely exaggerated and that he plans to exploit Shallow''s friendship for money.'),
('2H4', 4, 1, 'The rebel army is encamped at Gaultree Forest in Yorkshire. The Archbishop delivers a speech justifying the rebellion on the grounds that the commonwealth is sick and King Henry broke faith with those who helped him take the throne. Westmorland arrives from the king''s camp with a message; the two sides agree to negotiate. Mowbray distrusts the offer but is overruled.'),
('2H4', 4, 2, 'Prince John of Lancaster meets with the rebel leaders and offers to redress all their grievances if they disband their army. The rebels, trusting his promise, send their forces home. Then Prince John immediately orders the rebel leaders arrested for treason, arguing that he promised to redress their grievances but never promised their personal safety. The rebels are sent to execution. Falstaff captures the fleeing Coleville of the Dale. Prince John dismisses Falstaff''s self-congratulatory account of his own valor coldly.'),
('2H4', 4, 3, 'Falstaff delivers a comic soliloquy in praise of sherris-sack (sherry), arguing that it is the source of all valor and wit and that Prince John is cold and humorless because he drinks no wine. Falstaff is summoned to court where the king is gravely ill.'),
('2H4', 5, 1, 'Falstaff visits Justice Shallow in Gloucestershire on his way back from the campaign. Shallow is an enthusiastic, foolish host, full of reminiscences. Shallow offers Falstaff every hospitality. Falstaff privately plans to exploit Shallow''s wealth and vanity once Prince Hal becomes king.'),
('2H4', 5, 2, 'The Lord Chief Justice, worried about his position under the new king, meets with the other nobles after Henry IV''s death. Prince Hal, now Henry V, arrives. The Chief Justice fears he will be punished for having once sent the prince to prison. But Henry V surprises everyone by praising the Chief Justice for his impartiality and law, and confirms him in his post, pledging to be a just and reformed king.'),
('2H4', 5, 3, 'Justice Shallow hosts a merry evening with Falstaff, Silence, and their companions, with much eating, drinking, and singing. Pistol arrives with the news that King Henry IV is dead and that Hal is now Henry V. Falstaff is overjoyed, certain that his old friend the prince will make him rich and powerful. Falstaff sets off immediately for London.'),
('2H4', 5, 4, 'Mistress Quickly and Doll Tearsheet are being dragged through the streets by officers, arrested on a charge related to a man who has died after a beating linked to Pistol''s activities. The scene is brief and comic but darkens the mood before the final rejection of Falstaff''s world.'),
('2H4', 5, 5, 'Falstaff and his companions arrive at Westminster for Henry V''s coronation procession, expecting to be greeted as favorites. Henry V publicly rejects Falstaff in front of the crowd: "I know thee not, old man." He banishes Falstaff from his presence and court, and orders him imprisoned if he comes within ten miles of the king. Falstaff assures his companions he will be privately recalled, but the rejection is clearly final. The Lord Chief Justice orders Falstaff and his companions to prison. Prince John observes that England will soon be at war with France.'),
('2H4', 6, 0, 'The epilogue is spoken by a dancer, who begs the audience''s pardon if the play has displeased them and promises the company will offer a better play at another time. The dancer mentions that Falstaff will appear again in a future play (Henry V), though the historical Oldcastle (from whom Falstaff derives) was not a martyr. The epilogue ends with a prayer for the queen.'),
('2H6', 1, 1, 'King Henry VI welcomes his new bride, Margaret of Anjou, to England. Suffolk reads the terms of the marriage treaty, which shocks the assembled lords: England has given up the provinces of Maine and Anjou in France without any dowry. Gloucester protests vigorously. Henry, besotted, ignores the controversy. The great lords — York, Salisbury, Warwick — are deeply angry and begin to plot. York privately reveals his ambition to claim the throne.'),
('2H6', 1, 2, 'The Duchess of Gloucester urges her husband Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester, to be more ambitious and seek the crown. Gloucester firmly rejects the idea. The Duchess consoles herself by describing a dream of greatness. After Gloucester leaves, the Duchess meets with a conjuror and a witch whom she has hired to summon spirits and ask about the future of the king and various nobles.'),
('2H6', 1, 3, 'Petitioners arrive at court intending to present grievances to Gloucester, but are intercepted by Suffolk and Queen Margaret, who dismisses their complaints contemptuously. Margaret and Suffolk discuss their desire to remove Gloucester from power; they find allies in Winchester, York, Buckingham, and Somerset. The queen slaps the Duchess of Gloucester during a dispute over a hawk. York and Somerset quarrel over who should be regent of France.'),
('2H6', 1, 4, 'The Duchess of Gloucester attends the séance she has arranged. The conjuror Bolingbroke and witch Margery Jourdain summon a spirit that gives cryptic prophecies about the fates of King Henry, Suffolk, and Somerset. York and Buckingham arrive and arrest the participants in the act. The Duchess is seized and the evidence against her gathered.'),
('2H6', 2, 1, 'At Saint Albans, the king, queen, and court watch a hawking party. A man named Simpcox claims he has been miraculously cured of blindness at the shrine of Saint Alban. Gloucester exposes the fraud through clever tests. News arrives that the Duchess of Gloucester has been arrested for sorcery. Henry is distressed; Gloucester resigns his staff of office (the Lord Protectorship) to let justice take its course.'),
('2H6', 2, 2, 'York privately explains his claim to the English throne to Salisbury and Warwick through a detailed genealogical argument, showing that his line has a stronger right than Henry VI''s Lancastrian claim. Salisbury and Warwick, convinced, pledge their support to York''s cause. They all agree to wait for the right moment.'),
('2H6', 2, 3, 'The Duchess of Gloucester is sentenced to public penance (walking barefoot through London''s streets) and then permanent exile. Gloucester watches his wife''s humiliation in anguish but accepts the sentence. The king holds a trial by combat between an armorer named Horner — accused by his man Peter of saying York had a right to the throne — and Peter. Peter, though seemingly the weaker fighter, wins when Horner is drunk and falls; Horner dies confessing his treason.'),
('2H6', 2, 4, 'The Duchess of Gloucester does her public penance, walking through the streets in a white sheet, followed by jeering crowds. Gloucester meets her and they exchange anguished farewells; she warns him that his enemies are using her downfall to destroy him next. She is led away into exile. Gloucester is summoned to parliament at Bury St Edmunds.'),
('2H6', 3, 1, 'At the parliament, Queen Margaret and the lords launch a coordinated attack on Gloucester, accusing him of various offenses. Henry is grieved but cannot protect him; Gloucester is arrested. Suffolk, Cardinal Winchester, York, and Buckingham plot to have him murdered before he can be tried. York is appointed to go put down a rebellion in Ireland; he plans to use this as a chance to build his power, and he reveals that he has already arranged for the rebel Jack Cade to stir up trouble in England as a distraction.'),
('2H6', 3, 2, 'Gloucester is found dead in his bed — murdered, though it is announced as natural causes. Henry faints with grief. Queen Margaret performs a theatrical display of sorrow. Warwick examines the body and declares it clear signs of murder. Suffolk is accused by Warwick, and the commons outside demand Suffolk''s exile or death. Henry, overwhelmed by the pressure, banishes Suffolk. Queen Margaret and Suffolk make a passionate farewell. Winchester dies offstage, raving madly in his final delirium, reportedly haunted by Gloucester''s ghost.'),
('2H6', 3, 3, 'Cardinal Winchester dies in a delirium, raving about Gloucester''s ghost and unable to find peace. Henry prays over him sadly. Warwick and Salisbury report that the common people are in uproar demanding Suffolk''s punishment.'),
('2H6', 4, 1, 'Off the coast, Suffolk''s ship is intercepted by pirates. When his identity is discovered, the crew decides to execute him in revenge for his crimes against England. Suffolk meets his end proudly and defiantly. His head and body are sent to Queen Margaret.'),
('2H6', 4, 2, 'In Kent, the rebellion of Jack Cade begins. Cade, coached by York, claims to be John Mortimer and thus a legitimate heir to the throne. His followers are a crowd of tradesmen and laborers who have been told that all laws will be abolished, the realm will be held in common, and everyone will eat and drink for free. A Clerk is brought before Cade and executed because he can read and write. Dick the Butcher makes the famous suggestion to "kill all the lawyers." The rebels grow in number and march toward London.'),
('2H6', 4, 3, 'Sir Humphrey Stafford and his brother confront Cade''s rebels and demand they disperse. Cade presents his claims to royal descent. A fight breaks out; the Staffords are killed and the rebels take their armor. Cade''s confidence grows as his army swells.'),
('2H6', 4, 4, 'King Henry and his court receive alarming news about Cade''s rebellion and its growing size. Henry is indecisive and distressed. Queen Margaret arrives carrying Suffolk''s severed head, grief-stricken. Buckingham and Clifford are sent to try to persuade the rebels to disperse by offering a royal pardon.'),
('2H6', 4, 5, 'Lord Scales is inside the Tower of London with his garrison as Cade''s rebels attack the city. Citizens appeal to him for help. He cannot leave the Tower but agrees to send Matthew Gough with forces to help defend London Bridge.'),
('2H6', 4, 6, 'Jack Cade enters London and strikes his sword on London Stone, declaring himself lord of the city. He orders the tap of the conduit to run with wine. He commands that all records be burned and decrees that going forward all things shall be in common.'),
('2H6', 4, 7, 'Cade holds a mock trial of Lord Say, who is dragged before him. Cade accuses Lord Say of treason for building a grammar school, causing men to speak Latin, and printing books. Lord Say defends himself eloquently but Cade has him and his son-in-law executed and their heads mounted on poles and made to kiss at each crossroads.'),
('2H6', 4, 8, 'Buckingham and Clifford confront Cade''s rebels in Southwark. Clifford gives a patriotic speech reminding them of Henry V and English glory in France. The fickle mob switches sides and shouts for the king. Cade flees, cursing his followers as fools. The commons are pardoned.'),
('2H6', 4, 9, 'King Henry pardons those rebels who have returned to loyalty. News arrives that York is returning from Ireland with an army, demanding the removal of Somerset, whom he calls a traitor. Henry, hoping to forestall conflict, agrees to have Somerset committed to the Tower temporarily.'),
('2H6', 4, 10, 'Jack Cade, starving and hiding in a garden in Kent, breaks in to find food. The garden''s owner, Alexander Iden, a gentleman, finds him. Cade attacks but is killed. Iden is horrified to discover who the dead man is and cuts off his head to bring to the king.'),
('2H6', 5, 1, 'York arrives with his army and learns that Somerset has been released and is still in favor, not imprisoned as promised. He openly declares his claim to the crown. Buckingham and Henry attempt to reason with him. Iden presents Cade''s head and is knighted. York demands Somerset''s arrest. Margaret brings Somerset out; York furiously declares open war and calls his sons Edward and Richard to his side. Warwick and Salisbury declare for York. The two sides prepare for the first battle.'),
('2H6', 5, 2, 'The First Battle of Saint Albans begins. York kills old Clifford in battle. Young Clifford, finding his father''s body, delivers a passionate grief-and-vengeance speech, resolving to show no mercy to the Yorkists. Young Clifford carries his father''s body from the field. Richard of York defeats and humiliates Somerset, who is killed beneath a tavern sign — fulfilling a prophecy. The Yorkists carry the day.'),
('2H6', 5, 3, 'The Yorkists have won the battle. The king and queen flee. York, Warwick, and Salisbury celebrate and agree to pursue the king to London. The play ends with the Yorkist victory at Saint Albans and the War of the Roses now openly begun.'),
('3H6', 1, 1, 'York and his sons Richard, Edward, and George enter the Parliament House with armed men and York sits on the throne. King Henry arrives and confronts him. After a dispute, Henry strikes a deal: yielding the throne to York and his heirs in exchange for being allowed to reign for life. Westmorland and Clifford are furious and leave in disgust. Queen Margaret arrives and condemns Henry for disinheriting their son Prince Edward; she renounces Henry as her husband and king and leads away those lords loyal to the prince.'),
('3H6', 1, 2, 'York''s sons Edward and Richard urge their father to seize the crown immediately rather than wait for Henry''s death. A messenger reports that Queen Margaret is advancing with a great northern army. York resolves to fight and sends for reinforcements. His son Rutland and tutor are with the army.'),
('3H6', 1, 3, 'Young Rutland, York''s youngest son, tries to flee the battle but is captured by Lord Clifford. Despite Rutland''s pleas for mercy and his tutor''s intercession, Clifford kills him in revenge for his own father''s death at Saint Albans.'),
('3H6', 1, 4, 'York''s forces are overwhelmed and he is captured. Queen Margaret and Clifford mock and torment him: they give him a napkin soaked in Rutland''s blood to wipe his tears, place a paper crown on his head, and allow the queen to stab him. York grieves for his murdered son but dies defiant and cursing Margaret. Northumberland, though a Lancastrian, weeps at York''s suffering. York is killed and his head is displayed above the gates of York.'),
('3H6', 2, 1, 'York''s sons Edward and Richard learn of their father''s death and vow revenge. Three suns appear in the sky as an omen, which Edward interprets as a sign for the three York brothers. Warwick arrives with news that his forces were defeated by Margaret''s army at Saint Albans. Together Edward, Richard, and Warwick resolve to march on London. Edward declares his intention to claim the throne.'),
('3H6', 2, 2, 'Outside York, Queen Margaret, King Henry, and the Lancastrian lords display York''s head on the gates. Prince Edward urges his father to be more kingly and fight. York''s sons and Warwick arrive with their army and a verbal battle of insults and challenges ensues between the two sides. Clifford challenges Warwick; Richard challenges Clifford; Edward challenges the queen. Henry tries to negotiate but Margaret takes charge. Battle is joined.'),
('3H6', 2, 3, 'The battle near York goes badly for the Lancastrians. Warwick is tired and wounded. Edward and Richard arrive and encourage him. They resolve to push for victory.'),
('3H6', 2, 4, 'Richard pursues Clifford during the battle. Clifford escapes into the press of battle.'),
('3H6', 2, 5, 'King Henry sits alone on a molehill while the battle rages, musing that he wishes he were a simple shepherd rather than a king. He watches a father who has unknowingly killed his own son and a son who has unknowingly killed his own father — a pair of emblematic figures who show the horror of civil war in which families are torn apart. Henry weeps at the sight. Queen Margaret and Prince Edward urge him to flee as the battle is lost.'),
('3H6', 2, 6, 'The mortally wounded Clifford enters alone. He dies before the Yorkists arrive. Edward, Richard, George, and Warwick discover his body and heap insults and mockery on his corpse. Richard removes York''s head from the city gates and replaces it with Clifford''s. Edward is proclaimed king.'),
('3H6', 3, 1, 'Two gamekeepers in a forest in northern England encounter King Henry, who is wandering in disguise after fleeing the battle. Henry speaks openly of his situation, missing his queen and grieving for his kingdom. The gamekeepers recognize him and arrest him, and he is taken to Edward to be imprisoned in the Tower.'),
('3H6', 3, 2, 'King Edward IV holds court. Lady Grey comes to petition for her slain husband''s lands. Edward woos her; she deflects his advances with modest virtue and Edward, impressed, decides to marry her despite the political damage this will cause, since she is a commoner and widow. Richard, watching, delivers a long soliloquy in which he reveals his own ambition for the crown, his determination to wade through any obstacle to get it, and his self-awareness that he is misshapen and incapable of love.'),
('3H6', 3, 3, 'At the French court, Queen Margaret and Prince Edward appeal to King Louis XI of France for military support to restore Henry VI. Warwick arrives as Edward''s ambassador to negotiate a French princess as a bride for Edward. Louis is inclined to help Warwick. A messenger announces that Edward has married Lady Grey; Warwick is furious at being humiliated and publicly switches sides, declaring for Lancaster. Louis agrees to support Margaret''s invasion of England. Warwick promises to marry his daughter to Prince Edward as a seal of the alliance.'),
('3H6', 4, 1, 'Edward IV''s brothers Clarence and Gloucester hear about Edward''s sudden marriage to Lady Grey with dismay. The Woodville relatives of Lady Grey are favored with titles and honors, angering the established nobility. Clarence is particularly upset and indicates he may switch sides. Warwick''s brothers and friends begin to defect from Edward''s cause.'),
('3H6', 4, 2, 'Warwick and Clarence''s forces surprise Edward''s small camp near Warwick. Edward''s friends urge him to flee but he refuses. Warwick''s men surround and capture Edward.'),
('3H6', 4, 3, 'Warwick''s men capture King Edward IV in his tent at night and take him prisoner. He is sent as a prisoner to the Archbishop of York. Gloucester plots to free Edward.'),
('3H6', 4, 4, 'Queen Elizabeth (Lady Grey, now queen) is told that Edward has been captured. She is pregnant with his child. She resolves to seek sanctuary to protect herself and her unborn child. She laments the reversal of fortune.'),
('3H6', 4, 5, 'Richard, Hastings, and Stanley rescue Edward from his captivity at the Archbishop''s hunting lodge. Edward escapes on horseback and they flee toward the coast to take ship to Burgundy for safety.'),
('3H6', 4, 6, 'Warwick releases Henry VI from the Tower and restores him as king. Henry is weary of power and wishes only for peace. He makes Warwick and Clarence joint protectors of the realm. A messenger reports that Edward has escaped and fled to Burgundy with a small party. Henry pardons those who return to his loyalty and agrees to welcome back those who were Yorkists.'),
('3H6', 4, 7, 'Edward returns from Burgundy with a small force of soldiers and lands at Ravenspur. He claims at first only to want his dukedom of York back, not the throne. The Mayor of York lets him in. He gains the support of Montague''s forces through clever argument. Clarence, changing sides again, defects back to the Yorkists with his army. Edward now marches on London to reclaim the throne.'),
('3H6', 4, 8, 'Edward IV boldly enters London and captures King Henry VI again, sending him back to the Tower. The Bishop of London and other Londoners welcome Edward back as king. Edward marches to meet Warwick''s forces.'),
('3H6', 5, 1, 'The battle of Barnet. Warwick''s forces and Edward''s forces prepare to fight. Warwick receives word that his French allies and Queen Margaret have landed in the west but are not yet arrived. He resolves to fight without them. The battle begins and Edward''s side wins. Warwick is mortally wounded and dies alone on the battlefield.'),
('3H6', 5, 2, 'After the battle of Barnet, Edward celebrates. He receives word that Queen Margaret has landed with a French army in the west. He marches to meet her.'),
('3H6', 5, 3, 'Queen Margaret rallies her army for battle at Tewkesbury. Prince Edward gives an inspiring speech. Margaret herself speaks bravely, encouraging the troops. They prepare to fight against Edward''s army.'),
('3H6', 5, 4, 'The battle of Tewkesbury. The Lancastrians are defeated. Queen Margaret, Prince Edward, Somerset, Oxford, and others are captured. Edward IV, Clarence, and Richard confront the young Prince Edward; when Prince Edward speaks defiantly, Edward, Clarence, and Richard all stab him. Queen Margaret calls for death, grieving her son. Richard leaves, heading toward the Tower for a secret errand.'),
('3H6', 5, 5, 'Richard goes to the Tower and murders King Henry VI as he prays. Henry prophesies over Richard, predicting disaster for England at his hands and that many will rue the day Richard was born. Richard kills him and leaves, soliloquizing about his own monstrous ambition and ruthlessness.'),
('3H6', 5, 6, 'Edward IV is securely on the throne. He and the court celebrate. He announces that Queen Margaret has been ransomed back to France by her father. He speaks of peace. He holds up his infant son as proof of a secure succession. Richard embraces the infant but aside expresses his malicious intentions. The play ends with the Yorkists triumphant but with Richard''s ominous presence already shadowing the future.'),
('3H6', 5, 7, 'Edward IV gathers his court in celebration of his secure rule. He speaks of peace and prosperity. He holds his young son and the future seems bright. But Richard''s soliloquy in the previous scene already signals that this peace will not last. The court exits in triumph.'),
('H5', 0, 0, 'The Chorus opens the play by apologizing for the limitations of the theater and asking the audience to use their imaginations to supply armies, horses, and vast battlefields. It introduces the warlike Henry V and calls on the audience to piece out the imperfections of the stage with their thoughts.'),
('H5', 1, 1, 'The Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop of Ely discuss King Henry V''s transformation: the dissolute prince has become a wise, serious, and devout king. They also worry about a parliamentary bill that would confiscate much of the Church''s land. They plan to encourage Henry''s French campaign, which will redirect his attention from the bill, by offering substantial Church funds to support the war.'),
('H5', 1, 2, 'King Henry asks the Archbishop of Canterbury for a formal justification of his claim to the French throne. The Archbishop delivers a long legal argument that the Salic law — which France uses to exclude Henry from inheriting — does not actually apply to France. The assembled lords and Canterbury urge Henry to war. French ambassadors arrive with the Dauphin''s insulting gift of tennis balls, mocking Henry as a frivolous youth. Henry responds with controlled fury, promising the French will regret the insult.'),
('H5', 2, 0, 'The Chorus describes the excitement of the English mobilizing for war and the scene in Southampton where the army is about to embark. It also warns of a dark subplot: three English traitors — Scroop, Cambridge, and Grey — have been bribed by France to assassinate Henry before he can sail.'),
('H5', 2, 1, 'In Eastcheap, London, Nym and Bardolph quarrel: Nym is angry because Pistol married Mistress Quickly (formerly Nell), whom Nym had been promised. They almost come to blows. Pistol and Quickly arrive; Quickly makes peace between them and they are reconciled. The boy (Falstaff''s page) comes with news: Falstaff is gravely ill and Quickly rushes to him.'),
('H5', 2, 2, 'In Southampton, Henry confronts the three traitors — Scroop, Cambridge, and Grey — who had plotted to kill him. He allows them to argue for mercy for a man who spoke against the king, then reveals their own arrest warrants as punishment for their treachery. All three confess their guilt and accept their fate. They are led off to execution and Henry orders the fleet to sail.'),
('H5', 2, 3, 'In Eastcheap, Mistress Quickly, Pistol, Nim, Bardolph, and the boy bid farewell before sailing to France. Quickly describes Falstaff''s death with touching comic detail: she says he died like a child and cried out of God and green fields. The men set off for war, and the scene is both funny and sad.'),
('H5', 2, 4, 'At the French court, King Charles of France and his lords discuss the English invasion. The Dauphin dismisses Henry as a frivolous youth; the Duke of Exeter and others urge caution. Henry''s ambassador Exeter arrives and delivers Henry''s demand for the French crown, backed by the Archbishop of Canterbury''s genealogical argument. He also delivers a personal warning to the Dauphin. Charles says he will reply by the next day.'),
('H5', 3, 0, 'The Chorus describes the crossing of the English fleet to France and the siege of Harfleur. It asks the audience to imagine the warships, the sea voyage, and the armies now before the town of Harfleur, and carries the scene forward in time.'),
('H5', 3, 1, 'Henry rallies his troops to renewed assault on the walls of Harfleur with his famous "Once more unto the breach" speech, urging them to summon their courage and fight with the ferocity of English fighting men and the honor of their fathers.'),
('H5', 3, 2, 'Bardolph, Pistol, Nym, and the boy hang back from the assault, reluctant to fight. Captain Fluellen drives them toward the breach. Fluellen argues with Captain Macmorris about mining and military discipline; Captain Jamy the Scot and the four captains (English, Welsh, Irish, Scottish) quarrel comically about the conduct of the siege.'),
('H5', 3, 3, 'Henry addresses the Governor of Harfleur through the gates, threatening that if the town does not surrender immediately his troops will rape and pillage without mercy. The Governor announces the town''s surrender: the Dauphin cannot send relief in time. Henry orders Exeter to take possession of Harfleur and show mercy to the citizens. He notes that his army is sick and diminished and leads them toward Calais.'),
('H5', 3, 4, 'At the French court, Katherine, Princess of France, has an English lesson from her lady Alice. She learns the names of parts of the body in English — hand, fingers, nails, arm, elbow, neck, chin, and foot — and is amused by how similar some English words are to French rude words. The scene is charming and comic, and establishes Katherine as a character before her eventual courtship by Henry.'),
('H5', 3, 5, 'The French king and his lords are furious and shamed that Henry has taken Harfleur and is marching through France. The Constable and other nobles are impatient to fight and believe the French army will easily overwhelm the weakened English. The king orders his forces to intercept Henry before he reaches Calais. He urges the Dauphin not to join the battle personally.'),
('H5', 3, 6, 'Fluellen tells Gower that Pistol has been refused by the Duke of Exeter''s bridge and fought bravely. Pistol arrives and begs Fluellen to intercede for Bardolph, who has been sentenced to hang for stealing from a church. Fluellen refuses: military discipline must be maintained. Henry arrives and Fluellen reports the policy of no looting; Henry approves. Montjoy, the French herald, arrives and gives Henry the chance to offer ransom — implying the French see the English as already beaten. Henry refuses and says he will press on to Calais.'),
('H5', 3, 7, 'The night before the battle of Agincourt, the French nobles are confident, even boastful. They brag about their horses and armor and joke about how quickly they will defeat the English. The Constable and Orleans banter and wager. Only Rambures briefly worries that Henry is a brave man. The French are arrogant and underestimate their enemy.'),
('H5', 4, 0, 'The Chorus paints the scene of the night before Agincourt: the two armies camped close to each other, the French loud and confident, the English outnumbered and quiet. It describes Henry going through his camp in disguise to visit and encourage his soldiers, and asks the audience to imagine the scene.'),
('H5', 4, 1, 'On the night before battle, Henry borrows a cloak from Sir Thomas Erpingham and walks among his soldiers in disguise. He meets Pistol, who does not recognize him. He talks with Fluellen and Gower (who are arguing about camp noise). He speaks at length with three common soldiers — Bates, Court, and Williams — about the king''s responsibility for the souls of soldiers who die in battle. Henry argues the king is not to blame for the state of each soldier''s soul. He and Williams exchange gloves as a token of a later quarrel. Alone, Henry delivers a long meditation on the burdens and ceremony of kingship.'),
('H5', 4, 2, 'The French nobles arm themselves for battle in the morning, supremely confident of an easy victory over the outnumbered and sick English. The Dauphin boasts. A messenger reports the English are drawn up in order for battle. The French are certain it will be over quickly.'),
('H5', 4, 3, 'Westmorland wishes they had more men. Henry rejects this, saying the fewer men, the greater each man''s share of honor. He delivers the great Saint Crispin''s Day speech, telling his soldiers they are a "band of brothers" and that those who fight today will remember this day with pride for the rest of their lives. Montjoy comes again offering Henry the chance to negotiate a ransom; Henry again refuses. Henry resolves the quarrel between Fluellen and Williams over the exchange of gloves.'),
('H5', 4, 4, 'On the battlefield, Pistol captures a French soldier named Monsieur Le Fer and threatens to kill him. The boy interpreter translates the threats and the prisoner''s fearful responses. Le Fer offers a ransom and Pistol greedily accepts. The boy comments that Pistol is all words and no valor, and that Bardolph and Nym have both been hanged.'),
('H5', 4, 5, 'The French nobles are horrified and shamed by the English victory. They agree it is better to die fighting than to live with the shame of defeat by such a small army. They rush back into the battle.'),
('H5', 4, 6, 'In the heat of battle, Henry receives word that his friend the Duke of York has died bravely. Alarmed by a regrouping French force, Henry gives the order to kill all the French prisoners, which was considered a war crime by the standards of chivalry.'),
('H5', 4, 7, 'Fluellen and Gower discover that the French have massacred the boys guarding the English baggage. Henry is furious. Fluellen compares Henry to Alexander the Great; Gower disagrees on minor points. Henry confronts Montjoy, who now acknowledges the English victory. Henry sees Williams wearing his glove and a quarrel nearly erupts; Henry reveals himself and reconciles with Williams, giving him a glove full of crowns as compensation.'),
('H5', 4, 8, 'Henry assembles all his lords to hear the casualty count. The French losses are enormous — thousands of knights and nobles. The English losses are extraordinarily small: only a handful of gentlemen and Edward Duke of York. Henry attributes the victory entirely to God and forbids any boasting or claiming credit for the victory. He orders a march back to England via Calais with songs of praise.'),
('H5', 5, 0, 'The Chorus covers the time gap: Henry''s triumphant return to England, his welcome in London, the Holy Roman Emperor''s visit to negotiate peace, and the return of the English to France for final negotiations. It apologizes again for the limitations of the stage in covering such vast events.'),
('H5', 5, 1, 'Fluellen forces Pistol to eat a leek — the Welsh symbol Pistol has been mocking — beating him until he complies. Pistol is humiliated. Alone, Pistol reveals that Mistress Quickly has died of syphilis and that he will return to England to become a cutpurse and braggart.'),
('H5', 5, 2, 'At the French court, Henry meets with the French king and queen to negotiate peace. Burgundy gives a moving speech about peace and asks Henry to moderate his demands so France can recover. Henry agrees to all terms except for the French title, which he insists on. He woos Katherine privately in a comic scene of blunt English courtship versus French formality. She eventually agrees to the match if her father approves. The French king accepts all Henry''s terms; the marriage is agreed and peace is made.'),
('H5', 6, 0, 'The Epilogue Chorus speaks the final summary: the play has shown Henry V''s great reign, which was later followed by the reign of his son Henry VI, who lost all his father''s French conquests through the strife of his nobles. The Chorus notes that this story has been told before on these stages, which is a reference to the Henry VI plays.'),
('H8', 0, 0, 'The Prologue asks the audience to come to the play in a truthful spirit, promising sad, noble, and important scenes — not comic or satirical content. It asks that those who weep easily be warned that the play may move them to tears, and those who come for mirth should prepare for sorrow. The stage is set for a history of great and tragic events.'),
('H8', 1, 1, 'Norfolk tells Buckingham about the spectacular pageant at the Field of the Cloth of Gold, where Henry VIII and Francis I of France met. Buckingham is outraged that Cardinal Wolsey managed the whole affair without consulting the nobility. Wolsey is identified as the king''s powerful favorite and fixer. Norfolk warns Buckingham not to cross Wolsey. Buckingham accuses Wolsey of corruption and self-dealing; a Surveyor (informant) arrives and Buckingham is immediately arrested for treason.'),
('H8', 1, 2, 'King Henry VIII holds a council meeting. Queen Katherine appears and asks Henry to relieve the people from a recent heavy tax — the people are grumbling and fleeing. Henry agrees and cancels the tax, turning on Wolsey, who claims the reversal was his own idea all along. The Surveyor gives his testimony against Buckingham, claiming Buckingham said he would kill the king if Henry died without an heir. Henry is troubled and orders Buckingham''s trial.'),
('H8', 1, 3, 'Courtiers mock the English lords who have taken up French fashions from the trip to France. Lovell is ordered by the king to restrain English excess in foreign fashions. Anne Bullen and an old lady discuss the unhappy position of a queen — great power, but also great danger and sorrow. Anne says she would not be a queen for all the world; the old lady teases her about her ambitions. Henry notices Anne at a masque and is attracted to her.'),
('H8', 1, 4, 'At a great banquet at Wolsey''s house, the Cardinal entertains lavishly. Henry arrives in disguise with other masquers pretending to be shepherds. Henry is drawn to Anne Bullen and dances with her. Wolsey whispers to a chamberlain asking who the beautiful woman is; the answer is Anne Bullen. The king reveals his identity and takes the seat of honor, making much of Anne.'),
('H8', 2, 1, 'Two gentlemen discuss the trial and sentencing of the Duke of Buckingham. They recount that Buckingham was found guilty of treason on the evidence of his Surveyor and is to be executed. Buckingham passes through on his way to execution, forgiving his enemies nobly and accepting his fate with dignity. The gentlemen report rumors that Henry wants a divorce from Queen Katherine, and that Wolsey has arranged it to spite the Emperor.'),
('H8', 2, 2, 'Two lords discuss the king''s desire for a divorce from Katherine. Wolsey and Campeius (the papal legate) meet with Henry; Wolsey urges the king''s "conscience" to question the validity of the marriage. Henry is clearly infatuated with Anne Bullen and eager for the divorce, but presents it as a matter of conscience. The lords observe that the matter of the divorce is really driven by Henry''s love for Anne.'),
('H8', 2, 3, 'Anne Bullen and the old lady discuss the queen''s sad situation. Anne expresses sympathy for Katherine and again says she would not want to be queen. The Lord Chamberlain arrives and informs Anne that the king has appointed her Marchioness of Pembroke with a large annual income. The old lady teases Anne that she will yet be queen; Anne demurs, but is clearly moved.'),
('H8', 2, 4, 'The legantine trial of Queen Katherine''s marriage is held at Blackfriars. Henry and Katherine both appear before the Cardinals Wolsey and Campeius. Katherine kneels before Henry and movingly defends her honor as a wife and a queen, asking why her marriage of twenty years is being questioned. She refuses to recognize the court''s jurisdiction and walks out despite being called back. Henry praises her publicly. The trial is adjourned.'),
('H8', 3, 1, 'Two cardinals visit Queen Katherine to urge her to submit to the king''s will and accept a quiet resolution. Katherine is dignified but defiant; she cross-examines Wolsey sharply and accuses him of being her enemy. She finally says she will do nothing without the advice of good men or the Pope''s direction. The cardinals leave having made no progress.'),
('H8', 3, 2, 'Suffolk, Norfolk, and the lords tell the king that Wolsey has secretly written to the Pope asking him to delay the divorce. Henry is shown evidence of Wolsey''s enormous private wealth and his hidden resistance. Henry publicly humiliates Wolsey, stripping him of the Lord Chancellorship. After Henry leaves, Wolsey soliloquizes on his fall from power and his ruined life — the famous "farewell to all my greatness" speech. Cromwell enters; Wolsey urges him to serve the king honestly and avoid the path of ambition that destroyed Wolsey himself.'),
('H8', 4, 1, 'Two gentlemen watch the coronation procession of Anne Bullen as the new Queen of England. They describe the spectacle — the bishops, lords, and the new queen herself — in elaborate detail. A third gentleman arrives who was actually inside the abbey and gives a vivid account of the ceremony. They also note that Thomas Cranmer has been made Archbishop of Canterbury and that Wolsey is dead.'),
('H8', 4, 2, 'Katherine, now reduced to the title "Princess Dowager," lies dying at Kimbolton. She has a vision: six dancing figures place a garland on her head. Capucius, the Emperor''s ambassador, visits her. She dictates a final letter to Henry asking him to be kind to their daughter Mary and to reward her loyal servants. She dies peacefully, forgiving all her enemies, as her ladies prepare to take her to burial.'),
('H8', 5, 1, 'Gardiner (Bishop of Winchester) and another lord conspire against Cranmer, the Archbishop of Canterbury. Henry secretly watches as Cranmer is kept waiting outside the Council chamber at night, treated as an inferior. Henry privately gives Cranmer his ring as a token of royal protection. Henry speaks with a doctor who announces that Anne has delivered a child — a daughter, not the hoped-for son.'),
('H8', 5, 2, 'Cranmer is brought before the Council and accused of heresy. When they are about to send him to the Tower, Cranmer produces the king''s ring. Henry enters and publicly rebukes the councillors for treating his Archbishop so contemptuously. The king orders Cranmer and his accusers to shake hands. Henry shows the full force of his royal authority and protection of those he favors.'),
('H8', 5, 3, 'The christening of the infant Elizabeth takes place. The doorkeeper tries to manage the enormous crowd of common people who want to see the ceremony. Cranmer stands as godfather.'),
('H8', 5, 4, 'Archbishop Cranmer baptizes the infant Princess Elizabeth and delivers a famous prophecy at the christening: this child will grow to be a great and good queen, and after a long reign she will be succeeded by another great monarch, and England will flourish under both. Henry is delighted and vows to be a good father to the child.'),
('H8', 6, 0, 'The Epilogue is a brief, gracious address to the audience asking for their good opinion of the play. It specifically addresses the women in the audience, hoping the play has pleased them, and asks for applause.'),
('R2', 1, 1, 'King Richard II presides over a formal dispute between his cousin Henry Bolingbroke and the Duke of Norfolk (Mowbray). Bolingbroke accuses Mowbray of treason and of misappropriating royal funds, and specifically of arranging the murder of the Duke of Gloucester. Mowbray denies the charges. Richard cannot or will not resolve the dispute, and both men call for trial by combat.'),
('R2', 1, 2, 'John of Gaunt and the Duchess of Gloucester discuss the murder of her husband, Gloucester. The Duchess urges Gaunt to seek revenge, but Gaunt refuses, saying that to act against the king — whom he believes ordered Gloucester''s death — would be sacrilege. The Duchess is grief-stricken and resolves to go to Plashy to die.'),
('R2', 1, 3, 'The trial by combat is staged at Coventry with great ceremony. At the last moment, just as the combatants are about to charge, King Richard throws down his warder (staff) and stops the fight. He banishes both men: Bolingbroke for ten years (soon reduced to six for Gaunt''s sake) and Mowbray for life. Mowbray accepts his banishment bitterly. Gaunt and Bolingbroke say farewell; Gaunt tries to comfort his son, who leaves England.'),
('R2', 1, 4, 'Richard and his favorites discuss Bolingbroke''s departure and Richard''s suspicion of his rival''s popularity with the common people. News arrives that John of Gaunt is dying. Richard says he hopes Gaunt dies quickly so he can seize his wealth to fund the Irish wars. The lords are shocked by the king''s callousness but say nothing.'),
('R2', 2, 1, 'The dying John of Gaunt delivers his famous "This royal throne of kings, this sceptr''d isle" speech, a patriotic lament for England under Richard''s misrule. When Richard arrives, Gaunt reproves him sharply for squandering England''s heritage. Richard responds coldly. Gaunt is carried out and shortly dies. Richard immediately seizes all of Gaunt''s estate, ignoring the protests of York and the other lords. York warns this action — denying Bolingbroke his legal inheritance — will have terrible consequences. Northumberland reveals to Ross and Willoughby that Bolingbroke is sailing back to England with an army.'),
('R2', 2, 2, 'Queen Isabella is left in England, stricken with a premonition of disaster. Bushy and Green try to console her. News arrives in rapid succession: Bolingbroke has landed with an army in the north, the Duke of York (left as regent) is powerless to resist, and the country is rising to join Bolingbroke. York is so overwhelmed by conflicting loyalties that he is paralyzed. Bushy, Green, and Bagot (Richard''s favorites) flee in different directions.'),
('R2', 2, 3, 'Bolingbroke and Northumberland march through Gloucestershire gathering support. Berkeley brings them before the Duke of York, who confronts Bolingbroke for returning without permission from exile. Bolingbroke argues he has only come back to claim his rightful inheritance as Duke of Lancaster. York says this is treason but that he has no power to resist; he will remain neutral. They all march together to Bristol Castle where Bushy and Green have taken refuge.'),
('R2', 2, 4, 'A Welsh captain tells the Earl of Salisbury that his men can no longer wait for Richard: they have heard he is dead and have dispersed. Salisbury despairs; the omens — dying trees, meteors, ravens — seem to foretell Richard''s fall.'),
('R2', 3, 1, 'At Bristol Castle, Bolingbroke has captured Bushy and Green. He condemns them to death, accusing them of corrupting the king, coming between Richard and his queen, and ruining his own reputation with the king. Bushy and Green are executed.'),
('R2', 3, 2, 'Richard returns from Ireland to the Welsh coast. Initially he is full of poetic confidence, speaking of the land itself rallying to a rightful king. The news gets progressively worse: the Welsh army has dispersed, his favorites are dead, Northumberland has joined Bolingbroke. Richard''s mood swings between defiance and despair; his famous meditation on the "hollow crown" and the mortality of kings is here. By the end he accepts that all is lost and must go to Flint Castle.'),
('R2', 3, 3, 'Bolingbroke''s army approaches Flint Castle where Richard is sheltering. Bolingbroke sends Northumberland to negotiate. Richard appears on the walls and speaks defiantly. He agrees to come down and meet Bolingbroke. When Richard descends, the encounter is subtle: Bolingbroke kneels, Richard speaks bitterly of being forced to "base court" from his "high court," and it becomes clear, without being stated, that Richard will be deposing himself. They ride to London together.'),
('R2', 3, 4, 'In the Duke of York''s garden, the Queen overhears gardeners talking about affairs of state. The head gardener uses the imagery of tending a garden to discuss how Richard has failed to govern the realm properly while Bolingbroke has done what a good gardener would do — pruned away bad advisors, cut off Bushy and Green. The Queen confronts the Gardener; he confirms the news that Richard will be deposed. The Queen curses the garden and goes to find Richard.'),
('R2', 4, 1, 'In Parliament, Bolingbroke hears accusations of treason fly between various lords. The quarrel over who arranged Gloucester''s murder remains unresolved. The Bishop of Carlisle protests against the deposition of Richard as unlawful and prophesies civil war. Richard is brought in and in a elaborate, anguished ceremony surrenders the crown to Bolingbroke, who becomes Henry IV. Richard calls for a mirror and smashes it. He asks to leave; he is taken to the Tower. The Abbot of Westminster, Carlisle, and Aumerle begin to plan a conspiracy to restore Richard.'),
('R2', 5, 1, 'Richard is led through London to the Tower and encounters Queen Isabella. They grieve together and say farewell. Richard urges her to return to France and go to a nunnery. Northumberland arrives and says Richard is to be taken to Pomfret (Pontefract) Castle, not the Tower, and Isabella is to be taken to France. Richard curses Northumberland and prophesies that he will eventually betray Henry IV just as he betrayed Richard.'),
('R2', 5, 2, 'The Duke of York tells his Duchess how Bolingbroke and Richard rode through London — Bolingbroke cheered, Richard pelted with rubbish. Their son Aumerle is discovered to be part of a conspiracy against Henry IV; York is horrified and insists on going to the king to denounce his own son. The Duchess is torn and sends Aumerle ahead to get Henry''s pardon before York arrives.'),
('R2', 5, 3, 'Aumerle rushes to Henry IV and begs pardon for an undisclosed offense. York arrives and reveals the conspiracy. The Duchess arrives and on her knees begs for her son''s life. Henry IV pardons Aumerle but not the other conspirators.'),
('R2', 5, 4, 'Sir Pierce of Exton recalls Henry IV''s words — "Have I no friend will rid me of this living fear?" — and resolves to murder Richard at Pomfret Castle to please the king.'),
('R2', 5, 5, 'Richard is alone in his prison at Pomfret Castle and meditates at length on his situation, using his own mind to populate the solitary cell with a whole world. A groom visits him and tells how his horse Barbary carried Bolingbroke at the coronation; Richard grieves at the betrayal. Exton and murderers burst in; Richard fights back briefly but is killed. Exton has a moment of remorse.'),
('R2', 5, 6, 'Henry IV receives news that the rebellion has been crushed; various conspirators have been captured and executed. Exton arrives with Richard''s coffin, expecting reward for killing him. Henry is horrified and repudiates Exton, declaring him "the vilest murderer" — though he effectively ordered the deed. Henry vows to make a pilgrimage to the Holy Land to wash away the guilt of Richard''s blood, founding the concern that haunts him through the Henry IV plays.'),
('R3', 1, 1, 'Richard, Duke of Gloucester, opens the play with his famous "Now is the winter of our discontent" soliloquy. He reveals that in peacetime he has no place since he is deformed and cannot be a lover; he has resolved to be a villain and seize the crown. He has arranged false charges against his brother Clarence, who is now imprisoned in the Tower on King Edward''s orders. He plots with Buckingham and Hastings. Clarence is escorted past him to the Tower; Richard speaks comfortingly to his face while privately exulting.'),
('R3', 1, 2, 'Richard intercepts Lady Anne, who is accompanying the coffin of King Henry VI (whom Richard murdered) to burial. Anne curses Richard violently. Richard woos her with breathtaking audacity, even while she holds a sword to his chest. He gradually overwhelms her with the argument that her beauty drove him to crime. Against all reason, Anne accepts his ring and agrees to marry him. Alone, Richard marvels at his own success and plans to discard her once she has served her purpose.'),
('R3', 1, 3, 'At King Edward''s court, the dying king tries to make peace between his wife''s relatives (the Woodvilles: Rivers, Grey, Dorset) and the old nobles (Buckingham, Hastings). Richard arrives and quarrels with the Queen''s party. The old Queen Margaret appears — she has been living in England in secret — and curses Richard, Edward, the Queen, Hastings, Dorset, and Rivers in turn, prophesying disaster for them all. Richard privately laughs at his ability to set his enemies against one another.'),
('R3', 1, 4, 'Clarence is in the Tower and tells his keeper of a terrible dream: he drowned in the sea and encountered the spirits of those he had wronged. Two murderers sent by Richard arrive with a warrant for Clarence''s death. Clarence wakes and pleads for his life, arguing that even if the king ordered it, they should not obey such a sinful command. One murderer wavers but the other stabs Clarence and drowns him in a butt of malmsey wine. The wavering murderer is left with guilt.'),
('R3', 2, 1, 'King Edward, near death, succeeds in making the feuding lords — Hastings, Rivers, Dorset, Buckingham, the queen''s family — all swear peace and friendship. Richard arrives and falsely joins in the reconciliation. News is then brought that Clarence is dead; Edward is horrified, for he had revoked the order for execution but the revocation arrived too late. Richard insinuates that the Queen''s party were responsible for Clarence''s death.'),
('R3', 2, 2, 'Clarence''s children weep for their father. The Duchess of York, Richard''s and Clarence''s mother, laments her dead son. Queen Elizabeth enters with news that King Edward IV is dead. The court is in confusion and grief. The Duchess of York and Elizabeth mourn together. Rivers, Grey, and Dorset plan to go and bring the young Prince Edward from Ludlow to London for his coronation. Buckingham and Richard secretly plan to intercept them first.'),
('R3', 2, 3, 'Three citizens in London discuss the death of the king and the danger of a young prince on the throne. They worry about the factions around the prince and fear trouble. The scene presents the common people''s anxious perspective on the political crisis.'),
('R3', 2, 4, 'The Archbishop of Canterbury brings news to the Queen and the Duchess that Richard and Buckingham have intercepted the young prince''s escort and arrested Rivers, Grey, and Vaughan. The Queen, terrified, takes her younger son the Duke of York into sanctuary. The Archbishop debates whether sanctuary applies to children. The lords insist the Duke of York must be produced.'),
('R3', 3, 1, 'The young Prince Edward arrives in London and is received by Buckingham, Richard, and the Cardinal. Richard promises the prince that Rivers, Grey, and Vaughan are safely imprisoned for good reason. The prince asks to stay in the Tower until his coronation; Richard and Buckingham arrange for both young princes to be lodged there. Buckingham and Richard plan to get Hastings''s support for Richard''s seizure of the crown; if Hastings refuses, he must die.'),
('R3', 3, 2, 'On the eve of what is meant to be his own victory, Hastings receives a message and a warning from Lord Stanley, who has had an ominous dream about a boar (Richard''s symbol). Hastings dismisses the warning. He encounters Catesby, Richard''s spy, who probes him about supporting Richard''s claim to the throne; Hastings firmly refuses. He meets Stanley and they discuss going to the council meeting, relaxed and unsuspecting. A pursuivant and then a priest tell him there is nothing to fear; a messenger from Buckingham reassures him. He is cheerful — moments before his doom.'),
('R3', 3, 3, 'Rivers, Grey, and Vaughan are led to execution at Pomfret Castle. Each recalls Queen Margaret''s curses and accepts their fate. They are executed offstage.'),
('R3', 3, 4, 'At the Tower Council, the lords discuss arrangements for the young king''s coronation. Hastings is cheerful and confident. Richard arrives and asks what a man who has plotted against his life should face; Hastings says death. Richard then accuses Queen Elizabeth and Mistress Shore of using witchcraft to wither his arm — which has always been withered since birth. He orders Hastings arrested immediately; Hastings finally understands he is betrayed but it is too late. He is taken out and beheaded.'),
('R3', 3, 5, 'Richard and Buckingham stage a panicked scene for the Lord Mayor of London: they appear in battered old armor as if they have just survived an assault, claiming they had to execute Hastings immediately because he was about to kill them. They show the Mayor Hastings''s head. The Mayor is taken in and goes to explain the situation to the citizens of London. Richard and Buckingham arrange for preachers to spread rumors that Edward IV''s sons are illegitimate.'),
('R3', 3, 6, 'A scrivener (legal copyist) reads the indictment of Hastings that he has spent eleven hours writing out. He notes that Hastings was only arrested five hours ago — meaning the indictment was prepared before any evidence was gathered. He sees through the fraud but knows he dare not say so.'),
('R3', 3, 7, 'Buckingham reports to Richard that his speech to the citizens urging Richard''s claim to the throne was met with complete silence. They arrange for Richard to be "discovered" by the Lord Mayor in private prayer flanked by two bishops, presenting the image of a holy and reluctant man. Buckingham publicly begs Richard three times to take the crown; Richard pretends to refuse. Finally, on the third imploring, Richard accepts the crown with staged reluctance. The trap is closed.'),
('R3', 4, 1, 'Queen Elizabeth, the Duchess of York, and Lady Anne go to visit the princes in the Tower but are refused entry by the Lieutenant, on orders from King Richard. Lady Anne, now married to Richard, is summoned to be crowned queen; she goes reluctantly, full of dread. Elizabeth urges her son Dorset to flee to Richmond. They all grieve for the princes and the fallen state of the family.'),
('R3', 4, 2, 'Richard, now king, asks Buckingham to arrange the murder of the two young princes in the Tower. Buckingham hesitates and asks for time to think; Richard turns cold toward him. Richard hires Tyrrel to murder the princes. He also plans to divorce or kill Lady Anne and marry his niece Elizabeth (daughter of Edward IV) to cement his hold on the throne. Buckingham returns and asks for the earldom he was promised; Richard snubs him. Buckingham, frightened, flees to join Richmond''s forces.'),
('R3', 4, 3, 'Tyrrel reports that the two young princes have been smothered in the Tower. Richard receives the news of their deaths with satisfaction. He announces that Anne is dead (he has had her killed), that Clarence''s son is imprisoned, and that only Richmond, who is gathering an army in France, stands between him and total security. Richard plans to marry his niece Elizabeth.'),
('R3', 4, 4, 'Old Queen Margaret is ready to leave England, satisfied that her curses have come true. The Duchess of York and Queen Elizabeth join her in lamenting their dead children and loved ones. Margaret teaches Elizabeth how to curse. Richard arrives and attempts to win Elizabeth''s support for his plan to marry her daughter. The Duchess of York gives Richard a devastating maternal curse and leaves. Richard woos Elizabeth with great energy. She seems to reluctantly agree to sound out her daughter. Richard plans for battle. Messengers arrive with bad news: Richmond is landing, various lords are defecting, Buckingham''s forces are dispersed and he has been captured.'),
('R3', 4, 5, 'Lord Stanley secretly sends a message to Richmond, his stepson, explaining his situation: he cannot openly join Richmond because Richard holds his son George as a hostage. He urges Richmond to proceed and promises to help when he can.'),
('R3', 5, 1, 'Buckingham is led to execution at Salisbury. He recalls Queen Margaret''s curse on him and accepts his fate, saying that All-Souls'' Day is a good day to die on. He is executed.'),
('R3', 5, 2, 'Richmond''s forces are marching through England gathering support. Richmond gives an encouraging speech to his lords about the justice of their cause and the tyranny of Richard. They march toward Bosworth Field.'),
('R3', 5, 3, 'The night before the Battle of Bosworth. Both Richard and Richmond are in their respective tents. The ghosts of all Richard''s victims appear in sequence and curse Richard and encourage Richmond. Richard wakes from a terrible nightmare and delivers a tortured soliloquy: he is alone, loves no one and no one loves him, he fears his own shadow, he is a villain. He addresses his army and makes a rousing call to battle against the rebels.'),
('R3', 5, 4, 'In the thick of the Battle of Bosworth Field, Richard''s horse is killed under him. He calls out "A horse! A horse! My kingdom for a horse!" Catesby urges him to retreat; Richard refuses to flee and continues fighting on foot.'),
('R3', 5, 5, 'Richard is killed in battle by Richmond. Stanley presents Richmond with the crown found on Richard''s head. Richmond, soon to be Henry VII, thanks God for the victory and promises to end the civil wars by marrying Elizabeth of York, uniting the houses of Lancaster and York. He vows to heal England and prays for lasting peace.'),
('Jn', 1, 1, 'King John receives France''s ambassador Chatillon, who demands John surrender the English throne to his nephew Arthur, the son of John''s dead elder brother Geoffrey. John refuses and war is threatened. The bastard Philip Falconbridge is brought to court over an inheritance dispute with his brother; John and his mother Queen Eleanor determine from his appearance that Philip is actually the illegitimate son of the late King Richard I (the Lionheart). Philip renounces his inheritance and is knighted as Sir Richard Plantagenet. He delivers witty, irreverent asides about life at court.'),
('Jn', 2, 1, 'Before the walls of Angiers (Angers) in France, both the English army under King John and the French army under Philip II face each other, each demanding the city''s allegiance — the English for John, the French for Arthur''s claim. The citizens of Angiers refuse to open their gates until the true king is established. The two sides agree to a novel suggestion from a citizen: let John''s niece Blanche marry the Dauphin Lewis, ending the dispute through marriage alliance. John gives up the disputed French territories as Blanche''s dowry. Austria and France accept this deal, though Arthur''s cause is abandoned. Cardinal Pandulph is expected soon.'),
('Jn', 3, 1, 'King Philip and the Dauphin celebrate the peace, but Cardinal Pandulph arrives as the Pope''s legate and demands John submit to Rome''s choice of an Archbishop of Canterbury. John refuses and is excommunicated. Pandulph then pressures Philip to break the newly made peace and make war on John, threatening France with excommunication if he does not. Constance, Arthur''s mother, erupts in passionate grief and rage at the loss of Arthur''s cause. Philip reluctantly goes to war.'),
('Jn', 3, 2, 'During the battle near Angiers, John captures Arthur. The Bastard kills Austria. John sends Arthur with Hubert de Burgh, hinting that he wants Arthur dead without spelling it out. Eleanor is captured briefly but released. The battle goes well for John.'),
('Jn', 3, 3, 'John has won the battle and seized much of France. He sends his mother Eleanor to help govern the French territories. John speaks privately with Hubert and in elliptical language makes clear he wants Arthur killed; Hubert understands and agrees.'),
('Jn', 3, 4, 'Constance, Arthur''s mother, is grief-stricken at the fall of her son''s cause and his capture. She pulls her hair and speaks movingly of her grief, treating death as a friend. Cardinal Pandulph privately tells the Dauphin Lewis how Arthur''s death — which he anticipates — will actually serve France: the English lords will turn against John, and the Dauphin''s own claim through his wife Blanche will then be clear. Pandulph plants the seeds of a future French invasion of England.'),
('Jn', 4, 1, 'Hubert has been ordered to blind and kill Arthur. He arrives with red-hot irons, but Arthur''s pitiful pleading and the boy''s genuine affection for Hubert soften his heart. Hubert relents and decides to hide Arthur rather than execute him, telling John that Arthur is dead.'),
('Jn', 4, 2, 'John has himself crowned a second time, which his lords view as a sign of insecurity. News arrives from England: the barons are rebelling; the French Dauphin is about to invade; and John''s mother Eleanor has died. John also learns that Constance has died. Hubert tells John that Arthur is alive, not dead. John is relieved but then alarmed as Peter of Pomfret''s prophecy about John surrendering his crown on Ascension Day seems to be coming true.'),
('Jn', 4, 3, 'Arthur tries to escape from the castle walls by jumping, but he falls and is killed. The English lords find the body and are horrified, certain John ordered his murder. The Bastard arrives and finds them furious and preparing to defect to the French. He urges loyalty to John but the lords leave. Hubert arrives; the Bastard angrily confronts him and learns Arthur''s death was accidental. The Bastard reflects grimly on the chaos of England.'),
('Jn', 5, 1, 'Facing invasion, John yields his crown to Cardinal Pandulph, who gives it back on behalf of the Pope — John has submitted to Rome. Pandulph promises to stop the French invasion. The Bastard arrives with news that the English lords have joined the French, and the French Dauphin has already landed and is advancing. Pandulph goes to negotiate with the Dauphin.'),
('Jn', 5, 2, 'The Dauphin and the defected English lords refuse Pandulph''s demand to stop the invasion; the Dauphin says the French war machine is in motion and cannot be stopped by the Pope. He gives a list of English lords he plans to reward after his victory. The Bastard arrives as John''s messenger and delivers a defiant ultimatum. Both sides prepare for battle.'),
('Jn', 5, 3, 'The battle goes badly. John is ill and feverish; the Bastard arrives with news that a French supply ship has been wrecked. John withdraws to Swinstead Abbey. The situation is desperate on both sides.'),
('Jn', 5, 4, 'The defected English lords learn from Melun (a dying French lord) that the Dauphin secretly plans to execute them after his victory. Shocked, they resolve to return to King John. The Bastard continues to rally what forces remain.'),
('Jn', 5, 5, 'The Dauphin receives news that Salisbury, Pembroke, and other English lords have returned to John. He also learns that the French supply ships have sunk and that Melun is dead. Things are beginning to go wrong for the French.'),
('Jn', 5, 6, 'Hubert tells the Bastard that King John has been poisoned by a monk at Swinstead Abbey and is dying. The Bastard is dismayed and races to the king.'),
('Jn', 5, 7, 'King John dies in agony in the orchard at Swinstead, attended by Prince Henry and Salisbury. The Bastard arrives too late. England is without a leader while the French are in the field. However, Salisbury reveals that the Dauphin has agreed to peace terms and is leaving England. Prince Henry will be king. The Bastard gives a famous final speech: England will never fall to foreign foes if it remains true to itself.')
;
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO scene_synopses (work_abbrev, div1, div2, synopsis) VALUES
('Mac', 1, 1, 'Three witches meet on a heath in thunder and lightning. They agree to meet again after the battle to encounter Macbeth. Their cryptic chant — "Fair is foul, and foul is fair" — sets the play''s moral atmosphere of deception and evil.'),
('Mac', 1, 2, 'A wounded sergeant reports to King Duncan that Macbeth fought heroically against the rebel Macdonwald, killing him in single combat. Ross then brings news that Macbeth also defeated the treacherous Thane of Cawdor and repelled a Norwegian invasion. Duncan orders the Thane of Cawdor''s execution and decrees that Macbeth shall receive his title as a reward.'),
('Mac', 1, 3, 'The three witches greet Macbeth with prophecies: he is Thane of Glamis, he will be Thane of Cawdor, and he will be king hereafter. They also tell Banquo that he will be lesser than Macbeth yet greater, not king himself but father to a line of kings. Ross arrives and confirms Macbeth has been named Thane of Cawdor, making the first prophecy come true and sparking Macbeth''s dark ambition. Macbeth begins to contemplate murdering Duncan to seize the throne.'),
('Mac', 1, 4, 'Duncan executes the traitorous Thane of Cawdor and warmly welcomes Macbeth and Banquo, praising their valor. He names his son Malcolm as heir to the throne, which Macbeth privately registers as an obstacle to his ambition. Duncan announces he will visit Macbeth''s castle at Inverness, and Macbeth rides ahead to prepare, his thoughts turning toward murder.'),
('Mac', 1, 5, 'Lady Macbeth reads a letter from Macbeth describing the witches'' prophecies. She fears her husband is too full of the milk of human kindness to seize the crown through ruthless action. She calls on dark spirits to unsex her and fill her with cruelty. When Macbeth arrives and tells her Duncan is coming, she urges him to look innocent but act decisively, hinting at murder.'),
('Mac', 1, 6, 'King Duncan arrives at Macbeth''s castle at Inverness with his attendants and is welcomed by Lady Macbeth. Duncan praises the pleasant air of the castle and speaks warmly of Macbeth''s hospitality. Lady Macbeth plays the perfect hostess, hiding her murderous intentions beneath courteous words.'),
('Mac', 1, 7, 'Macbeth slips away from Duncan''s banquet and wrestles with his conscience. He lists reasons not to kill the king: Duncan is his kinsman, his guest, and a good ruler, and the murder would likely provoke outrage and retribution. He tells Lady Macbeth he will proceed no further. She attacks his courage, mocks him, and lays out a specific plan to kill Duncan while he sleeps and blame the grooms. Persuaded, Macbeth agrees to go through with the murder.'),
('Mac', 2, 1, 'Late at night, Banquo and his son Fleance keep watch in Macbeth''s castle. Banquo confesses he has been dreaming of the witches and senses evil in the air. Macbeth appears and they speak briefly before Banquo retires. Alone, Macbeth hallucinates a bloody dagger pointing toward Duncan''s chamber. He follows it, steeling himself to commit murder as the castle bell tolls.'),
('Mac', 2, 2, 'Lady Macbeth waits while Macbeth kills King Duncan offstage. Macbeth returns shaking, clutching the bloody daggers he was supposed to leave with the king''s grooms. He is so shattered he cannot return to plant the daggers. Lady Macbeth takes the daggers herself and smears the grooms with blood. Both hear knocking at the castle gate; Macbeth is tormented, wishing Duncan could wake, while Lady Macbeth urges him to wash his hands and go to bed.'),
('Mac', 2, 3, 'A drunken Porter imagines himself gatekeeper of hell as he stumbles to answer loud knocking. Macduff and Lennox have arrived to wake King Duncan. Macbeth greets them and leads Macduff to the king''s chamber. Macduff discovers Duncan murdered and raises the alarm. Macbeth kills the grooms before they can speak, claiming he acted in rage and grief. Duncan''s sons Malcolm and Donalbain, fearing they too are targets, decide to flee — Malcolm to England, Donalbain to Ireland.'),
('Mac', 2, 4, 'Ross speaks with an Old Man about the unnatural events surrounding Duncan''s murder: the sky was dark at noon, an owl killed a falcon, and Duncan''s horses turned wild and ate each other. Macduff reports that suspicion has fallen on Malcolm and Donalbain, who have fled, and that Macbeth has been named king. Macduff notes that he will not attend Macbeth''s coronation at Scone and plans to return to Fife, sensing that something is deeply wrong.'),
('Mac', 3, 1, 'Banquo suspects Macbeth killed Duncan to fulfill the witches'' prophecy and wonders about his own promised line of kings. Macbeth, now king, questions Banquo closely about his plans and then arranges for two murderers to kill Banquo and his son Fleance that evening. Macbeth reasons that Banquo''s survival poses a threat, and he refuses to let the witches'' prophecy of Banquo''s royal descendants come true.'),
('Mac', 3, 2, 'Lady Macbeth finds Macbeth distracted and miserable despite being king. Macbeth speaks of terrible dreams and envies Duncan''s peaceful sleep in death. He hints darkly that a terrible deed will be done that night without telling Lady Macbeth the details, invoking night and darkness to hide what he plans. Their partnership has shifted — Macbeth now acts alone, shutting Lady Macbeth out of his schemes.'),
('Mac', 3, 3, 'Three murderers ambush Banquo and Fleance on a road near the palace as they return on horseback. They kill Banquo but Fleance escapes in the darkness. One of the murderers goes to report what happened to Macbeth. The escape of Fleance leaves the prophecy about Banquo''s royal line still potentially open.'),
('Mac', 3, 4, 'Macbeth hosts a banquet for his court. One of the murderers arrives and reports Banquo''s death but Fleance''s escape. When Macbeth goes to sit, he sees the ghost of Banquo in his chair; only Macbeth can see it. He addresses the ghost with horror and guilt while Lady Macbeth tries to cover for him, telling the guests he has had fits since childhood. The ghost appears twice; the second time Macbeth''s ravings alarm the lords and Lady Macbeth dismisses the banquet. Alone, Macbeth resolves to seek out the witches again.'),
('Mac', 3, 5, 'The witch-goddess Hecate scolds the three witches for dealing with Macbeth without her. She orders them to meet her at the pit of Acheron where Macbeth will come to learn his fate. She plans to mislead him with confusing prophecies and visions that will build overconfidence and lead to his ruin.'),
('Mac', 3, 6, 'Lennox speaks ironically with a Lord about the convenient way suspicion has fallen on others for Duncan''s and Banquo''s murders. The Lord reports that Macduff has fled to England to join Malcolm and seek help from King Edward of England to raise an army against Macbeth. Macbeth is preparing for war, and the country suffers under his tyrannical rule.'),
('Mac', 4, 1, 'Macbeth visits the witches at a cavern where they are brewing a foul potion. They conjure three apparitions for him: an armed head warns him to beware Macduff; a bloody child tells him no man born of woman can harm him; a crowned child holding a tree says he will never be defeated until Birnam Wood comes to Dunsinane Hill. Macbeth is reassured but asks about Banquo''s line — the witches show him eight kings descended from Banquo. Furious, Macbeth learns Macduff has fled to England and resolves to kill Macduff''s entire family.'),
('Mac', 4, 2, 'Lady Macduff is left alone at her castle with her young son after Macduff''s sudden flight to England. She angrily tells Ross that her husband acted out of madness or cowardice in abandoning them. She tells her clever young son his father is dead, and the boy argues back wittily. Murderers burst in, kill the boy, and pursue Lady Macduff offstage as she flees.'),
('Mac', 4, 3, 'Malcolm tests Macduff''s loyalty by pretending to be a far worse person than Macbeth, confessing to vices of lust and greed. Macduff passes the test by despairing for Scotland rather than flattering Malcolm. Malcolm then reveals his true virtuous character and that King Edward has given them an army. Ross arrives from Scotland with the news that Macduff''s wife and children have all been murdered. Macduff is stricken with grief and rage, vowing personal revenge on Macbeth as they prepare to march on Scotland.'),
('Mac', 5, 1, 'A gentlewoman and a doctor observe Lady Macbeth sleepwalking at night. Lady Macbeth wrings her hands as though washing them and speaks fragments of the murders she and Macbeth committed: Duncan''s blood, Banquo''s killing, Lady Macduff''s death. The doctor recognizes her guilt-driven illness and says her sickness is beyond his medicine, needing divine rather than medical help.'),
('Mac', 5, 2, 'Scottish lords who have turned against Macbeth — Menteith, Caithness, Angus, and Lennox — march to join Malcolm''s approaching English army near Birnam Wood. They describe Macbeth fortifying Dunsinane Castle, his soldiers obeying him out of fear rather than love, and his mental state as disordered. They resolve to purge Scotland of his tyranny.'),
('Mac', 5, 3, 'Macbeth receives reports that soldiers are deserting to the English side. He clings to the witches'' prophecies — no man born of woman shall harm him, he cannot fall until Birnam Wood moves. He asks the doctor about Lady Macbeth''s illness and is told there is no medicine for a diseased mind. Macbeth dresses for battle, raging and desperate, ordering his armor even as he swings between resolve and despair.'),
('Mac', 5, 4, 'Malcolm orders each soldier to cut a bough from Birnam Wood to carry as camouflage, making the forest appear to move toward Dunsinane. Malcolm outlines the battle plan: Malcolm will lead one force, Macduff and Siward will lead another. Reports indicate Macbeth is pinned in his castle, abandoned by many of his men, who hope to escape Macbeth''s doom.'),
('Mac', 5, 5, 'Macbeth defies the siege from within his castle walls. He is brought news that Lady Macbeth has died, and he responds with hollow numbness, reflecting on the meaninglessness of life in his famous "Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow" speech. A messenger then reports that Birnam Wood appears to be moving toward the castle. Macbeth is shaken but calls for his armor and prepares to fight, preferring to die in battle than surrender.'),
('Mac', 5, 6, 'Malcolm, Siward, and Macduff arrive before Dunsinane Castle and throw down the leafy branches. Malcolm orders the drums to beat and the battle to begin. The opposing forces engage as the English and Scottish rebel army attacks Macbeth''s position.'),
('Mac', 5, 7, 'On the battlefield Macbeth encounters and kills young Siward, emboldened by the prophecy that no man born of woman can harm him. Macduff searches through the battlefield looking specifically for Macbeth, unwilling to kill common soldiers. Macduff finally hears Macbeth''s voice and moves toward him. Malcolm and old Siward enter the castle, finding it nearly taken.'),
('Mac', 5, 8, 'Macbeth and Macduff face each other on the battlefield. Macbeth declares no man born of woman can kill him. Macduff reveals he was born by caesarean section — "untimely ripped" from his mother''s womb — and so is not "born of woman" in the traditional sense. Macbeth realizes the witches'' prophecies have betrayed him with half-truths but fights on defiantly. Macduff kills Macbeth offstage and returns with his severed head. Malcolm is hailed as the new King of Scotland.'),
('Oth', 1, 1, 'Iago reveals to Roderigo that he hates Othello, who has passed him over for promotion and made Cassio his lieutenant instead. They wake Brabantio, a Venetian senator, in the middle of the night to tell him his daughter Desdemona has secretly eloped with Othello. Iago describes the marriage in crude racial terms to provoke Brabantio''s outrage. Brabantio, furious and believing Desdemona must have been bewitched, gathers men to confront Othello.'),
('Oth', 1, 2, 'Iago warns Othello that Brabantio is coming with armed men to arrest him. Othello is calm, confident that his military service to Venice will protect him. Cassio arrives with an urgent summons from the Duke of Venice about a Turkish fleet threatening Cyprus. Brabantio arrives and accuses Othello of using witchcraft to steal his daughter. All parties agree to bring the matter before the Duke.'),
('Oth', 1, 3, 'The Duke of Venice is dealing with reports of a Turkish fleet sailing toward Cyprus. Brabantio accuses Othello of bewitching Desdemona, but Othello explains he won her love by telling her the story of his adventurous life. Desdemona is brought in and confirms she loves Othello freely. The Duke sides with Othello and orders him to Cyprus to defend it. Desdemona insists on going with her husband. Roderigo despairs, but Iago promises to help him win Desdemona while privately plotting to use Cassio to destroy Othello.'),
('Oth', 2, 1, 'A storm disperses the Turkish fleet, eliminating the military threat to Cyprus. Cassio, then Iago and Desdemona, and finally Othello arrive safely at the port. While waiting, Iago observes the courteous behavior between Cassio and Desdemona and decides to use Cassio''s attentiveness as the basis of his plan against Othello. Iago persuades Roderigo that Desdemona loves Cassio and urges him to provoke Cassio into a brawl that night.'),
('Oth', 2, 2, 'A herald reads Othello''s proclamation to the people of Cyprus celebrating the destruction of the Turkish fleet and also celebrating his marriage to Desdemona. The people are invited to celebrate from this hour until eleven that night. This brief scene establishes the festive atmosphere before Iago''s plot begins to unfold.'),
('Oth', 2, 3, 'Othello leaves Cassio in charge of the night watch and retires with Desdemona. Iago gets Cassio drunk despite his protests that wine unsettles him. Roderigo provokes Cassio into a fight; Montano tries to intervene and is wounded by Cassio. The alarm bell rings and Othello arrives to find disorder. Iago feigns reluctance as he blames Cassio. Othello strips Cassio of his lieutenancy. Iago then advises Cassio to seek Desdemona''s help in being reinstated, setting the next stage of his trap.'),
('Oth', 3, 1, 'Cassio hires musicians to play outside Othello''s window as a morning serenade. The Clown dismisses them. Cassio asks the Clown to fetch Emilia, Iago''s wife and Desdemona''s attendant, so he can speak with Desdemona about being restored to Othello''s favor. Iago arranges for the Clown to bring Cassio in to see Desdemona privately, while Iago plans to keep Othello occupied elsewhere.'),
('Oth', 3, 2, 'Othello sends letters to the Senate with Iago and then goes to inspect the fortifications of Cyprus. This very brief scene establishes Othello''s movements so that Iago can arrange for Othello to find Cassio alone with Desdemona.'),
('Oth', 3, 3, 'Desdemona promises Cassio she will plead his case to Othello. When Othello approaches, Cassio slips away. Iago murmurs cryptically about this, planting suspicion. Desdemona immediately begins pressing Othello to reinstate Cassio, which irritates Othello under Iago''s influence. Iago poisons Othello''s mind systematically, warning of jealousy — "the green-eyed monster" — while feeding it. He asks Othello to watch whether Desdemona presses Cassio''s case too eagerly. When Desdemona drops a handkerchief Othello gave her, Emilia picks it up and gives it to Iago. Iago plans to plant it in Cassio''s room. By the scene''s end, Othello''s trust in Desdemona is shattered and he demands proof, threatening Iago if he cannot produce it.'),
('Oth', 3, 4, 'Desdemona asks the Clown to find Cassio and then asks Othello about Cassio''s reinstatement. Othello instead demands to see the handkerchief he gave her — a family heirloom with magical properties. Desdemona does not have it but tries to deflect; Othello grows increasingly alarmed and leaves in a cold fury. Cassio arrives and Desdemona explains she cannot help him just now. Bianca, a courtesan who loves Cassio, appears; Cassio gives her the handkerchief — which Iago planted in his room — and asks her to copy the embroidery.'),
('Oth', 4, 1, 'Iago tells Othello that Cassio has confessed to sleeping with Desdemona. Othello falls into a trance; when he comes around, Iago stages a conversation with Cassio about Bianca so that Othello can watch from a distance. Othello believes the laughter is about Desdemona. Bianca arrives and throws the handkerchief back at Cassio in front of Othello, calling it a gift from a lover. Othello is now convinced. He tells Iago to kill Cassio and decides to strangle Desdemona in her bed. Lodovico arrives from Venice with a letter recalling Othello to Venice and naming Cassio governor of Cyprus; Othello strikes Desdemona in front of the ambassador.'),
('Oth', 4, 2, 'Othello questions Emilia about Desdemona''s faithfulness; Emilia defends her strongly. Othello then confronts Desdemona, calling her a whore and dismissing her denials. Desdemona weeps to Emilia, unable to understand what has changed. Iago pretends concern and assures her Othello is only troubled by state business. Roderigo complains to Iago that Desdemona is no closer to him despite his gifts; Iago reveals that Cassio must be killed to keep Othello in Cyprus and Roderigo therefore near Desdemona, and persuades Roderigo to murder Cassio.'),
('Oth', 4, 3, 'Othello tells Desdemona to dismiss Emilia and go to bed. Desdemona and Emilia speak as Desdemona prepares for bed, sensing a coming tragedy. Desdemona sings the Willow Song, a mournful ballad about a woman abandoned by her love. Emilia and Desdemona discuss the idea of wives being unfaithful to husbands; Emilia takes a pragmatic view while Desdemona says she could not act dishonorably for the whole world.'),
('Oth', 5, 1, 'Iago stations Roderigo in the dark to ambush Cassio. Roderigo attacks Cassio but only wounds him; Cassio wounds Roderigo. Iago stabs Cassio in the leg from behind and then slips away. Othello hears Cassio''s cries and takes them as confirmation of Iago''s loyalty before going to kill Desdemona. Lodovico and Gratiano arrive; Iago returns and kills Roderigo to silence him, pretending it is mercy. Bianca is blamed and taken away with the wounded Cassio. Emilia is sent to tell Othello what has happened.'),
('Oth', 5, 2, 'Othello enters Desdemona''s bedchamber, kisses her, and smothers her despite her pleas of innocence. Emilia arrives and realizes what has happened. When Othello says Iago revealed Desdemona''s infidelity, Emilia is horrified and shouts the truth loudly. Iago, Montano, and Gratiano enter; Emilia exposes Iago''s lies about the handkerchief despite his threats. Iago kills Emilia and flees. Othello wounds Iago when caught, then kills himself with a hidden weapon rather than face disgrace, dying over Desdemona''s body. Iago is taken alive for torture and punishment; Cassio is confirmed as governor of Cyprus.'),
('Lr', 1, 1, 'King Lear announces he will divide his kingdom among his three daughters based on how much each says she loves him. Goneril and Regan deliver lavish speeches of devotion. Cordelia, his youngest and favorite, refuses to flatter him and says she loves him as a daughter should — no more, no less. Enraged, Lear disowns Cordelia and divides the kingdom between Goneril and Regan. The Earl of Kent objects and is banished. The King of France agrees to marry Cordelia despite her lost inheritance. Edmund, the illegitimate son of the Earl of Gloucester, is also introduced.'),
('Lr', 1, 2, 'Edmund, Gloucester''s illegitimate son, schemes to steal his legitimate brother Edgar''s inheritance. He forges a letter suggesting Edgar plans to murder their father Gloucester to gain his estate sooner. He shows the letter to Gloucester, who is horrified. Edmund then convinces Edgar that Gloucester is furious with him and he should go armed. Edmund relishes his villainy and plans to betray both brother and father.'),
('Lr', 1, 3, 'Goneril complains to her steward Oswald about Lear''s behavior since coming to stay with her. Lear''s knights have been rowdy, and Lear himself acts dismissively toward her authority. She instructs Oswald to treat Lear''s knights coolly and plans to write to her sister Regan to coordinate their response to their father''s demands.'),
('Lr', 1, 4, 'Kent, disguised, offers his services to Lear and is accepted. Lear''s Fool arrives and mocks Lear repeatedly for having given away his kingdom. Goneril appears and criticizes Lear''s knights as riotous and demands he dismiss half of them. Lear is furious and curses Goneril, calling down sterility and grief upon her. He resolves to go to Regan instead. The Fool continues to comment on how foolish Lear was to give away his power.'),
('Lr', 1, 5, 'Lear sends Kent with letters to Regan and prepares to travel to her. The Fool continues to mock Lear for his folly in dividing the kingdom, warning him that Regan will treat him no better than Goneril. Lear begins to sense the onset of madness and prays to keep his wits. This short scene marks Lear''s first movement toward Regan''s house and the deepening of his psychological crisis.'),
('Lr', 2, 1, 'Edmund tricks Edgar into fleeing by pretending Gloucester is coming to arrest him, then cuts his own arm and tells Gloucester that Edgar attacked him when challenged about treason. Gloucester is deceived and disinherits Edgar, naming Edmund his heir. Cornwall and Regan arrive at Gloucester''s castle, having left home to avoid hosting Lear. They welcome Edmund and treat him as a man of worth, rewarding him for his supposed loyalty.'),
('Lr', 2, 2, 'Kent, carrying Lear''s letter to Regan, meets Oswald and beats him for his insolence toward Lear. Cornwall and Regan have Kent put in the stocks as punishment. Gloucester objects that it is a disrespect to the king, but Cornwall insists. Kent waits in the stocks, reads a letter from Cordelia suggesting she knows of his disguise, and stoically awaits dawn.'),
('Lr', 2, 3, 'Edgar, fleeing and hunted, decides to disguise himself as Tom o'' Bedlam — a wandering mad beggar — to survive. He will smear himself with filth, tangle his hair, and howl like a madman. This short monologue establishes his disguise before he reappears in the storm scenes alongside Lear.'),
('Lr', 2, 4, 'Lear arrives at Gloucester''s castle to find Kent in the stocks, evidence of Regan''s disrespect. Regan and Cornwall refuse to come out to greet him, then emerge but side with Goneril against Lear''s demands. Goneril arrives and Regan takes her hand. The sisters negotiate Lear''s retinue down from a hundred knights to fifty to twenty-five to none. Lear, overwhelmed by their ingratitude, rushes out into a storm beginning on the heath, accompanied only by the Fool. The sisters coldly order the gates shut against him.'),
('Lr', 3, 1, 'During the raging storm, Kent finds a gentleman and learns that Lear is on the heath with only the Fool, raging against the weather. Kent reveals to the gentleman that France has secretly landed in England with agents already operating there. He sends the gentleman to Dover to find Cordelia and tell her what is happening to her father, giving him a ring to identify himself to her.'),
('Lr', 3, 2, 'Lear storms on the heath in the tempest, calling on the winds and lightning to destroy the world. He rages at the ingratitude of his daughters and begins to slide toward madness, though he still has flashes of self-awareness. The Fool tries to comfort him and urges him to seek shelter. Kent finds them and leads them toward a hovel. Lear begins to feel compassion for the Fool and for all homeless people in storms, recognizing his own previous failures as king.'),
('Lr', 3, 3, 'Inside Gloucester''s castle, Gloucester tells Edmund secretly that he has received a letter indicating that France has invaded to avenge Lear''s mistreatment. He plans to find Lear on the heath and help him, even at risk to himself. Edmund, in an aside, decides to betray his father to Cornwall to advance his own position. This scene reveals Gloucester''s loyalty and Edmund''s treachery.'),
('Lr', 3, 4, 'Lear, Kent, and the Fool take shelter in a hovel on the heath. Inside they find Edgar disguised as Poor Tom the mad beggar. Lear is fascinated by Tom and repeatedly asks what has reduced him to this state. Lear begins to tear off his own clothes to be as bare as Tom, whom he calls a "poor, bare, forked animal." Gloucester arrives with a torch and leads them toward a farm building where there is fire and food.'),
('Lr', 3, 5, 'Edmund delivers Gloucester''s letter about the French invasion to Cornwall. Cornwall rewards Edmund with the title of Earl of Gloucester and orders Edmund to find his father. Cornwall plans to arrest Gloucester for treason. Edmund pretends to be tormented by the conflict between his filial duty and his loyalty to Cornwall.'),
('Lr', 3, 6, 'In a farmhouse outbuilding, Lear''s madness deepens as he stages a mock trial of Goneril and Regan using the Fool and Edgar as judges. Gloucester hurries them along, having learned of a plot to kill Lear; he urges Kent to take Lear to Dover. Kent, the Fool, and Edgar carry the sleeping Lear to safety. Edgar muses alone on his own suffering in comparison to Lear''s, finding some comfort in not being alone in misery.'),
('Lr', 3, 7, 'Cornwall, Regan, and Goneril receive word of the French landing and order Gloucester arrested as a traitor. Cornwall and Regan interrogate Gloucester brutally about his communication with France. When Gloucester defiantly invokes the gods and says Cordelia''s forces will avenge Lear, Cornwall gouges out one of Gloucester''s eyes. A servant tries to stop Cornwall and wounds him; Regan kills the servant. Cornwall then gouges out Gloucester''s second eye. Gloucester cries out for Edmund, only to be told that Edmund betrayed him. Gloucester, blinded, is thrust out of his own castle to "smell his way to Dover."'),
('Lr', 4, 1, 'Edgar, disguised as Poor Tom, encounters his blinded father Gloucester being led by an old man. Gloucester, unable to see, says he would give everything to see Edgar once more. He expresses despair and wants to be taken to the cliffs at Dover. Edgar, still disguised, offers to lead him there. The scene is a painful reunion in which Gloucester does not know his guide is his own son.'),
('Lr', 4, 2, 'Goneril and Edmund arrive at Goneril''s palace. Albany, Goneril''s husband, has heard about what happened to Lear and Gloucester and rebukes Goneril fiercely, calling her actions monstrous. Goneril dismisses him as a coward. A messenger brings news that Cornwall has died from the wound the servant gave him, and that Gloucester''s eyes were gouged out. Albany is horrified and vows justice against Gloucester''s tormentors. Goneril is alarmed that Regan, now widowed, might pursue Edmund.'),
('Lr', 4, 3, 'Kent speaks with a Gentleman who has seen Cordelia reading the letter about her father''s treatment. The Gentleman describes Cordelia''s tearful reaction — she wept with restraint and dignity, like "sunshine and rain at once." Kent explains that Lear is in Dover but is too ashamed to face Cordelia after having rejected her. Cordelia''s army is encamped nearby and she is actively seeking her father.'),
('Lr', 4, 4, 'Cordelia sends soldiers to find Lear, who has been seen wandering and singing, crowned with weeds and flowers. A doctor assures Cordelia that sleep and medicine can restore Lear''s reason. A messenger brings news that the British forces of Albany and Cornwall are advancing. Cordelia says she has come not for political ambition but entirely out of love and duty to her father.'),
('Lr', 4, 5, 'Regan questions Oswald, who is carrying a letter from Goneril to Edmund. Regan suspects the letter contains a romantic proposal to Edmund and tries to read it; Oswald refuses to hand it over. Regan reveals she has already spoken to Edmund about marriage and asks Oswald to tell Goneril that Edmund is more appropriate for her (Regan). She gives Oswald a token to give Edmund and asks him to kill Gloucester if he encounters him.'),
('Lr', 4, 6, 'Edgar leads the blind Gloucester to what he pretends is the cliff''s edge at Dover. He describes an elaborate imaginary view of the sea far below. Gloucester jumps forward — but onto flat ground. Edgar then pretends to be a different person who saw Gloucester fall from a great height and survive, treating it as a miracle. Gloucester resolves to accept his suffering. Lear appears, wearing his crown of flowers and raving. He and Gloucester have a fragmented, moving encounter. Cordelia''s soldiers find Lear and take him gently to be cared for. Oswald arrives and tries to kill Gloucester for the reward; Edgar kills Oswald in a fight. Among Oswald''s papers Edgar finds Goneril''s letter to Edmund plotting Albany''s death.'),
('Lr', 4, 7, 'Lear is brought before Cordelia, clean and restored in fresh clothes after sleep. He wakes in a daze, at first not recognizing Cordelia, believing he has died. When he realizes it is Cordelia he falls to his knees asking her forgiveness. Cordelia gently reassures him and they are reconciled. Lear''s mind is not fully healed but his madness has largely lifted. A report comes that the British forces are approaching for battle.'),
('Lr', 5, 1, 'Edmund meets with Albany and Regan on the English side. Goneril and Albany arrive; there is obvious tension between the two sisters over Edmund. Albany agrees to fight against France to defend England but intends to pardon Lear and Cordelia if they win. Edgar, still disguised, gives Albany the letter exposing Goneril''s plot against his life and asks Albany to read it before battle. Edmund intends to show mercy to Lear and Cordelia only as long as he needs Albany, then do as he pleases.'),
('Lr', 5, 2, 'The battle takes place offstage. Edgar leaves Gloucester near a tree and goes to fight. He returns quickly to report that the battle is lost, Lear and Cordelia are captured. Gloucester is tempted to despair, but Edgar urges him to keep moving: "Men must endure their going hence even as their coming hither."'),
('Lr', 5, 3, 'Edmund sends Lear and Cordelia to prison with secret orders for their execution. Lear is reconciled to prison as long as he is with Cordelia. Albany confronts Goneril and Edmund; Edgar appears in armor and challenges Edmund to a duel. Edmund is mortally wounded by Edgar, who reveals his identity. Goneril, exposed by the letter Edgar gave Albany, poisons Regan and kills herself. As Edmund lies dying, he hears that Gloucester died of a broken heart when Edgar revealed himself. Edmund tries to countermand his orders to execute Cordelia, but too late — Cordelia has been hanged. Lear enters carrying Cordelia''s body and dies of grief over her. Albany resolves to restore the kingdom; Edgar is left to rule.'),
('JC', 1, 1, 'Two tribunes, Flavius and Murellus, encounter Roman commoners celebrating Julius Caesar''s return to Rome after defeating Pompey''s sons. The tribunes scold the people for forgetting Pompey, whom they once adored, and disperse them. They go to remove decorations honoring Caesar from his statues, fearing Caesar''s growing power will threaten the republic.'),
('JC', 1, 2, 'During the festival of Lupercal, Caesar is warned by a soothsayer to "beware the Ides of March." Cassius probes Brutus about his feelings toward Caesar, arguing that Caesar is dangerously ambitious and no better a man than they are. He tells the story of nearly drowning alongside Caesar to make the point that Caesar is physically weak and unfit to rule alone. Loud cheering is heard, and Casca reports that Caesar has been offered the crown three times by Antony and refused it each time — but reluctantly. Cassius, already planning against Caesar, begins to recruit Brutus.'),
('JC', 1, 3, 'A violent storm tears through Rome on the night of March 14th. Casca meets Cicero and describes terrifying omens — a lion in the Capitol, men on fire, an owl hooting at noon. He interprets these as signs against Caesar. Cassius meets Casca and interprets the same omens differently, as signs of the danger Caesar represents. He recruits Casca into the conspiracy. Cinna brings word that many senators will side with the conspirators if Brutus joins them, and Cassius plans to plant forged letters for Brutus to find.'),
('JC', 2, 1, 'Brutus, sleepless and conflicted, reads one of Cassius''s planted letters urging Rome to act against Caesar. He decides Caesar must be killed before he is crowned, comparing him to a serpent''s egg that must be destroyed before it hatches. The conspirators — Cassius, Casca, Decius, Cinna, Metellus, and Trebonius — meet secretly at Brutus''s house. Brutus overrules suggestions to kill Antony as well, a decision that will prove fatal. Portia, Brutus''s wife, confronts him about his distraction and demands to share his secret, even wounding herself in the thigh to prove her worth.'),
('JC', 2, 2, 'Caesar''s wife Calpurnia has dreamed of Caesar''s statue spouting blood and begs him not to go to the Capitol. Caesar initially agrees to stay home. Decius, one of the conspirators, arrives and reinterprets the dream favorably — blood as a life-giving fountain — and taunts Caesar with the suggestion that the Senate might mock his fear. Caesar is persuaded and decides to go. The other conspirators arrive to escort him, including Brutus, hiding their murderous intentions.'),
('JC', 2, 3, 'Artemidorus stands in a street reading aloud from a letter he has written warning Caesar by name of each conspirator. He plans to hand it to Caesar as he passes on the way to the Capitol. This very brief scene raises the question of whether Caesar will be warned in time.'),
('JC', 2, 4, 'Portia, knowing of the conspiracy, is agitated and sends the servant Lucius to the Capitol to observe how Brutus looks. She encounters the soothsayer who is going to warn Caesar and asks if he knows of any harm intended to Caesar today; he says he fears there may be harm but knows none. Portia struggles to keep the secret and not betray herself.'),
('JC', 3, 1, 'Caesar arrives at the Capitol and dismisses the soothsayer''s warning and Artemidorus''s letter. The conspirators surround him under the pretext of pleading for a banished man. At a signal, they stab Caesar one by one; Brutus gives the final blow and Caesar falls, saying "Et tu, Brute?" Chaos ensues; Antony flees briefly. Brutus speaks calmly and asks people not to panic. Antony returns and shakes hands with each bloody conspirator, inwardly vowing revenge. Over Brutus''s objection, Cassius allows Antony to speak at Caesar''s funeral. Antony, alone with Caesar''s body, promises to avenge the murder.'),
('JC', 3, 2, 'Brutus addresses the crowd in the Forum and justifies the killing of Caesar as necessary to preserve Roman liberty — Caesar was ambitious and would have enslaved them. The crowd is persuaded and wants to crown Brutus. Then Antony speaks, repeatedly calling the conspirators "honorable men" while describing Caesar''s generosity to Rome and reading Caesar''s will, which leaves money to every citizen. Antony displays Caesar''s wounded body and works the crowd into a fury. The mob riots, hunting down the conspirators to kill them, and Rome descends into civil war.'),
('JC', 3, 3, 'The poet Cinna is caught by an angry mob. He tells them his name is Cinna the Poet, not Cinna the conspirator, but the crowd kills him anyway for having a conspirator''s name. This brief scene dramatizes the blind fury of the mob that Antony has unleashed.'),
('JC', 4, 1, 'Antony, Octavius, and Lepidus meet to draw up a list of men to be killed in the political purge following Caesar''s death. Antony sends Lepidus on an errand, then dismisses him as unfit for real power — merely useful to share the blame for difficult decisions. Antony and Octavius plan their military campaign against Brutus and Cassius, who have gathered an army in the East.'),
('JC', 4, 2, 'Brutus and Cassius are encamped with their armies outside Sardis. Cassius arrives to complain that Brutus has wronged him. Brutus tells him to come inside the tent so they can not argue in front of their soldiers, where dissension would be harmful. The scene establishes the rift between them before their heated confrontation inside.'),
('JC', 4, 3, 'Inside the tent, Cassius and Brutus argue bitterly. Cassius accuses Brutus of punishing Lucius Pella, an officer who took bribes from the Sardians, after Cassius had written recommending leniency. Brutus attacks Cassius for compromising his honor by condoning corruption. They nearly come to blows before reconciling and sharing wine. Brutus reveals that Portia has killed herself by swallowing hot coals. A poet who tries to intervene is dismissed. Cassius and Brutus discuss strategy: Brutus insists on marching to Philippi to meet the enemy before they grow stronger; Cassius argues against it but defers to Brutus''s judgment. That night, the ghost of Caesar appears to Brutus and says they will meet again at Philippi.'),
('JC', 5, 1, 'The two armies face each other on the plains of Philippi. Octavius and Antony parley with Brutus and Cassius, and the four exchange insults. Cassius tells Messala he has bad omens — two eagles that accompanied the army have flown away, replaced by ravens and crows. Cassius and Brutus say farewell in case they do not meet again after the battle, and Cassius says he will not be taken prisoner.'),
('JC', 5, 2, 'Brutus sends word to Cassius to attack Octavius''s forces immediately, as he sees an opening. Brutus presses Octavius''s troops back and his men begin to loot the enemy camp prematurely. This very brief scene sets up the uneven fortunes of the two wings of the conspirators'' army.'),
('JC', 5, 3, 'Cassius''s forces are overwhelmed by Antony''s troops. Titinius is sent to report on whether approaching horsemen are friend or foe. Pindarus, Cassius''s servant, reports that Titinius has been surrounded and apparently captured. Believing the battle lost and Titinius dead, the despairing Cassius asks Pindarus to kill him, and Pindarus runs him through with his own sword. Titinius returns with a wreath of victory — the horsemen were friends celebrating Brutus''s success. He places the wreath on the dead Cassius''s head and kills himself in grief. Brutus arrives and mourns Cassius as "the last of all the Romans."'),
('JC', 5, 4, 'Brutus''s troops are hard pressed in battle. Young Cato, the son of Cato and Portia''s brother, fights bravely and is killed. Lucilius pretends to be Brutus to draw enemy soldiers away and is captured. Antony recognizes Lucilius and orders him treated well, not killed, because a man this loyal is worth having on their side.'),
('JC', 5, 5, 'Brutus and a few remaining followers rest, defeated. He asks Clitus and Dardanius to help him die; they refuse in horror. He tells Volumnius that Caesar''s ghost has appeared to him twice and the hour has come; he asks Volumnius to hold his sword while he runs on it, but Volumnius also refuses. The soldiers flee as Antony''s forces close in. Brutus asks Strato to hold his sword and turns himself on it, dying. Antony and Octavius find Brutus; Antony eulogizes him as the noblest Roman of them all, the only conspirator who acted for Rome''s good rather than envy of Caesar.'),
('Ant', 1, 1, 'Two of Antony''s soldiers, Philo and Demetrius, lament that their general has abandoned his military greatness for love of Cleopatra. Antony and Cleopatra enter with attendants; a messenger from Rome brings news, but Antony dismisses it, declaring that Rome is nothing and only this love matters. Cleopatra playfully taunts him about his wife Fulvia and his duty. The soldiers watch with disapproval as their great general appears besotted and diminished.'),
('Ant', 1, 2, 'Cleopatra''s attendants Iras and Charmian have their fortunes told by a soothsayer, who predicts both will outlive their mistress. Enobarbus and others joke about the news from Rome. Antony is brought news that Fulvia has warred against his brother Lucius, then both fought against Caesar; and that Labienus has invaded Rome''s eastern territories for Parthia. Antony receives word that Fulvia is dead. He is momentarily saddened, then resolves to break free of Cleopatra''s enchantment and return to Rome to deal with the political crisis. He tells Enobarbus he must leave Egypt.'),
('Ant', 1, 3, 'Cleopatra tries to hold Antony by feigning illness and emotional scenes. When he tells her Fulvia is dead and he must return to Rome for urgent affairs, she mocks him and accuses him of insincerity — if he could leave Fulvia so easily, he could leave her too. Antony defends himself with increasing frustration. Cleopatra finally lets him go, bidding him farewell with both love and calculated reproach.'),
('Ant', 1, 4, 'In Rome, Octavius Caesar reads letters about Antony''s behavior in Egypt with disapproval, criticizing his debauchery and negligence. Lepidus urges him to be patient with Antony''s faults. Caesar reports serious military threats: Pompey has a powerful fleet, and Menecrates and Menas are raiding the Italian coast and stirring up the common people. He wishes Antony were the soldier he once was.'),
('Ant', 1, 5, 'Cleopatra, in Alexandria without Antony, thinks of him constantly and asks for mandragora to sleep through the time until he returns. She questions her messenger Alexas about how Antony looked and what he said. She sends Charmian to find twenty messengers to carry her letters to Antony. She compares herself to the great queens who loved great men before her.'),
('Ant', 2, 1, 'Pompey meets with Menecrates and Menas on the coast and expresses confidence in his sea power. He believes Antony is still in Alexandria, held there by Cleopatra, and that Caesar and Lepidus are not strong enough without him. Varrius brings surprising news: Antony has left Egypt and is coming to Rome. Pompey reassesses and notes that the triumvirs'' love of power will bind them together against him.'),
('Ant', 2, 2, 'Enobarbus mediates as Antony and Caesar meet. Their meeting is initially tense, with Antony defending his behavior in Egypt and his role in Fulvia''s war. They agree to a political alliance sealed by Antony''s marriage to Caesar''s sister Octavia. Agrippa proposes the match and both sides agree. Privately, Enobarbus tells Maecenas and Agrippa that the match is doomed — Antony will never leave Cleopatra. Enobarbus gives his famous description of Cleopatra on her barge, saying "Age cannot wither her, nor custom stale her infinite variety."'),
('Ant', 2, 3, 'Antony and Octavia make their farewells, Antony promising faithfulness. The soothsayer warns Antony privately that his fortune always falls when near Caesar''s and urges him to return east. Antony admits privately that the soothsayer speaks truth, and that his pleasure lies in the East. He sends Ventidius to Parthia to fight for Rome while he considers returning to Cleopatra.'),
('Ant', 2, 4, 'A brief scene showing Lepidus, Agrippa, and Maecenas making their separate ways: Lepidus is not well and will catch up later; Agrippa and Maecenas will travel to Rome ahead of Antony and Octavia. It establishes the movement of key figures toward the next set of events.'),
('Ant', 2, 5, 'In Alexandria, Cleopatra passes the time without Antony by fishing, billiards, and hearing music. A messenger arrives from Rome and she greets him with dramatic anticipation. When he reveals that Antony has married Octavia, Cleopatra erupts in fury and beats him. She demands to know what Octavia looks like, and the terrified messenger describes her in unflattering terms to please the queen. Charmian counsels Cleopatra to calm herself.'),
('Ant', 2, 6, 'Pompey meets the triumvirs on land near Misenum to negotiate. Pompey has an agreement in principle: he will give up Sicily and Sardinia in exchange for Rome''s help against pirates and a large supply of wheat to Rome. He comes close to accepting, notes the insult of Antony''s father being refused help, is mollified by Antony, and signs the treaty. Enobarbus and Menas, the pirate captains, talk privately and Menas offers to cut the cable of the galley carrying all three triumvirs and kill them all, making Pompey master of the world. Pompey refuses — he wishes it had been done without being asked, but cannot sanction it now that it has been proposed.'),
('Ant', 2, 7, 'The triumvirs and Pompey feast aboard Pompey''s galley. Everyone becomes increasingly drunk except Octavius, who dislikes the revelry. Lepidus is carried off helplessly drunk. Menas pulls Pompey aside and again suggests killing the triumvirs; Pompey again refuses. The scene ends with a drunken dance and song. Enobarbus carries the sleeping Lepidus off the ship.'),
('Ant', 3, 1, 'Ventidius has defeated the Parthians and killed Pacorus, the Parthian prince, avenging Crassus''s defeat. He speaks with Silius about why he will not pursue the Parthians further, explaining that a subordinate who wins too much glory risks provoking a superior''s jealousy — it would reflect badly on Antony, who sent him, if Ventidius outshone him. They head to Athens to report to Antony.'),
('Ant', 3, 2, 'Agrippa and Enobarbus exchange cynical observations about Lepidus''s sycophancy to both Antony and Caesar. Antony bids farewell to Caesar and gives Octavia to his care. Caesar is visibly moved and instructs Octavia to write to him often. Enobarbus privately predicts to Agrippa that Antony will return to Cleopatra and that the bond between Antony and Caesar will ultimately break, spelling Antony''s ruin.'),
('Ant', 3, 3, 'Cleopatra interrogates the returning messenger about Octavia: her height, her hair, her eyes, her age, and her manner of speaking. The messenger delivers unflattering descriptions — she is low-voiced, low in stature, and round-faced — pleasing Cleopatra, who concludes Octavia is not worthy of Antony and that all will be well.'),
('Ant', 3, 4, 'In Athens, Antony shows Octavia letters revealing that Caesar has made war on Pompey, spoken publicly against Antony, and used him with contempt in his will. Antony is furious. Octavia urges him not to go to war against her brother and asks to go to Rome as a mediator. Antony agrees; she departs. With Octavia gone, Antony will be free to return to Cleopatra.'),
('Ant', 3, 5, 'Enobarbus and Eros exchange news: Caesar has defeated Pompey at sea with Lepidus''s help, then accused Lepidus of treason and imprisoned him. Antony is outraged. Caesar has refused him permission to ride through his territories with armed troops. Antony prepares for war against Caesar.'),
('Ant', 3, 6, 'In Rome, Caesar is incensed that Antony, now back in Alexandria, has publicly crowned Cleopatra and her children as rulers of eastern kingdoms. Octavia arrives, poorly attended, and Caesar contrasts her humble appearance with Cleopatra''s pomp. He tells Octavia that Antony has already left and is with Cleopatra again, gathering forces for war against Caesar. Octavia is heartbroken.'),
('Ant', 3, 7, 'Enobarbus argues strongly against Cleopatra''s insistence on fighting Caesar at sea — her presence is a distraction and the sea is Caesar''s strength. Antony overrides him and decides to fight at sea to match Caesar''s challenge. A soldier urges Antony to fight on land where he is experienced and powerful; Antony again refuses. Caesar''s forces are moving fast and have already taken Toryne. The scene establishes the strategic mistake of fighting by sea.'),
('Ant', 3, 8, 'Caesar orders his forces not to engage Antony''s land forces until the sea battle has been decided. This brief scene establishes Caesar''s tactical plan and the waiting posture of his armies.'),
('Ant', 3, 9, 'Antony positions his troops to observe the sea battle from a hill. He gives orders for a squadron under Taurus to hold and not advance until the naval battle is resolved. This very brief scene mirrors the preceding one from Antony''s side.'),
('Ant', 3, 10, 'Enobarbus and Scarus watch in horror as Cleopatra''s sixty ships suddenly flee from the sea battle at a critical moment. Antony, seeing Cleopatra''s retreat, abandons his fleet and follows her. His sailors are left without leadership. The battle is lost. Scarus is furious and ashamed; Enobarbus is shattered by what he has witnessed. Antony has thrown away an almost certain victory to follow Cleopatra.'),
('Ant', 3, 11, 'Antony sits alone on shore in the aftermath of the battle, overwhelmed by shame at having fled and lost. Cleopatra enters with her attendants and Antony''s officers. Antony is too ashamed to look at them and sends them to Caesar to negotiate. He tells Cleopatra he knows she has destroyed him but cannot bring himself to be angry with her; she weeps and he comforts her. He resolves to write to Caesar.'),
('Ant', 3, 12, 'Dolabella brings Antony''s ambassador to Caesar in Egypt. Antony asks to live in Egypt or as a private citizen in Athens; Cleopatra asks that the Egyptian crown pass to her heirs. Caesar refuses Antony''s requests outright but tells Euphronius he will grant Cleopatra''s requests if she drives Antony from Egypt or kills him. He sends Thidias to Cleopatra with persuasive words and gifts, to tempt her away from Antony.'),
('Ant', 3, 13, 'Thidias arrives and begins to flatter Cleopatra, suggesting she should abandon Antony and accept Caesar''s protection. Antony enters and catches them; he has Thidias whipped and sent back to Caesar. He furiously accuses Cleopatra of betraying him. Cleopatra calms him with expressions of love and loyalty. Enobarbus privately decides that Antony has lost his judgment and resolves to leave him. Antony rallies, calls for wine and feasting, vowing to fight Caesar again and win.'),
('Ant', 4, 1, 'Caesar reads Antony''s challenge to single combat and laughs at him. Maecenas advises that Antony''s anger makes him reckless and that he should be attacked now while his passions are inflamed. Caesar orders a general feast for his troops and prepares for battle the next day. He intends to use all the kings and soldiers who have defected from Antony against him.'),
('Ant', 4, 2, 'Antony, knowing the odds are against him, holds a farewell supper for his servants and soldiers. He becomes emotional, telling them he may not be their master much longer and thanking them for their service. Enobarbus is moved to tears but troubled by Antony''s melancholy. Cleopatra and the servants do not know how to interpret the farewell, and Antony tries to lighten the mood, saying he was only preparing them to fight harder tomorrow.'),
('Ant', 4, 3, 'Antony''s soldiers keep watch during the night before the battle. They hear mysterious music — hautboys playing underground — which they interpret as the god Hercules, Antony''s patron deity, abandoning him. The men are shaken by this omen and speak of it with foreboding.'),
('Ant', 4, 4, 'On the morning of battle, Cleopatra and Eros help Antony into his armor. Antony is energized and martial; Cleopatra fumbles with the buckles and Antony is affectionately amused. A soldier brings good news that Enobarbus has defected but left his treasure behind; Antony sends the treasure after Enobarbus with kind words. Antony kisses Cleopatra farewell and marches to battle with a soldier''s spirit.'),
('Ant', 4, 5, 'Antony is told that Enobarbus has defected to Caesar. Rather than curse him, Antony sends Enobarbus''s treasure after him. Enobarbus, receiving his master''s generous response, is overwhelmed with shame and remorse for his betrayal. He resolves to find a ditch somewhere to die in, unable to bear the disgrace. This brief scene sets up Enobarbus''s death from grief.'),
('Ant', 4, 6, 'At Caesar''s camp, Enobarbus hears that Caesar is using former followers of Antony in the front line so that Antony will be fighting against his own men. Enobarbus receives Antony''s treasure and a letter with kind words and is devastated by shame. He cannot outlive this dishonor and goes to find a place to die.'),
('Ant', 4, 7, 'During the land battle, Antony''s forces are winning. Agrippa''s troops fall back. Scarus has been wounded but fights on. Antony and his men return victorious from the field, with Caesar''s forces in retreat. Antony praises Scarus''s valor and leads him to Cleopatra to receive her thanks.'),
('Ant', 4, 8, 'Antony returns triumphant to Alexandria after the land battle victory. He praises Scarus to Cleopatra and asks her to give Scarus her hand to kiss. He thanks all his soldiers and invites them to celebrate. He holds Cleopatra and speaks of their love with renewed passion, promising another victory in the next day''s battle. The mood is celebratory but shadowed by the knowledge that the battle is not over.'),
('Ant', 4, 9, 'In Caesar''s camp at night, sentries hear Enobarbus lamenting and calling on the moon to witness his repentance for betraying Antony. He speaks Antony''s name and dies, apparently of a broken heart. The soldiers report his death to Enobarbus''s superiors. This scene marks the death of the play''s most clear-eyed observer.'),
('Ant', 4, 10, 'Antony and Scarus go to observe the sea battle, positioning themselves to watch. Antony notes that Caesar has dared him to fight at sea again. The scene is extremely brief, setting up the next scene in which the sea battle''s outcome is reported.'),
('Ant', 4, 11, 'Caesar orders his forces to rest on the hills and instructs them not to engage Antony''s land army if he makes it to land after the sea battle. This minimal scene positions Caesar''s troops and contributes to the structural mirroring of the two commanders'' perspectives.'),
('Ant', 4, 12, 'Antony watches as his fleet rows toward Caesar''s ships but then the Egyptian fleet surrenders without fighting. Antony is furious, believing Cleopatra has betrayed him to Caesar and that this treachery has cost him everything. He turns his rage on Cleopatra, threatening her with terrible punishment. Cleopatra, terrified, flees to her monument.'),
('Ant', 4, 13, 'Cleopatra, hiding in her monument and terrified of Antony''s rage, takes Charmian''s advice to send word to Antony that she is dead. She will see how he takes the news. Mardian is sent to deliver the false message, with instructions to say she died with Antony''s name on her lips. Cleopatra hopes this will calm his fury.'),
('Ant', 4, 14, 'Antony, despairing, talks to Eros about how everything has dissolved and he can no longer trust his own identity. Mardian arrives and delivers the false news of Cleopatra''s death. Antony is devastated and tells Eros to kill him. Eros refuses and kills himself rather than harm his master. Shamed by Eros''s nobility, Antony falls on his own sword but does not die immediately — he is only wounded. His guards refuse to finish the job. Diomedes arrives from Cleopatra with word that she is alive, having sent the false news in fear. The wounded Antony asks to be carried to the monument.'),
('Ant', 4, 15, 'The dying Antony is hauled up into Cleopatra''s monument. Cleopatra and her women pull him up with ropes because she will not open the doors. Antony, dying in her arms, tells her to seek her safety from Caesar through Proculeius and to remember him by his former greatness, not his fall. He dies. Cleopatra is shattered, declaring the world meaningless without him, and falls into a swoon. She begins to plan to die rather than be displayed in Caesar''s triumph.'),
('Ant', 5, 1, 'Caesar receives news of Antony''s death and is genuinely moved, weeping, even though Antony was his rival. He sends Proculeius and Gallus to Cleopatra to persuade her to come out and assure her of good treatment, primarily because he wants her alive for his Roman triumph. He does not want her to kill herself. A Roman soldier is also on his way to deliver the news from Caesar''s perspective.'),
('Ant', 5, 2, 'Cleopatra, in the monument, resolves to die rather than be exhibited in Rome. Proculeius gains access and prevents her from stabbing herself. Caesar''s man Dolabella admits to Cleopatra that Caesar intends to parade her in Rome. She stages a dignified meeting with Caesar and pretends to submit, but gives Seleucus''s revelation of hidden treasure as cover for her true plans. After Caesar leaves, she has royal robes and her crown brought and applies an asp (a poisonous snake) to her breast, and Iras and Charmian die with her. Caesar arrives to find all three women dead and orders a state funeral for Antony and Cleopatra.'),
('Cor', 1, 1, 'In Rome, hungry citizens riot against the patricians, blaming Caius Martius (Coriolanus) for their suffering. Martius appears and insults the citizens with contempt, refusing to show them sympathy. News arrives that the Volscian enemy, led by Aufidius, has risen with an army. Martius is sent to serve under the Roman general Cominius. Menenius Agrippa tries to pacify the citizens with the fable of the belly and the limbs, arguing the senate acts like a stomach distributing nourishment.'),
('Cor', 1, 2, 'In the Volscian city of Corioli, the Volscian senate and their general Aufidius discuss their plans. They know Rome has heard of their preparations but will move forward anyway. Aufidius will meet Cominius in the field while the city holds. The senators urge speed and confidence.'),
('Cor', 1, 3, 'At home in Rome, Volumnia, Coriolanus''s mother, tells Virgilia, his wife, that she has always pushed her son toward military glory over safety and ease. She imagines him covered in blood and finds it beautiful. Virgilia is horrified and refuses to go out until he returns. Valeria arrives to invite them out; she reports news from the war that Martius is at siege of Corioli with Cominius.'),
('Cor', 1, 4, 'The Romans attack the gates of Corioli. Martius berates his retreating soldiers as cowards. When the Volscians open the gates, Martius charges inside alone and the gates close behind him. His soldiers mourn him as lost. Martius then fights his way back out of the city, bloodied but alive, and leads his men to take the gates. He insists on pursuing the retreating Volscians to rejoin Cominius''s army.'),
('Cor', 1, 5, 'With Corioli taken, Roman soldiers begin looting. Martius is contemptuous of them and drives them back to the fight. He and Titus Lartius talk briefly before Martius goes to reinforce Cominius, refusing to rest despite his wounds.'),
('Cor', 1, 6, 'Cominius''s forces are hard-pressed in battle with Aufidius''s Volscian army. Martius arrives covered in blood — so much that soldiers mistake the blood for paint. He reports that Corioli is taken and asks which part of the field is hardest; Cominius directs him to where Aufidius is fighting. Martius calls for volunteers to follow him. An inspiring group steps forward and they charge to confront Aufidius directly.'),
('Cor', 1, 7, 'Titus Lartius leaves a garrison in the captured Corioli and goes to rejoin the main battle. This very brief scene marks the securing of the city and the consolidation of Roman forces for the climax of the battle.'),
('Cor', 1, 8, 'Martius and Aufidius meet face to face on the battlefield and fight a fierce single combat. Volscian soldiers intervene to save Aufidius. Martius taunts Aufidius for being rescued by his own men. Aufidius is ashamed and furious, vowing that wherever they next meet he will defeat Martius "mine own way" without mercy. The scene establishes their personal enmity.'),
('Cor', 1, 9, 'After the victory, Cominius praises Martius publicly before the assembled army and offers him a share of the spoils. Martius deflects the praise with fierce modesty, refusing gifts. Cominius announces that henceforth Martius will bear the cognomen "Coriolanus" in honor of his conquest of Corioli. Martius accepts the name but still refuses personal wealth from the spoils and asks only one favor: the release of a Volscian man who once showed him hospitality, now a prisoner. He cannot remember the man''s name.'),
('Cor', 1, 10, 'Aufidius, bitterly defeated, meets with a Volscian senator. He swears to pursue vengeance against Coriolanus by any means — trickery, poison, or ambush — if he cannot beat him fairly in battle. He declares that even in dreams he fights and defeats Coriolanus. He will welcome any opportunity for revenge.'),
('Cor', 2, 1, 'Menenius clashes humorously with two tribunes, Sicinius and Brutus, about Coriolanus''s nature. Coriolanus returns to Rome as a hero; his mother Volumnia is overcome with pride. The tribunes plot together, fearing Coriolanus''s popularity will lead to his becoming consul, which they dread because of his hatred for the common people.'),
('Cor', 2, 2, 'The Roman senate formally recommends Coriolanus for consul. A senator praises his military record at length. Coriolanus himself is present but hates being praised and leaves before the full account. The tribunes note he refuses to speak humbly to the people and predict he will offend them when he must ask for their voices in the marketplace. The senate votes to support him for consul, pending the people''s approval.'),
('Cor', 2, 3, 'According to Roman custom, Coriolanus must stand in the marketplace in a gown of humility and show his wounds to citizens to ask for their votes. He goes through with this but does so contemptuously, mocking the citizens under his breath. The citizens are confused by his manner but largely agree to give him their voices. After he leaves, the tribunes Sicinius and Brutus manipulate the citizens into reconsidering, telling them Coriolanus showed contempt for the custom and them. The citizens, persuaded, agree to revoke their approval.'),
('Cor', 3, 1, 'Coriolanus and the senators are heading to the marketplace when the tribunes bring news that the people have changed their votes. Coriolanus erupts with contempt for the tribunes and the popular power they represent, calling it a cancer in the state. His language is so extreme that the tribunes declare him a traitor and call for him to be seized. A brawl breaks out; patricians and tribunes struggle. Menenius persuades everyone to calm down and asks Coriolanus to appear before the people for a formal trial, which Coriolanus furiously resists.'),
('Cor', 3, 2, 'Volumnia and the patricians urge Coriolanus to return to the marketplace and humble himself before the people and tribunes — to dissemble, to be gentle, to ask for pardon. Coriolanus is appalled that his mother, who taught him to value honor above all, now asks him to play a false role. She argues that the temporary dishonesty is a political necessity, not a real surrender. He reluctantly agrees to go and try to speak gently.'),
('Cor', 3, 3, 'The tribunes prepare in advance, intending to provoke Coriolanus to anger. In the Forum, Coriolanus attempts to remain calm but the tribunes deliberately use the word "traitor," knowing it will inflame him. He erupts and denounces the entire system of tribunician power. The tribunes declare him banished from Rome forever. In a remarkable reversal, Coriolanus declares that he banishes Rome, saying "I banish you!" He walks out defiantly.'),
('Cor', 4, 1, 'Coriolanus says farewell to his mother, wife, Menenius, and Cominius outside the city gate. He comforts them, telling them not to weep, and speaks proudly of facing his exile alone. He tells them he will write often and go to find his fortune where he will be heard. Volumnia tries to be strong but breaks down. He departs alone.'),
('Cor', 4, 2, 'After Coriolanus leaves, Volumnia confronts the tribunes Sicinius and Brutus with fierce anger, cursing them and blaming them for her son''s exile. Virgilia is too grief-stricken to speak. Menenius tries to calm Volumnia, and the tribunes escape her rage.'),
('Cor', 4, 3, 'A Roman, Nicanor, encounters a Volscian, Adrian, on a road. Adrian has been looking for Nicanor, who is a spy for the Volscians. Nicanor reports the news of Coriolanus''s banishment, which he knows will delight the Volscians, who can now move against Rome more safely. The two travel together to Antium to bring this news to Aufidius.'),
('Cor', 4, 4, 'Coriolanus arrives alone at Antium, the Volscian capital, in disguise. He reflects bitterly on the strangeness of coming to his enemy''s city as a supplicant. He finds Aufidius''s house and goes to the door. This brief scene of his arrival sets up the remarkable confrontation to follow.'),
('Cor', 4, 5, 'Servants try to throw Coriolanus out of Aufidius''s house during a banquet. He reveals himself to Aufidius and offers either his services against Rome or his life — he is willing to die if Aufidius is not interested. He proposes they join forces and destroy Rome. Aufidius is moved and embraces him enthusiastically, welcoming him as a partner. The servants are amazed at the reversal and discuss Coriolanus with admiration. Aufidius''s officers begin to give Coriolanus power over their troops.'),
('Cor', 4, 6, 'Back in Rome, the tribunes are congratulating themselves on a peaceful city with Coriolanus gone. News arrives that the Volscians are advancing with Coriolanus in command of their forces, burning and destroying everything in their path. The tribunes are blamed by the citizens who gave Coriolanus''s banishment voice. Cominius confirms the threat is real; Menenius mourns the city''s fate and blames the tribunes.'),
('Cor', 4, 7, 'Aufidius speaks privately with a Volscian lieutenant about Coriolanus''s growing power. The soldiers adore Coriolanus and he has taken command as though he were born to it. Aufidius is alarmed and jealous. He reflects that Coriolanus''s virtues — his pride, his inability to moderate himself — are also his fatal flaws. He plans to let Coriolanus carry out the attack on Rome, then turn on him when the time is right.'),
('Cor', 5, 1, 'In Rome, Cominius reports that he went to Coriolanus to plead for mercy and was refused — Coriolanus would not even look at him at first, and then refused to hear anything about Rome. He told Cominius to tell the tribunes that they are the ones who must beg. Menenius is persuaded to go himself, believing his old bond with Coriolanus may work. The tribunes ask Volumnia and Virgilia to go but they refuse for now.'),
('Cor', 5, 2, 'Menenius arrives at the Volscian camp and is refused entry by guards. He invokes his closeness to Coriolanus. Coriolanus emerges and coldly dismisses Menenius — he says he has no personal identity left, only the Volscian cause — and tells the guards to turn him away. Menenius departs, shattered, telling the guards that Rome is doomed.'),
('Cor', 5, 3, 'Volumnia, Virgilia, young Martius, and Valeria arrive at Coriolanus''s camp. Coriolanus steels himself not to be moved but is shattered by the sight of them kneeling to him. Volumnia makes a long, powerful speech arguing that he is destroying both Rome and the Volscians, his own honor, and his family. She says he must either kill them or spare Rome; she falls silent and prepares to leave. Coriolanus breaks down, takes her hand, and agrees to make peace. He knows this will likely cost him his life among the Volscians. He arranges peace terms and turns to negotiate.'),
('Cor', 5, 4, 'Menenius and Sicinius wait in Rome, Menenius convinced all is lost. A messenger brings news that Volumnia''s embassy has succeeded — Coriolanus has agreed to withdraw. Rome rejoices; there is the sound of trumpets and celebration. Sicinius asks the messenger what Coriolanus said when he agreed to turn back.'),
('Cor', 5, 5, 'The Roman senators, people, and attendants go out to meet Volumnia and the women returning from the Volscian camp. They bring them in with great honor and celebration. The scene is very brief — a ceremonial welcoming of Rome''s saviors.'),
('Cor', 5, 6, 'Coriolanus returns to Corioli and reports the peace terms to the Volscian lords. Aufidius immediately accuses him of treachery, calling him "boy of tears" for being moved by a woman''s tears to betray the Volscian cause. Coriolanus is enraged by the insult and reminds them of his victories at Corioli. Aufidius''s conspirators stab Coriolanus to death. Aufidius stands on the body. The Volscian lords immediately feel shame; they order a noble funeral for Coriolanus, calling him a noble memory.'),
('Tim', 1, 1, 'In Athens, Timon of Athens is celebrated as a magnificent patron of the arts and a man of boundless generosity. He gives away a jewel, pays a debt for his friend Ventidius, and arranges his servant Lucilius''s marriage by supplying the dowry. A cynical philosopher, Apemantus, observes and condemns the flattery surrounding Timon. The scene introduces Timon''s reckless open-handedness and the crowd of flatterers feeding off it.'),
('Tim', 1, 2, 'Timon hosts a lavish banquet for his friends and the grateful Ventidius, who has repaid him and now offers money back; Timon refuses. Apemantus attends and rails against the flattery and feasting, saying Timon is surrounded by people who will abandon him the moment his money runs out. Timon gives away further expensive gifts, dismissing Apemantus''s warnings. Flavius, Timon''s steward, tries to get Timon''s attention to tell him the money is running out; Timon does not listen.'),
('Tim', 2, 1, 'A Senator, to whom Timon owes money, sends his servant Caphis to collect the debt. He reflects that Timon''s resources are exhausted and that many creditors are now demanding payment. Caphis leaves with instructions to press the matter firmly.'),
('Tim', 2, 2, 'Flavius tries again to tell Timon how dire his finances are, but creditors'' servants keep interrupting them. Timon finally listens and is shocked to learn he is completely broke and deeply in debt. Flavius tells him he has been warning him for months. Timon is untroubled — he will simply ask his rich friends for help as he has helped them. He sends servants to Ventidius, Lucius, and others to borrow money.'),
('Tim', 3, 1, 'Timon''s servant Flaminius asks Lucullus to lend Timon money in his moment of need. Lucullus was one of Timon''s most enthusiastic flatterers. He now refuses, pretending he always warned Timon against extravagance. He tries to bribe Flaminius to say he was not found. Flaminius throws the coins back at him in disgust and curses him.'),
('Tim', 3, 2, 'Lucius hears from strangers what happened to Flaminius when he asked Lucullus for money and ostentatiously declares he would never be so base. When Timon''s servant Servilius arrives to ask Lucius for a loan, Lucius claims he has just committed all his money elsewhere and cannot help. The strangers are appalled by this hypocrisy after all Timon did for Lucius.'),
('Tim', 3, 3, 'Timon''s servant goes to Sempronius, another friend, who refuses to lend money and is offended that Timon approached others before him. The servant cannot believe Sempronius has used this excuse to refuse. He curses him. This scene completes the pattern of betrayal by Timon''s three closest supposed friends.'),
('Tim', 3, 4, 'Multiple servants of Timon''s creditors descend on his house demanding repayment. Flavius tells them Timon is sick and cannot see anyone, but the servants are aggressive. Timon briefly appears, furious, and dismisses them. He tells Flavius to invite all his false friends to one more banquet.'),
('Tim', 3, 5, 'In the Athenian Senate, Alcibiades pleads for the life of a soldier friend who killed a man in a quarrel over honor. The senators refuse and sentence the soldier to death. Alcibiades argues with increasing passion. The senators, angered, banish Alcibiades from Athens. He vows revenge and will use his army against the city.'),
('Tim', 3, 6, 'Timon''s supposed friends gather for what they expect to be another lavish banquet. Timon serves them covered dishes, which they assume contain food. When uncovered, the dishes reveal only warm water and stones, which Timon throws at them, cursing them as hypocrites and flatterers. He drives them out. This is Timon''s final break with Athens and humanity.'),
('Tim', 4, 1, 'Outside the walls of Athens, Timon curses the city, calling down disease, ingratitude, and destruction upon it. He prays for sons to rebel against fathers, slaves against masters, and every form of social corruption. He resolves to go live in the wilderness outside the city and dig for roots to eat, rejecting all human society.'),
('Tim', 4, 2, 'Flavius and the other servants of Timon''s household say farewell to each other in Timon''s empty house. Flavius shares what little money he has left with them. The other servants leave; Flavius resolves to find Timon in the woods and serve him still, out of genuine love.'),
('Tim', 4, 3, 'In the woods, Timon digs for roots and finds gold buried in the earth. He curses gold as the root of human corruption. Alcibiades arrives with his soldiers and two courtesans, Phrynia and Timandra, marching against Athens. Timon gives him gold, urging him to destroy Athens without mercy. He gives more gold to the courtesans, urging them to spread disease. Apemantus arrives and argues with Timon — he accuses Timon of performing misanthropy, of having adopted misery as a pose, while true philosophy requires never having flattered to begin with. Thieves come and Timon gives them gold, mocking them as no worse than other men. Flavius finds Timon and Timon is moved by his loyalty — Flavius is the one human being he cannot condemn entirely. He gives Flavius gold and tells him to go away and hate mankind.'),
('Tim', 5, 1, 'Two senators and a poet and painter, having heard Timon has gold, come to visit him in the woods seeking his favor. Timon drives the poet and painter away with blows. The senators come to offer Timon reconciliation: Athens will reverse his wrongs, give him great authority, and pay all his debts if he returns to lead the defense against Alcibiades. Timon responds that he has nothing to offer them — only a tree on which they may hang themselves — and sends them away.'),
('Tim', 5, 2, 'Two other senators have been to see Timon and return empty-handed. They compare notes with the first two senators. All agree that Athens must negotiate with Alcibiades, as Timon will not help. They head back to the city.'),
('Tim', 5, 3, 'A soldier finds Timon''s cave in the woods. Timon is not there but the soldier finds a tomb with an epitaph. The soldier cannot read it but makes a wax impression of the inscription to bring to Alcibiades.'),
('Tim', 5, 4, 'Alcibiades and his army stand before the walls of Athens. Senators of Athens appear on the walls and plead for the city, arguing that the guilt belongs to a few specific people, not all of Athens. Alcibiades agrees to enter the city peacefully and punish only his and Timon''s actual enemies. A soldier brings the wax impression of Timon''s epitaph; Alcibiades reads it aloud — Timon''s final curse on humanity. Alcibiades mourns Timon with some respect.'),
('Tit', 1, 1, 'Saturninus and Bassianus dispute the Roman throne. Titus Andronicus returns to Rome in triumph from the Goth wars, bringing with him the Goth queen Tamora, her sons Alarbus, Chiron, and Demetrius, and the Moor Aaron as prisoners. Titus sacrifices Alarbus over his sons'' bodies as a ritual offering, despite Tamora''s pleas. Saturninus is made emperor and claims Lavinia, Titus''s daughter, for his bride. Bassianus protests and takes Lavinia himself, since they are already pledged. Titus kills his own son Mutius for blocking his pursuit of Lavinia. Saturninus, angered, instead makes Tamora his empress. Tamora secretly promises Saturninus she will destroy Titus''s family for him.'),
('Tit', 2, 1, 'Aaron the Moor reveals in a soliloquy that he plans to advance himself through Tamora''s love now that she is empress. Chiron and Demetrius quarrel over their desire for Lavinia; Aaron proposes that they both satisfy their lust with her during a royal hunt in the forest, framing her husband Bassianus for murder to cover the crime.'),
('Tit', 2, 2, 'Titus and his sons and Saturninus prepare for a royal hunt. Aaron plants a letter that will implicate Titus''s sons Quintus and Martius in a staged murder. The scene is brief and sets up the conspiracies that will play out in the forest.'),
('Tit', 2, 3, 'In the forest, Aaron leads Tamora''s sons to a pit where Bassianus''s body has been placed. Tamora pretends to plead for Lavinia while her sons drag her off to rape her. Quintus and Martius fall into the pit with Bassianus''s body. Aaron brings Saturninus to find them apparently guilty. The forged letter is produced. Saturninus has Quintus and Martius arrested for Bassianus''s murder.'),
('Tit', 2, 4, 'Chiron and Demetrius have raped Lavinia and cut off her hands and cut out her tongue so she cannot identify them. Lavinia is found wandering in the forest by her uncle Marcus, who is horrified and mourns for her in an extended poetic lament. He leads her away to tell Titus what has happened.'),
('Tit', 3, 1, 'Titus pleads in the street for mercy for his condemned sons Quintus and Martius, but the senators pass by without listening. Marcus brings Lavinia to Titus. Aaron arrives and tells Titus that his sons'' lives may be spared if a hand is sent to the emperor — Titus, Marcus, or Lucius must cut off a hand. Titus cuts off his own hand and sends it. A messenger returns with the hand and his sons'' severed heads. Titus weeps, then laughs — his grief has passed into something beyond grief. He sends Lucius to raise an army of Goths against Rome and vows revenge.'),
('Tit', 3, 2, 'A fly scene: Titus, Marcus, Lavinia, and Titus''s son Young Lucius have a meal. Titus grieves continuously. Marcus kills a fly, and Titus angrily rebukes him for killing an innocent creature. When Marcus says the fly resembled Aaron the Moor, Titus savagely strikes at it in a fit of violence-by-proxy. Lavinia communicates by guiding her nephew to a copy of Ovid''s Metamorphoses, apparently seeking a story there.'),
('Tit', 4, 1, 'Lavinia, using the stumps of her arms and her nephew''s hand, writes the names "Chiron" and "Demetrius" in the sand along with the word "Stuprum" (rape) and the name Ovid''s Philomela. The family now knows who raped and mutilated her. Titus begins to plan his revenge systematically and sends Young Lucius with gifts for Chiron and Demetrius.'),
('Tit', 4, 2, 'Young Lucius delivers wrapped weapons as gifts to Chiron and Demetrius with a message in Latin that they are able to read, recognizing the quotation as a threat. Aaron intercepts the moment and understands the meaning. A nurse arrives with Aaron''s newborn black baby, fathered on Tamora, and tells Aaron that Tamora wants the baby killed to hide the evidence. Aaron refuses to kill his son and kills the nurse to keep the secret. He plots to replace the child with a pale baby and hide his son with Muliteus, a Goth.'),
('Tit', 4, 3, 'Titus, appearing to have gone mad, organizes his friends and family to shoot arrows with messages tied to them directed at the gods, asking for justice. A clown is persuaded to carry a letter and a pair of pigeons to Saturninus. The scene mixes apparent madness with deliberate action as Titus continues his plan for revenge.'),
('Tit', 4, 4, 'Saturninus is furious at the arrows shooting messages into his court. The Clown delivers Titus''s letter and is hanged for it. News arrives that Lucius is advancing on Rome with a Goth army. Tamora assures Saturninus she can manipulate Titus; she will visit him, pretending to be Revenge, to draw out his intentions and neutralize the threat.'),
('Tit', 5, 1, 'Lucius, commanding the Goth army outside Rome, encounters Aaron and Aaron''s baby son, captured by a Goth soldier. Aaron, to save his son''s life, confesses everything he has plotted: the rape and mutilation of Lavinia, the framing of Titus''s sons, the cutting off of Titus''s hand. Lucius promises Aaron''s son will be spared. A Roman comes to bring Lucius to a parley with Saturninus, requested through Titus.'),
('Tit', 5, 2, 'Tamora arrives at Titus''s house disguised as the figure of Revenge, bringing Chiron and Demetrius disguised as Rape and Murder. She tries to manipulate the apparently mad Titus into convincing Lucius to meet with Saturninus. Titus plays along with the pretense while secretly seeing through it. He sends Tamora away but keeps Chiron and Demetrius. Once Tamora leaves he has them bound, tells them he knows who they are and what they did to Lavinia, and kills them. He and Lavinia drain their blood into a basin.'),
('Tit', 5, 3, 'Titus hosts a banquet at which Saturninus and Tamora are guests. He serves Tamora a pie made from her sons'' flesh and bones. Titus kills Lavinia himself to end her dishonor. He tells Tamora and Saturninus what is in the pie. He kills Tamora; Saturninus kills Titus; Lucius kills Saturninus. Marcus and Lucius explain to the Roman people all that has happened. Aaron is brought out and sentenced to be buried chest-deep and starved. Lucius is made emperor and Titus is given a state funeral.'),
('Rom', 0, 0, 'The Chorus speaks a prologue before the play begins. Two households of equal dignity in Verona have an ancient feud. A pair of star-crossed lovers will be born from these two families, and their deaths will end the feud. The Chorus briefly outlines the plot: the lovers'' tragic end, and the two hours of stage time the play will take to show their story.'),
('Rom', 1, 1, 'Servants of the Capulet and Montague households start a street brawl in Verona. Benvolio tries to stop it; Tybalt inflames it. Prince Escalus breaks up the fight and warns that the next person to disturb the peace will be executed. Montague and Lady Montague ask Benvolio about their son Romeo, who has been seen moping alone. Benvolio finds Romeo, who confesses he is in love with Rosaline, who has sworn to remain chaste and will not return his love.'),
('Rom', 1, 2, 'Count Paris asks Lord Capulet for Juliet''s hand in marriage. Capulet says Juliet is still young (not yet fourteen) and asks Paris to wait two years and to try to win her heart at a feast he is planning tonight. A servant is sent with a list of invitees but cannot read; Romeo and Benvolio help him and discover Rosaline will be at the party. Benvolio convinces Romeo to come to the Capulet feast to see Rosaline alongside other beautiful women.'),
('Rom', 1, 3, 'Lady Capulet and the Nurse tell Juliet about Paris''s proposal. The Nurse recalls Juliet''s childhood lovingly. Lady Capulet praises Paris and asks Juliet to consider him favorably at tonight''s feast. Juliet agrees to look and see whether she might love him, but with little enthusiasm. She is obedient to her parents'' wishes.'),
('Rom', 1, 4, 'Romeo, Benvolio, and Mercutio prepare to go to the Capulet feast in masks. Romeo is melancholy about love. Mercutio delivers the famous Queen Mab speech, a fantastical description of the fairy queen who visits dreamers. Romeo has had a premonition of early death stemming from this night''s events but decides to go despite it, putting himself in fate''s hands.'),
('Rom', 1, 5, 'At the Capulet feast, Romeo sees Juliet and is instantly overwhelmed, forgetting Rosaline entirely. He approaches her and they speak in the form of a sonnet, sharing a first kiss. Tybalt recognizes Romeo as a Montague and is furious at his presence; Capulet restrains Tybalt, not wanting trouble at his party. Romeo and Juliet discover each other''s identity through the Nurse and are horrified — they have fallen in love with their enemy.'),
('Rom', 2, 0, 'The Chorus speaks a second prologue, summarizing the new situation: Romeo''s infatuation with Rosaline is dead, replaced by love for Juliet. Both Romeo and Juliet are in love but face the obstacle of their families'' hatred. Despite the danger, love will find a way to bring them together.'),
('Rom', 2, 1, 'After the feast Romeo slips away from Benvolio and Mercutio rather than go home. Benvolio calls for him; Mercutio mocks Romeo in a bawdy speech invoking Rosaline''s name. Romeo, hiding in the darkness, cannot come out to them. Eventually Benvolio and Mercutio give up and go home. Romeo moves toward Juliet''s orchard wall.'),
('Rom', 2, 2, 'Romeo climbs into the Capulet orchard and sees Juliet appear at her window. He speaks of her beauty in famous soliloquy — "But soft, what light through yonder window breaks?" Juliet, not knowing he is there, speaks aloud of her love for him and laments that he is a Montague. Romeo reveals himself; they confess their love. Juliet proposes marriage; Romeo agrees. She must go in but keeps coming back, reluctant to part. They plan to exchange marriage vows the next day through the Nurse as go-between.'),
('Rom', 2, 3, 'Romeo goes to Friar Lawrence''s cell before dawn. Friar Lawrence is gathering herbs and reflecting on the dual nature of plants — some healing, some poisonous, like human beings. Romeo tells him he loves Juliet and wants to be married that day. The Friar is surprised that Rosaline is forgotten so quickly but agrees to marry them, hoping the union might reconcile the feuding families.'),
('Rom', 2, 4, 'Benvolio and Mercutio have been looking for Romeo and know that Tybalt has sent a challenge to the Montague house. Romeo arrives and banters with Mercutio in wordplay and wit, fully recovered from his melancholy. The Nurse arrives to speak with Romeo privately; Mercutio mocks her mercilessly. Romeo privately tells the Nurse that Juliet should come to Friar Lawrence''s cell that afternoon to be married, and that Romeo''s servant will bring a rope ladder to climb to Juliet''s room that night.'),
('Rom', 2, 5, 'Juliet has been waiting impatiently for hours for the Nurse to return with news. The Nurse arrives but is exhausted and teases Juliet by delaying, complaining of her aching bones. Finally she reveals that Romeo waits at Friar Lawrence''s cell and Juliet is to go there to be married. Juliet rushes off joyfully.'),
('Rom', 2, 6, 'Romeo and Friar Lawrence wait in the cell for Juliet. The Friar cautions moderation in love — "These violent delights have violent ends." Juliet arrives and she and Romeo greet each other with happiness. Friar Lawrence hurries them to the ceremony to marry before anyone discovers them.'),
('Rom', 3, 1, 'Mercutio and Benvolio encounter Tybalt on the street. Tybalt is looking for Romeo to challenge him. Romeo arrives, fresh from his secret marriage, and tries to deflect Tybalt''s insults with love — they are now kinsmen by marriage. Mercutio is disgusted by Romeo''s apparent submission and draws on Tybalt himself. Romeo tries to stop the fight; Tybalt stabs Mercutio under Romeo''s arm and flees. Mercutio dies, cursing both houses. Tybalt returns; Romeo, maddened by grief, kills Tybalt. Benvolio urges Romeo to flee; the Prince arrives and, hearing the accounts, banishes Romeo from Verona. Lady Capulet demands death for Romeo; Montague argues Romeo was within his right. Prince Escalus banishes Romeo and warns that further offenses will be met with death.'),
('Rom', 3, 2, 'Juliet, alone, impatiently waits for night and Romeo. The Nurse arrives weeping and incoherently describes deaths. Juliet initially thinks Romeo is dead, then learns it was Tybalt who was killed and Romeo who killed him. She is torn between grief for Tybalt and love of Romeo. When she hears Romeo is banished she is devastated — banishment is a worse death than Tybalt''s. The Nurse promises to bring Romeo to her that night.'),
('Rom', 3, 3, 'Romeo is hiding in Friar Lawrence''s cell and is desperate — banishment feels worse than death to him because it means separation from Juliet. The Friar scolds him for ingratitude: he is alive, and the Prince has been merciful. The Nurse arrives and tells Romeo of Juliet''s grief. Friar Lawrence gives Romeo a plan: spend the night with Juliet, then go to Mantua before dawn. The Friar will find a way to announce the marriage, reconcile the families, and get the Prince to pardon Romeo.'),
('Rom', 3, 4, 'Lord Capulet tells Paris that given the grief over Tybalt''s death there has been no time to talk to Juliet about marriage. He then impulsively decides to set the wedding for Thursday, three days away. He is confident Juliet will obey. Lady Capulet is to tell Juliet immediately. This reckless decision sets up the tragedy''s final crisis.'),
('Rom', 3, 5, 'Romeo and Juliet are together in her room at dawn and argue lovingly over whether it is yet day — he must leave before light. He finally departs. Lady Capulet arrives and tells Juliet of her impending marriage to Paris. Juliet refuses, saying she would rather marry Romeo, whom she hates, than Paris. Lord Capulet arrives and is furious at Juliet''s refusal, threatening to disown her and throw her into the street. The Nurse suggests Juliet should marry Paris since Romeo is banished. Juliet decides to go to Friar Lawrence for counsel, or if he cannot help, she will kill herself.'),
('Rom', 4, 1, 'Juliet goes to Friar Lawrence and finds Paris there, who speaks to her proprietarily. After Paris leaves, Juliet threatens to kill herself rather than marry Paris. The Friar proposes a plan: Juliet should agree to the marriage but on the night before the wedding drink a potion that will make her appear dead for forty-two hours. She will be placed in the Capulet tomb; the Friar will send a letter to Romeo in Mantua to come and be there when she wakes, and they can flee together. Juliet takes the vial.'),
('Rom', 4, 2, 'Juliet returns home and tells her father she has repented of her disobedience and will marry Paris. Capulet is overjoyed and moves the wedding forward a day, to Wednesday. Lady Capulet worries they are not prepared in time; Capulet dismisses her concerns and bustles about making arrangements. Juliet goes with the Nurse to be dressed in her bridal clothes.'),
('Rom', 4, 3, 'Alone in her room at night, Juliet sends the Nurse away and prepares to drink the Friar''s potion. She is afraid: what if the potion does not work? What if it is poison? What if she wakes too early in the tomb and goes mad from the horror and the stench and the ghosts? She imagines waking and taking Tybalt''s bones as a weapon. She drinks, crying out for Romeo, and falls.'),
('Rom', 4, 4, 'The Capulet household is busy through the night and early morning preparing for the wedding. Lord Capulet is in high spirits, directing everything, sending people to wake Juliet and fetch Paris. Lady Capulet and the Nurse bustle about. This brief cheerful domestic scene is immediately followed by the discovery of Juliet''s apparent death.'),
('Rom', 4, 5, 'The Nurse goes to wake Juliet and finds her apparently dead. She screams; the Capulets and Paris rush in and grieve wildly. Friar Lawrence arrives with Paris and tries to console them — Juliet has gone to a better place, and their grief is selfish. He supervises the funeral preparations: the wedding will become a funeral. The musicians who came for the wedding are dismissed; they have a brief comic exchange with Peter about music and grief.'),
('Rom', 5, 1, 'Romeo is in Mantua and wakes from a dream in which Juliet found him dead and her kisses brought him back to life. He interprets this as a good omen. Balthasar arrives from Verona with news that Juliet is dead and buried in the Capulet tomb. Romeo is shattered; he buys a fast-acting poison from a poor apothecary, determined to go to Juliet''s tomb and die beside her.'),
('Rom', 5, 2, 'Friar John, who was supposed to deliver Friar Lawrence''s letter to Romeo in Mantua, returns having never left Verona — he was quarantined in a house suspected of plague and could not travel. Friar Lawrence realizes Romeo has not been told the truth about Juliet''s sleep. He rushes to the Capulet tomb to be there when she wakes.'),
('Rom', 5, 3, 'Paris comes to Juliet''s tomb to mourn and scatter flowers; Romeo arrives and Paris tries to stop him. They fight; Romeo kills Paris and buries him in the tomb. Romeo finds Juliet, drinks the poison, and dies. Friar Lawrence arrives, finds Romeo and Paris dead, and Juliet begins to wake. He tries to persuade her to flee; she refuses when she sees Romeo dead. The Friar hears people coming and flees. Juliet kisses Romeo''s lips hoping for poison, then stabs herself with his dagger and dies. The Prince, the Capulets, and Montague arrive. Friar Lawrence confesses everything. Balthasar produces Romeo''s letter. The Prince declares that all are punished. Montague and Capulet reconcile over their children''s bodies and pledge to raise golden statues to the dead lovers.')
;
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO scene_synopses (work_abbrev, div1, div2, synopsis) VALUES
('MND', 1, 1, 'Theseus, Duke of Athens, prepares to wed Hippolyta, queen of the Amazons, in four days. Egeus enters and demands his daughter Hermia marry Demetrius, but Hermia loves Lysander. Theseus warns Hermia she must obey her father or face death or a life as a nun. Hermia and Lysander plan to elope through the forest the next night and confide in Helena. Helena, who loves Demetrius, decides to tell him of the plan in hopes of winning his favour.'),
('MND', 1, 2, 'A group of Athenian craftsmen—Bottom the weaver, Quince the carpenter, Flute the bellows-mender, Snout, Snug, and Starveling—meet to plan a play they will perform at Theseus''s wedding. Quince assigns roles for the play of Pyramus and Thisbe. Bottom eagerly wants to play every part and must be talked into taking just the role of Pyramus. They agree to rehearse in secret in the forest the next night.'),
('MND', 2, 1, 'In the fairy realm, Puck tells a fairy that Oberon, king of the fairies, quarrels with his queen Titania over a changeling Indian boy. Oberon and Titania meet and argue; she refuses to give him the boy and leaves. Oberon sends Puck to fetch a magical flower called love-in-idleness whose juice, when dropped on sleeping eyes, causes the sleeper to fall in love with the first creature seen on waking. Oberon plans to use it on Titania to distract her. He also witnesses Demetrius cruelly rebuffing Helena in the forest and tells Puck to anoint Demetrius''s eyes so he will love Helena.'),
('MND', 2, 2, 'Titania falls asleep while her fairies sing to her. Oberon squeezes the love-juice on her eyelids. Lysander and Hermia, lost and tired, stop to sleep in the same part of the forest. Puck, mistaking Lysander for Demetrius, puts the juice on Lysander''s eyes. Helena enters, pursued by Demetrius; she wakes Lysander, who immediately falls in love with her. Helena thinks he is mocking her and flees; Lysander abandons the sleeping Hermia to follow. Hermia wakes from a nightmare to find herself alone.'),
('MND', 3, 1, 'The craftsmen rehearse their play in the forest near where Titania sleeps. Puck secretly gives Bottom the head of a donkey. When Bottom reappears, the other craftsmen flee in terror. Bottom, unaware of his transformation, begins singing to keep his spirits up. Titania wakes, sees Bottom, and instantly falls in love with him. She commands her fairies to wait on him and brings him to her bower.'),
('MND', 3, 2, 'Puck reports to Oberon that Titania loves a monster and that he has anointed the Athenian''s eyes, but Oberon realizes Puck has enchanted the wrong man when Hermia appears with Demetrius. Oberon sends Puck to fetch Helena while he anoints Demetrius''s eyes. Demetrius wakes and declares love for Helena, but she thinks both he and Lysander are mocking her. Hermia accuses Helena of stealing Lysander''s love. The four lovers quarrel bitterly, and the men nearly come to blows. Puck misleads the men through the dark forest until they collapse from exhaustion, then applies the remedy to Lysander''s eyes so he will wake loving Hermia again.'),
('MND', 4, 1, 'Titania dotes on Bottom in the forest while her fairies tend to him. Oberon, taking pity on Titania and having secured the changeling boy, removes the spell from her eyes. She wakes, sees Bottom with his donkey head, and is horrified. Oberon removes Bottom''s ass-head while he sleeps. Theseus, Hippolyta, and Egeus arrive hunting and discover the four young lovers asleep together. The lovers wake, their quarrels forgotten; Lysander loves Hermia again and Demetrius now loves Helena. Theseus overrules Egeus and decrees the lovers will be married alongside him. Bottom wakes alone and marvels at his strange dream.'),
('MND', 4, 2, 'The craftsmen are distraught because Bottom has disappeared and they cannot perform without him. Bottom suddenly returns, bursting with excitement about his adventure but refusing to tell them details now. He announces they must put on their play at the palace immediately.'),
('MND', 5, 1, 'Theseus and Hippolyta discuss the lovers'' strange tale. The court chooses the craftsmen''s play for the evening entertainment. Bottom, Quince, and the others perform the tragic tale of Pyramus and Thisbe with hilarious incompetence, and the court watches with amused commentary. After the play the fairies bless the palace and the three couples as Oberon, Titania, and their train dance through the sleeping house. Puck closes the play by asking the audience''s forgiveness if they were offended and promising the actors will make amends.'),

('MV', 1, 1, 'Antonio, a prosperous Venetian merchant, is inexplicably sad despite his ships at sea carrying great wealth. His friends Salerio and Solanio suggest he is worried about his cargo, but he dismisses this. His friend Bassanio arrives and confesses he is in love with Portia, a wealthy heiress of Belmont, and needs money to court her properly. Antonio''s funds are all tied up in his ships, but he offers to help Bassanio borrow money on his credit.'),
('MV', 1, 2, 'At Belmont, Portia complains to her maid Nerissa about the terms of her father''s will: she must marry whichever suitor correctly chooses among three caskets of gold, silver, and lead. Nerissa lists the current suitors and Portia describes each one''s faults. Both women agree the only man worthy of Portia is Bassanio. A servant announces the arrival of the Prince of Morocco.'),
('MV', 1, 3, 'Bassanio approaches Shylock, a Jewish moneylender, for a loan of three thousand ducats, with Antonio as guarantor. Shylock agrees in principle but insists on speaking with Antonio. Shylock reveals in an aside his hatred of Antonio for lending money without charging interest. When Antonio arrives, Shylock proposes a bond: if the money is not repaid within three months, Antonio must forfeit a pound of his own flesh. Antonio confidently agrees, certain his ships will return in time. Bassanio is uneasy but Antonio dismisses his concern.'),
('MV', 2, 1, 'The Prince of Morocco arrives at Belmont and makes his case to Portia. He asks her not to judge him by his dark complexion, saying his blood is as warm and red as any fair-skinned man''s. Portia reminds him the choice is her father''s, not hers, and she is bound to accept whoever chooses correctly. Morocco vows to attempt the casket choice after dinner.'),
('MV', 2, 2, 'Launcelot Gobbo, Shylock''s clownish servant, debates with himself whether to leave Shylock''s service. He meets his nearly blind father, Old Gobbo, who does not recognize him, and plays a trick on him before revealing himself. Launcelot asks to become Bassanio''s servant. Bassanio agrees and also invites Gratiano to come with him to Belmont, on condition that Gratiano behave with more decorum there.'),
('MV', 2, 3, 'Shylock''s daughter Jessica says farewell to Launcelot, who is leaving her father''s service. She is ashamed of her home and her father, and gives Launcelot a letter to secretly deliver to Lorenzo, a friend of Bassanio''s. She plans to elope with Lorenzo, converting to Christianity and taking her father''s gold with her.'),
('MV', 2, 4, 'Lorenzo, Gratiano, Salerio, and Solanio plan a masque for that evening. Launcelot delivers Jessica''s letter to Lorenzo. Lorenzo reads it and is overjoyed—Jessica will disguise herself as a boy and slip out of Shylock''s house with money and jewels to elope with him.'),
('MV', 2, 5, 'Shylock reluctantly agrees to dine with Bassanio and the Christians, despite a premonition of trouble. He instructs Jessica to lock up the house and not watch the masque revelers from the windows. He leaves, and Launcelot secretly tells Jessica that Lorenzo will come for her that night.'),
('MV', 2, 6, 'Lorenzo arrives below Shylock''s house during the masque. Jessica, disguised as a boy, tosses down a casket of gold and jewels and climbs down to join him. They leave together to join Bassanio''s party. Gratiano and Salerio note that Antonio''s ship has sailed.'),
('MV', 2, 7, 'The Prince of Morocco studies the three caskets and reads their inscriptions. He reasons that gold best suits Portia''s worth and chooses the gold casket, which contains a skull and a scroll mocking him for choosing by outward show. He departs in shame and Portia is relieved he is gone.'),
('MV', 2, 8, 'Salerio and Solanio report on recent events in Venice: Shylock has discovered Jessica''s elopement and the theft of his money and jewels, and is in public anguish crying for his daughter and his ducats. They also convey worrying news that one of Antonio''s ships may have been wrecked in the English Channel, and that Shylock will enforce his bond if Antonio cannot repay.'),
('MV', 2, 9, 'The Prince of Arragon arrives at Belmont and chooses the silver casket, which promises what he deserves. Inside he finds a portrait of a blinking idiot and a poem mocking his presumption. He leaves, and a messenger brings news that a young Venetian—Bassanio—has arrived bringing magnificent gifts. Nerissa hopes this is the man they have been waiting for.'),
('MV', 3, 1, 'Solanio and Salerio discuss rumours of Antonio''s wrecked ship. Shylock confronts them about Jessica''s flight and insists he will have his pound of flesh from Antonio if the bond is forfeited. In a famous speech he defends his common humanity. Tubal, a Jewish friend, reports that Jessica has been spending Shylock''s money recklessly in Genoa—she traded a ring given to Shylock by his wife Leah for a monkey. Tubal also confirms that Antonio has lost a ship. Shylock''s grief over his daughter and his ducats mingles with his determination to press the bond.'),
('MV', 3, 2, 'Portia asks Bassanio to delay choosing, fearing she will lose him. He cannot wait and studies the three caskets. He chooses lead, correctly, and finds Portia''s portrait inside. The two pledge their love and Portia gives him a ring, making him swear never to lose it. Nerissa and Gratiano announce they are also engaged. Salerio arrives from Venice with a letter from Antonio: all his ships have failed and Shylock is demanding his bond. Bassanio confesses his poverty to Portia, who immediately offers to pay many times the debt. They arrange to marry quickly so Bassanio can go to Antonio''s aid.'),
('MV', 3, 3, 'Antonio is led through the streets by Shylock, who refuses all pleas for mercy and insists the bond must be honored. Antonio''s friend Solanio cannot persuade Shylock to relent. Antonio accepts his fate, placing his hope in Bassanio arriving from Belmont to bid him farewell before he dies.'),
('MV', 3, 4, 'Portia entrusts her household to Lorenzo and Jessica while she and Nerissa pretend to go to a monastery. In secret she sends a letter to her cousin Bellario, a famous lawyer in Padua, and arranges for herself and Nerissa to travel to Venice disguised as a young male lawyer and his clerk.'),
('MV', 3, 5, 'Launcelot jests with Jessica that she is damned because she is Shylock''s daughter, though Lorenzo counters that her conversion saves her. Lorenzo and Jessica praise each other in affectionate banter. A servant summons Lorenzo to dinner and Jessica commends Portia as incomparably virtuous.'),
('MV', 4, 1, 'The Duke presides over the trial. Antonio acknowledges the bond. Shylock refuses all offers of money and insists on the flesh. Bassanio offers twice the sum; Shylock refuses. The young lawyer Balthasar (Portia in disguise) arrives with Bellario''s endorsement. Portia delivers the famous "quality of mercy" speech, but Shylock will not yield. She declares the bond legal and tells Antonio to prepare. At the last moment she reveals that the bond gives Shylock flesh but not one drop of blood, and the flesh must be exactly one pound—any more or less and Shylock forfeits his life and wealth. Shylock tries to accept money instead but it is too late. Portia then invokes a Venetian law: an alien who plots the death of a citizen loses all his property. Shylock is stripped of half his estate (the other half going to Antonio and thence to Lorenzo and Jessica), and is compelled to convert to Christianity. The court accepts these terms and Shylock exits, broken.'),
('MV', 4, 2, 'Portia (still disguised as Balthasar) and Nerissa (as her clerk) are outside the court when Gratiano catches up to give Bassanio''s thanks and the ring Portia had asked for as payment. Nerissa decides to try to get Gratiano''s ring from him as well.'),
('MV', 5, 1, 'Lorenzo and Jessica sit in moonlit Belmont exchanging romantic allusions. Portia and Nerissa return ahead of their husbands. Bassanio and Gratiano arrive and the women demand to know where their rings are. Both men confess they gave the rings to the lawyers. After teasing them, Portia and Nerissa reveal they were the disguised lawyers. Portia also brings letters confirming that three of Antonio''s ships have come safely into harbor. Lorenzo and Jessica learn they will inherit Shylock''s estate. The play ends in joy and reconciliation.'),

('TN', 1, 1, 'Orsino, Duke of Illyria, wallows in romantic melancholy, obsessed with his love for the countess Olivia. He sends Curio to fetch musicians so he can indulge his mood. A servant reports that Olivia refuses all suitors, having vowed to mourn her dead brother for seven years. Orsino luxuriates in the idea of love, comparing his desires to the sea.'),
('TN', 1, 2, 'Viola is rescued from a shipwreck on the coast of Illyria. She fears her twin brother Sebastian has drowned. The Captain who saved her describes the land and tells her of Duke Orsino and the mourning Countess Olivia. Viola decides to disguise herself as a boy called Cesario and enter Orsino''s service while her future is uncertain.'),
('TN', 1, 3, 'At Olivia''s house, her uncle Sir Toby Belch carouses despite his niece''s grief. He has brought his friend Sir Andrew Aguecheek as a potential suitor for Olivia. Maria, Olivia''s lady-in-waiting, scolds Toby for his late-night reveling. Sir Andrew is gauche and foolish but is persuaded to stay on and continue courting Olivia.'),
('TN', 1, 4, 'Viola, now in Orsino''s service as Cesario, has quickly won the Duke''s trust. Orsino sends Cesario to woo Olivia on his behalf. Viola privately admits she has fallen in love with Orsino herself, making the mission painful, but she carries it out.'),
('TN', 1, 5, 'Olivia''s jester Feste returns after an absence and verbally spars with Malvolio, her pompous steward. Cesario (Viola) arrives and insists on speaking with Olivia directly. Olivia unveils her face and listens to Cesario''s prepared speech of love for Orsino. She is not moved by Orsino''s suit, but finds herself attracted to the young messenger. After Cesario leaves, Olivia sends Malvolio after him with a ring, pretending Cesario left it behind—a ruse to draw him back.'),
('TN', 2, 1, 'On the seacoast, Sebastian has also survived the wreck. He grieves for his twin sister Viola, whom he believes drowned. Antonio, the sailor who saved him, wants to accompany him despite the danger—Antonio is wanted in Illyria for past offenses against Orsino''s ships. Sebastian plans to go to Orsino''s court and Antonio follows despite the risk.'),
('TN', 2, 2, 'Malvolio catches up to Cesario and gives back the ring, as Olivia commanded. Viola realizes Olivia has fallen in love with her male disguise. She muses on the complicated situation: she loves Orsino, Orsino loves Olivia, and Olivia loves Cesario, who is really a woman. She trusts that time will untangle the knot.'),
('TN', 2, 3, 'Sir Toby, Sir Andrew, and Feste carouse late at night with drinking and singing. Maria warns them to be quiet or Olivia will dismiss Toby from the house. Malvolio appears and threatens them all with consequences. After he leaves, Maria reveals a plan to humiliate him: she will write a letter in Olivia''s handwriting implying that Olivia loves Malvolio, and leave it for him to find.'),
('TN', 2, 4, 'Orsino and Cesario talk about love. Orsino insists that no woman could love as deeply as a man; Viola-as-Cesario carefully hints that she herself knows such deep love, speaking of a "father''s daughter" who pined in silence. Feste sings the sad song "Come Away, Death." Orsino sends Cesario again to Olivia with his suit.'),
('TN', 2, 5, 'Sir Toby, Sir Andrew, and Fabian hide in the garden and watch as Malvolio, alone, daydreams aloud about marrying Olivia and becoming her lord. He finds Maria''s forged letter, which he instantly believes is from Olivia declaring her love for him. The letter instructs him to smile constantly, wear cross-gartered yellow stockings, and behave haughtily to Sir Toby. Malvolio resolves to follow every instruction. The conspirators are delighted.'),
('TN', 3, 1, 'Cesario arrives at Olivia''s house and exchanges witty banter with Feste. Olivia is alone with Cesario and openly declares her love. Cesario gently but firmly says he cannot return that love and his heart belongs to another. Olivia is undeterred and hopes Cesario will come again.'),
('TN', 3, 2, 'Sir Andrew, seeing Olivia''s attentiveness to Cesario, is ready to leave and give up his courtship. Fabian and Sir Toby convince him that Olivia is only showing favor to Cesario to provoke his jealousy, and urge him to challenge Cesario to a duel to prove his courage and impress Olivia. Andrew agrees and goes off to write a challenge letter. Maria enters to report that Malvolio has appeared before Olivia in yellow stockings, cross-gartered, and smiling—and Olivia thinks he has gone mad.'),
('TN', 3, 3, 'Antonio has followed Sebastian into the city despite the danger. He gives Sebastian his purse so Sebastian can shop for whatever he likes. They agree to meet later at an inn called the Elephant.'),
('TN', 3, 4, 'Malvolio appears before Olivia smiling strangely in yellow cross-gartered stockings. Olivia thinks he is ill and leaves him with Sir Toby to be looked after. Toby and the others pretend Malvolio is possessed and have him locked in a dark room. Sir Andrew delivers his absurd challenge letter to Cesario. Toby arranges a duel between Cesario and Andrew—neither of whom wants to fight—by telling each that the other is a fearsome duellist. Antonio appears and draws his sword to defend Cesario, whom he mistakes for Sebastian. Officers arrive and arrest Antonio for his old offenses. Antonio asks Cesario (thinking him Sebastian) for his purse back, but Viola does not know who he is. Antonio is led away in confusion.'),
('TN', 4, 1, 'Sebastian is mistaken for Cesario by Feste and then by Sir Andrew, who strikes him. Unlike Viola, Sebastian strikes back. Sir Toby draws his sword; Sebastian holds his own. Olivia enters, sends the men away, and leads Sebastian inside, calling him her beloved Cesario. Sebastian is bewildered but delighted by this beautiful woman''s attention.'),
('TN', 4, 2, 'Feste, disguised as a curate named Sir Topas, visits the imprisoned Malvolio in the dark room. He taunts Malvolio by pretending the room is not dark at all and that Malvolio is truly mad. Later, dropping the disguise, Feste speaks with Malvolio more directly. Malvolio begs for paper and ink to write to Olivia.'),
('TN', 4, 3, 'Sebastian marvels at his good fortune—the beautiful Olivia loves him and has given him a pearl. He wonders whether he is dreaming. Olivia arrives with a priest and asks Sebastian to enter into a formal betrothal contract with her immediately. Sebastian agrees without hesitation.'),
('TN', 5, 1, 'All the threads converge. Antonio is brought before Orsino and claims he saved Cesario, whom he calls Sebastian. Olivia enters and greets Cesario as her husband, causing Orsino to feel betrayed. Sir Andrew and Sir Toby enter bleeding from a fight with someone they believe is Cesario. Sebastian himself appears, greeting Antonio and apologizing to Toby and Andrew. The twins see each other for the first time and everyone is astonished by their likeness. Viola reveals her true identity; Orsino realizes he loves her and proposes. Malvolio is released and shows Olivia the forged letter; Maria and Toby''s trick is exposed. Olivia promises to investigate. Malvolio storms off vowing revenge. The play ends with the couples united and Feste singing a closing song.'),

('AYL', 1, 1, 'Orlando, youngest son of the late Sir Rowland de Bois, complains that his eldest brother Oliver keeps him in ignorance and poverty, denying him the education and gentleman''s status their father wished him to have. The brothers quarrel and Oliver seizes Orlando by the throat. After Orlando leaves, Oliver plots with the court wrestler Charles to injure or kill Orlando in the next day''s match.'),
('AYL', 1, 2, 'At court, cousins Rosalind and Celia watch the wrestling. Duke Frederick, Celia''s father, has usurped the throne from Rosalind''s father Duke Senior, who lives in exile in the Forest of Arden. Charles, the champion wrestler, easily defeats three men. Orlando steps up to wrestle despite the ladies'' warnings. Orlando beats Charles to everyone''s amazement. Rosalind is charmed by Orlando and gives him her chain. Oliver reports that Orlando is the son of the duke''s old enemy Rowland, and Frederick becomes hostile toward Orlando.'),
('AYL', 1, 3, 'Duke Frederick suddenly banishes Rosalind from court, claiming he cannot trust her. Celia refuses to stay without her cousin and decides to go with her. The two plan to disguise themselves: Rosalind will dress as a young man called Ganymede, and Celia as a country girl called Aliena. They take the court jester Touchstone with them and head for the Forest of Arden.'),
('AYL', 2, 1, 'In the Forest of Arden, the exiled Duke Senior lives contentedly with his followers, finding wisdom and beauty in the natural world. He goes hunting with his lords and philosophizes about their simple forest life. One lord reports that the melancholy Jacques has been moralizing at length over a wounded deer.'),
('AYL', 2, 2, 'Duke Frederick discovers that Celia has fled with Rosalind and that Touchstone is also missing. He is told that Orlando may have gone with them. Frederick orders Oliver to be brought before him.'),
('AYL', 2, 3, 'Orlando''s loyal old servant Adam warns him that Oliver plans to burn his lodgings and kill him. Adam urges Orlando to flee and offers his life savings of five hundred crowns. The two set off together for the forest.'),
('AYL', 2, 4, 'Rosalind (as Ganymede), Celia (as Aliena), and Touchstone arrive exhausted in the Forest of Arden. They overhear the shepherd Silvius lamenting his unrequited love for Phebe to the old shepherd Corin. Rosalind recognizes Silvius''s feelings and sympathizes. They learn that Corin''s master wants to sell his land and cottage. Rosalind and Celia buy the cottage and flock to stay in Arden.'),
('AYL', 2, 5, 'The melancholy Jacques encounters the singer Amiens and asks him to continue singing a song about the pleasures of forest life. Jacques adds his own sardonic verse about fools who willingly leave civilization for the woods. Duke Senior sends for Jacques.'),
('AYL', 2, 6, 'Orlando and the exhausted Adam arrive in the forest. Adam is too weak to go further. Orlando promises to find food for him and goes searching.'),
('AYL', 2, 7, 'Orlando bursts in on Duke Senior''s meal demanding food at sword-point. The Duke calmly invites him to eat first and explain. Orlando goes back to fetch Adam. In the meantime Jacques describes meeting a motley fool (Touchstone) in the forest and delivers his famous "All the world''s a stage" speech listing the seven ages of man. Orlando returns carrying Adam and they eat together. The Duke recognizes that Orlando is his old friend Rowland''s son and welcomes him warmly.'),
('AYL', 3, 1, 'Duke Frederick threatens Oliver: if he does not find Orlando and bring him back within a year, Oliver''s lands and property will be seized. Oliver promises to seek Orlando.'),
('AYL', 3, 2, 'Orlando hangs love poems to Rosalind on the trees of the forest. Touchstone mocks a shepherd''s pastoral life. Rosalind, still dressed as Ganymede, reads one of Orlando''s poems aloud. Celia reveals that she has seen the man writing the poems and it is Orlando. Rosalind is ecstatic but tries to contain herself. Orlando enters with Jacques. After Jacques leaves, Rosalind-as-Ganymede teases Orlando, offers to cure him of his lovesickness, and proposes he court her as if she were his Rosalind so she can rehearse him out of his infatuation.'),
('AYL', 3, 3, 'Touchstone woos the goat-girl Audrey in the forest. Jacques observes and comments. A vicar named Sir Oliver Martext arrives to marry them in the forest, but Jacques intervenes, advising Touchstone to be married properly in church. Touchstone, perhaps relieved, accepts the advice.'),
('AYL', 3, 4, 'Rosalind is upset because Orlando has not appeared for their mock-courtship session. Celia teases her. Corin invites them to watch the shepherd Silvius try to court the disdainful Phebe.'),
('AYL', 3, 5, 'Silvius pleads his love to Phebe, who scornfully rejects him. Rosalind-as-Ganymede interrupts and scolds Phebe for treating a good man so cruelly. Phebe, struck by the young stranger''s boldness, immediately falls in love with Ganymede. Rosalind tells Phebe to value Silvius. After Rosalind leaves, Phebe asks Silvius to carry a letter she will write to Ganymede, and Silvius, still devoted, agrees.'),
('AYL', 4, 1, 'Orlando arrives late for his appointment with Ganymede, who scolds him. They play out the mock-courtship with Celia acting as a minister; the game grows so real that Rosalind nearly forgets herself. Orlando says he must leave to attend the Duke but promises to return at two o''clock. After he goes Celia teases Rosalind about how deeply in love she really is.'),
('AYL', 4, 2, 'Jacques and the lords celebrate killing a deer in the forest with a song honoring the victorious hunter with the deer''s antlers.'),
('AYL', 4, 3, 'Orlando does not return. Silvius arrives with Phebe''s letter, which Rosalind says is full of insults—she reads it as insulting but Silvius, who has not read it, thinks it is gentle. Oliver suddenly arrives in the forest; he has been transformed by a change of heart. He explains that Orlando found him sleeping under a tree near a lioness and a snake, and saved his life at the risk of his own, suffering a wound on his arm. Oliver and Orlando are now reconciled. Oliver brings a bloody handkerchief from Orlando as a token to Ganymede. Rosalind, hearing Orlando is hurt, faints—breaking her disguise momentarily. She recovers and insists she was only acting.'),
('AYL', 5, 1, 'Touchstone encounters William, a country bumpkin who also has a claim on Audrey''s affections. Touchstone cleverly and comically intimidates William into giving up his suit for Audrey.'),
('AYL', 5, 2, 'Oliver tells Orlando he has fallen in love with Aliena (Celia) and wants to marry her the next day. Orlando is surprised but gives his blessing. Orlando admits he is weary of being wooed by proxy and longs for the real Rosalind. Rosalind-as-Ganymede promises that by magic she will arrange for the real Rosalind to appear and marry Orlando the next day. She also tells Phebe (who arrives) that if Ganymede ever refuses to marry her, she must agree to marry Silvius instead, and Phebe accepts this.'),
('AYL', 5, 3, 'Touchstone and Audrey look forward to their wedding tomorrow. Two pages arrive and sing a joyful spring song for them.'),
('AYL', 5, 4, 'All gather for the weddings. Rosalind, still as Ganymede, confirms the pledges: Phebe will marry Ganymede or Silvius; Orlando will marry Rosalind. Rosalind leaves with Celia and returns as herself, accompanied by the figure of Hymen, god of marriage. Phebe accepts Silvius when she realizes Ganymede is a woman. Four couples are united: Orlando and Rosalind, Oliver and Celia, Silvius and Phebe, Touchstone and Audrey. A messenger arrives with news that Duke Frederick, on his way to attack his brother with an army, met a religious hermit on the road and was converted to a good life; he restores the dukedom to Duke Senior. Jacques decides to stay with the converted Frederick rather than return to court. The happy company departs for celebrations.'),
('AYL', 6, 0, 'Rosalind speaks the epilogue directly to the audience. She acknowledges the unusual situation of a woman speaking an epilogue. She charmingly asks the women in the audience to approve the play for love of their men, and the men for love of their women. She curtsies and bids the audience farewell.'),

('Ado', 1, 1, 'Don Pedro, Prince of Aragon, arrives in Messina with his officers after a victorious war, welcomed by Governor Leonato and his daughter Hero. Beatrice and the soldier Benedick immediately exchange sharp and witty insults, resuming a "merry war" of words. Don Pedro''s illegitimate brother Don John also arrives, surly and resentful. Claudio, a young soldier, confides to Benedick that he is in love with Hero. Don Pedro promises to woo Hero in Claudio''s name during the evening''s masked ball.'),
('Ado', 1, 2, 'Leonato''s brother Antonio tells him a servant overheard Don Pedro telling Claudio of his plan to woo Hero himself at the dance. Antonio mistakenly thinks Don Pedro is in earnest and plans to marry Hero. Leonato takes the news with caution and tells Hero to be prepared for the offer.'),
('Ado', 1, 3, 'Don John broods in his villainy with his follower Conrade. Another follower, Borachio, reports what he overheard—that Don Pedro will woo Hero on Claudio''s behalf. Don John plans to use this information to cause trouble for Claudio, whom he hates.'),
('Ado', 2, 1, 'At the masked dance Leonato prepares his household. During the masquerade Don Pedro woos Hero on Claudio''s behalf while other couples dance. Don John and Borachio tell Claudio—as if not recognizing him—that Don Pedro is wooing Hero for himself. Claudio is briefly deceived and downcast. Beatrice, not knowing she speaks to Benedick, describes Benedick as "the Prince''s jester" in unflattering terms. Don Pedro clears up the confusion and formally gives Hero to Claudio. Leonato approves the match. Don Pedro and Leonato then conspire with others to trick Beatrice and Benedick into falling in love with each other.'),
('Ado', 2, 2, 'Don John and Borachio devise a plan to wreck the marriage of Claudio and Hero. Borachio will arrange to meet Hero''s servant Margaret at Hero''s window at night; Margaret will wear Hero''s clothes. Don John will bring Claudio and Don Pedro to witness the scene and make them believe Hero is unfaithful.'),
('Ado', 2, 3, 'Benedick hides in the orchard to eavesdrop, suspecting a trap. Balthasar sings a song. Don Pedro, Claudio, and Leonato then stage a conversation for Benedick to overhear, saying that Beatrice is secretly and hopelessly in love with Benedick but too proud to confess it. Benedick is completely taken in and decides then and there that he will love Beatrice in return.'),
('Ado', 3, 1, 'Hero and Ursula stage the same trap for Beatrice in the garden. Beatrice listens from behind an arbor as they discuss how Benedick is deeply in love with her but Beatrice is too mocking and proud to deserve him. Beatrice is equally taken in and resolves to return Benedick''s love and abandon her contempt for marriage.'),
('Ado', 3, 2, 'Don Pedro, Claudio, and Leonato tease Benedick about obvious signs that he is in love—he has shaved and begun dressing more carefully. Don John arrives and tells Don Pedro and Claudio that Hero is disloyal; he offers to show them proof at her window that very night.'),
('Ado', 3, 3, 'Dogberry, the bumbling constable, and his deputy Verges organize the incompetent night watch. The watchmen overhear Borachio boasting drunkenly to Conrade about how he deceived Don Pedro and Claudio by standing at Hero''s window with Margaret dressed as Hero. They arrest both Borachio and Conrade, though they barely understand what they have heard.'),
('Ado', 3, 4, 'Hero prepares for her wedding with the help of Margaret and Ursula. Beatrice appears, unusually subdued and sighing—clearly lovesick. Margaret teases her about it. The women head to the church.'),
('Ado', 3, 5, 'Dogberry and Verges try to report to Leonato about the arrested men before the wedding. Leonato, busy with preparations, cannot follow their confused explanations and tells them to examine the prisoners themselves. The information never properly gets through.'),
('Ado', 4, 1, 'At the church, Claudio refuses to marry Hero and denounces her publicly as a wanton who slept with another man the night before. Don Pedro supports the accusation and Don John adds his voice. Hero faints. Her father Leonato, in anguish, wishes her dead. Benedick and the Friar remain skeptical. The Friar proposes a plan: spread the word that Hero has died of shame. Claudio''s grief and the investigation may reveal the truth. After the others leave, Benedick and Beatrice declare their love for each other. Beatrice asks Benedick to prove his love by challenging Claudio to a duel.'),
('Ado', 4, 2, 'Dogberry and Verges conduct a farcical examination of Borachio and Conrade. Despite the disorder, Borachio''s confession is extracted. The Sexton writes it all down and exits to tell Leonato, and the prisoners are led away.'),
('Ado', 5, 1, 'Leonato and his brother Antonio are furious at Don Pedro and Claudio and confront them. Benedick arrives and quietly challenges Claudio to a duel. Don John has fled Messina. Dogberry arrives with Borachio, who confesses the whole plot and the innocence of Hero. Don Pedro and Claudio are stricken with remorse. Leonato tells Claudio that Hero died of grief and that to make amends he must publicly clear her name and then marry Leonato''s niece, whom he has never seen.'),
('Ado', 5, 2, 'Benedick tries to write a poem for Beatrice and fails. They talk and confirm their love. Margaret enters with news that Don Pedro and Claudio are coming; Benedick goes to meet them.'),
('Ado', 5, 3, 'At a monument, Claudio and Don Pedro solemnly read aloud an epitaph for Hero and promise to honor her memory annually. They depart for the wedding.'),
('Ado', 5, 4, 'All gather for the wedding of Claudio to Leonato''s supposed niece, who is veiled. When Claudio takes her and she unmasks, she is revealed to be Hero, who was not truly dead. Benedick and Beatrice each pretend they do not love the other until their friends produce the love sonnets each had written; they agree to marry. A messenger brings news of Don John''s capture. The play ends with dancing and celebration.'),

('AWW', 1, 1, 'Helena, an orphan physician''s daughter, lives in the household of the Countess of Rossillion. The Countess''s son Bertram is leaving for the court of the ailing King of France. Helena reveals in soliloquy that she loves Bertram but thinks him impossibly above her station. She also remembers a cure for the King''s illness that she learned from her father.'),
('AWW', 1, 2, 'At the court of France, the King mourns his illness and the death of Helena''s father, whom he remembers as a great physician. Young lords including Bertram arrive from Rossillion. The King regrets he is too ill to join the war in Florence and speaks warmly of old Sir Rossillion, Bertram''s father.'),
('AWW', 1, 3, 'The Countess discovers through the clown Lavatch and her steward Rinaldo that Helena loves Bertram. She questions Helena, who is embarrassed but confesses her love. The Countess treats her kindly and calls her a daughter. Helena reveals she plans to travel to Paris to cure the King with her father''s remedy, partly to help the King and partly to be near Bertram.'),
('AWW', 2, 1, 'Bertram stays at court as the King sends other lords off to the Italian wars; Bertram wants to go too but the King forbids it, telling him he is too young. Helena arrives at court and offers to cure the King. He is skeptical but she proposes a bold bargain: if she succeeds, she may choose any husband from among his noblemen; if she fails, she will die. The King accepts.'),
('AWW', 2, 2, 'The Countess sends the clown Lavatch to Paris with a letter to Helena. Lavatch provides comic interlude with his talk of court life.'),
('AWW', 2, 3, 'Helena has cured the King. As agreed, she selects her husband from among the assembled lords, choosing Bertram. Bertram is appalled and refuses to marry a girl of low birth. The King insists, invoking his royal authority. Bertram grudgingly agrees but immediately tells his friend Parolles that he intends to send Helena back to Rossillion without consummating the marriage and go off to the wars.'),
('AWW', 2, 4, 'Parolles delivers to Helena Bertram''s letter telling her to return to Rossillion and to ask the Countess''s blessing, while he goes to the wars. Helena accepts the dismissal with sad dignity.'),
('AWW', 2, 5, 'Bertram prepares to leave for Florence. When Helena approaches him for a farewell kiss, he coldly refuses and sends her away. He leaves immediately.'),
('AWW', 3, 1, 'In Florence, the Duke of Florence welcomes the French lords to the war. They report that France has officially stayed neutral, but many French gentlemen have come to fight voluntarily.'),
('AWW', 3, 2, 'Helena arrives at Rossillion with Bertram''s letter to his mother. The letter states he will only be her husband in name: he will never be with her as a wife unless she can get the ring from his finger—which he never means to give—and show him a child of his fathering, which is impossible since he refuses to sleep with her. The Countess is furious at her son. Helena, reading his words as absolving her of being an obstacle to him, decides to secretly leave Rossillion and wander in pilgrimage, so Bertram may return home.'),
('AWW', 3, 3, 'In Florence, Bertram is given command of the cavalry by the Duke and proves himself a courageous soldier.'),
('AWW', 3, 4, 'Helena has disappeared from Rossillion, leaving a letter saying she has gone on pilgrimage. The Countess is upset and writes to Bertram urging him to return and take back his wife.'),
('AWW', 3, 5, 'In Florence, a widow and her daughter Diana talk with neighbors about the French soldiers, including the gallant Count Rossillion who has been courting Diana. Helena arrives as a pilgrim and meets them. She reveals she is Bertram''s wife and offers the widow gold if Diana will help execute a plan.'),
('AWW', 3, 6, 'The French lords plot with Bertram to expose Parolles as a coward. They devise a trick: they will ambush Parolles when he goes to retrieve a drum he lost in battle, blindfold him, and let him betray his comrades under imaginary interrogation to save his own skin.'),
('AWW', 3, 7, 'Helena explains her plan to the widow: Diana will agree to sleep with Bertram if he gives her his ring and sets up a nighttime meeting, but Helena will take Diana''s place in the dark—the bed-trick. This will satisfy both conditions of Bertram''s impossible letter. The widow agrees.'),
('AWW', 4, 1, 'Parolles sets out to retrieve the drum. The lords ambush him, speak in a mock foreign language, and blindfold him. He immediately surrenders and promises to reveal all military secrets to save himself.'),
('AWW', 4, 2, 'Bertram woos Diana and she asks for his ancestral ring, which he has said he will never part with. Under pressure of desire he gives it. She agrees to meet him that night for one hour in the dark, on condition that he not speak. The meeting takes place—but it is Helena who is there in the dark, and Helena who receives him.'),
('AWW', 4, 3, 'The lords discuss Bertram''s behavior: he has rejected his wife, and Helena has reportedly died on pilgrimage. They also reveal that Diana has given them Helena''s ring as a token from Bertram. The "interrogation" of blindfolded Parolles proceeds: he denounces his friends and superiors one by one to save himself. His blindfold is removed and he sees his betrayers. He is disgraced but resolves to live on somehow.'),
('AWW', 4, 4, 'Helena tells the widow and Diana that all is proceeding according to plan. She has the ring from Bertram''s finger. She sends Diana ahead to the French court with a letter and instructions for what to say there. Helena herself is on her way.'),
('AWW', 4, 5, 'At Rossillion, the Countess and the lord Lafeu mourn Helena''s reported death. Lavatch is there for comic relief. Lafeu proposes his own daughter as a new wife for Bertram, should he return, and the Countess agrees. A letter arrives saying Bertram is coming home.'),
('AWW', 5, 1, 'Helena and the widow arrive in Marseilles to find the King has already moved on to Rossillion. They follow.'),
('AWW', 5, 2, 'Parolles arrives at Rossillion, now homeless and destitute after his disgrace. Lafeu, though contemptuous, takes pity on him and promises to put in a good word with the Countess.'),
('AWW', 5, 3, 'The King, the Countess, Lafeu, and Bertram gather. Bertram apologizes for his treatment of Helena and appears to accept Lafeu''s daughter as a new wife. He gives Lafeu a ring as a token—but the King recognizes it as the ring he gave Helena. Bertram is unable to explain where he got it. Diana and the widow appear and Diana claims Bertram seduced her and owes her marriage. Bertram denies everything. Diana produces Bertram''s ring and confusion mounts. At last Helena herself appears, visibly pregnant. She tells Bertram she has satisfied both conditions of his letter: she has his ring and carries his child. Bertram, astonished, pledges to love her truly. The King promises Diana her pick of a husband and a dowry.'),
('AWW', 6, 0, 'The King speaks directly to the audience, acknowledging the play''s improbable resolution with the phrase "All''s well that ends well." He asks the audience to grant the play their applause, and promises that if the story has seemed strange, their approval will make it worthwhile.'),

('MM', 1, 1, 'In Vienna, Duke Vincentio tells his trusted counselor Escalus that he is leaving the city and placing Angelo in charge as his deputy, with full ducal authority. The Duke says he has been too lenient in enforcing the city''s laws against vice. He leaves before Angelo arrives, and Angelo learns of his sudden elevation to power.'),
('MM', 1, 2, 'Lucio and two gentlemen discuss the war and the plague of venereal disease in Vienna. They encounter Mistress Overdone, a brothel-keeper, who tells them that Claudio has been arrested and sentenced to death for getting Juliet pregnant outside of marriage. Angelo is enforcing old laws against fornication with new severity. Pompey, Overdone''s servant, explains that the proclamation will close the city''s brothels.'),
('MM', 1, 3, 'The Duke visits the Friar Thomas and reveals his real plan: he is not traveling abroad but will disguise himself as a friar to observe Angelo''s rule. He explains that he himself has been too slack in enforcing the laws and wants to see how Angelo handles the city, as well as test Angelo''s character.'),
('MM', 1, 4, 'Claudio''s sister Isabella is about to enter a convent. Her friend Lucio arrives and tells her Claudio has been arrested and condemned to die. Lucio urges Isabella to go immediately to Angelo and plead for Claudio''s life, arguing that her eloquence and virtue will move Angelo more than anything else could.'),
('MM', 2, 1, 'Angelo and Escalus debate justice: Angelo insists on strict enforcement while Escalus argues for mercy. They hear a comic case involving Constable Elbow, the bawd Pompey, and the foolish Master Froth—a case so muddled that nothing is resolved. Escalus privately orders Pompey to stop his trade and lets him off with a warning.'),
('MM', 2, 2, 'Isabella comes to Angelo to plead for Claudio''s life. Lucio supports her from the sidelines. Angelo is unmoved at first. Isabella argues for mercy with increasing passion. Angelo finds himself unexpectedly moved—not by mercy but by desire for Isabella herself. He tells her to come back the next day. Alone, Angelo is horrified to find himself tempted by the very virtue he has always prided himself on, and by lust for a woman vowed to purity.'),
('MM', 2, 3, 'The disguised Duke, posing as Friar Lodowick, visits the prison and ministers to the condemned Juliet. He confirms she repents her sin of love, and she says she does more than Claudio does. The Duke tells her Claudio will die tomorrow.'),
('MM', 2, 4, 'Angelo summons Isabella. He hints that she must give herself to him to save her brother. She misunderstands at first. He states his terms bluntly: if she will sleep with him, Claudio will live; if not, Claudio dies. Isabella refuses indignantly and says she will expose him. Angelo calls her bluff, pointing out no one will believe her word against his. Isabella leaves, determined to tell Claudio and certain her brother will agree that honor matters more than life.'),
('MM', 3, 1, 'The disguised Duke speaks to the imprisoned Claudio about accepting death. Claudio seems resigned. When Isabella arrives and tells him Angelo''s offer, Claudio at first echoes her refusal—but then, in fear, he begs her to yield to Angelo and save his life. Isabella is furious and reproaches him bitterly. The Duke, who has overheard, intervenes and speaks privately to Isabella. He proposes a plan: Mariana, a woman Angelo once promised to marry but abandoned when her dowry was lost, will take Isabella''s place in the dark—another bed-trick. Angelo will sleep with his own former betrothed without knowing it.'),
('MM', 3, 2, 'At the prison gate, Pompey is arrested again and sent to the prison as a hangman''s assistant. Lucio encounters the disguised Duke and slanders Duke Vincentio extravagantly to his face—calling him dissolute and a drunk—not knowing who he is. The Duke is privately amused and irritated. Escalus also speaks to the disguised friar about the case and the Duke''s character.'),
('MM', 4, 1, 'The disguised Duke visits Mariana at her moated grange and finds her listening to a sad song. He explains the plan. At first Mariana is reluctant, but he assures her that lying with Angelo is no sin since he was her betrothed, and that the Duke knows of and approves the stratagem. Mariana agrees.'),
('MM', 4, 2, 'At the prison, the Provost and Pompey discuss the hangman''s work. Angelo''s written order arrives: Claudio is to die at dawn and his head sent to Angelo. Then a second order arrives with a new name—the Duke has apparently sent a counter-instruction. The disguised Duke tells the Provost that Claudio will be pardoned and gives him a letter to be opened only when the Duke returns in four days. He instructs the Provost to substitute the head of a convict named Ragozine, who has just died naturally, for Claudio''s.'),
('MM', 4, 3, 'Pompey catalogs the prison''s criminal inhabitants for comic effect. Angelo''s orders arrive again—he wants confirmation Claudio is dead. The Provost sends Ragozine''s head as Claudio''s. The disguised Duke tells Isabella that Claudio is dead (a deception to test her), but promises she will be satisfied. He gives her a letter to deliver to Friar Peter and tells her to meet him at the city gates when Duke Vincentio publicly returns.'),
('MM', 4, 4, 'Angelo and Escalus are baffled by the Duke''s sudden announced return and his public letter asking anyone with grievances against his deputy to speak freely at the city gates. Angelo begins to feel anxiety about what may be revealed.'),
('MM', 4, 5, 'The Duke, now in his own clothes, gives Friar Peter his instructions. Various lords arrive as the Duke re-enters Vienna.'),
('MM', 4, 6, 'Isabella and Mariana are coached by Friar Peter on what to say and do in the coming public scene at the city gate.'),
('MM', 5, 1, 'The Duke returns publicly to Vienna. Isabella steps forward and publicly accuses Angelo of demanding sex in exchange for Claudio''s life. Angelo denies everything and the Duke pretends not to believe her. The Duke leaves and reappears as the friar; Lucio unmasks him accidentally. The Duke reveals himself. Mariana unveils and reveals she was the woman in Angelo''s bed. The truth about the bed-trick comes out. Angelo confesses and is sentenced to marry Mariana and then be executed. Mariana pleads for his life and Isabella joins her. The Provost produces the living Claudio. The Duke pardons Angelo. He then proposes marriage to Isabella. Lucio is sentenced to marry the woman he has wronged. Claudio and Juliet are reunited.'),

('Err', 1, 1, 'In Ephesus, a Syracusan merchant named Egeon is condemned to die because Ephesus and Syracuse are at war and Syracusans who enter Ephesus must pay a large ransom or be executed. Egeon cannot pay. He tells the Duke his sad story: years ago his twin sons were separated in a shipwreck along with twin slaves who bore the same names—one Antipholus and one Dromio were raised with him in Syracuse while their brothers were lost. His Syracusan son has gone searching and not returned, and Egeon followed to find him. The Duke is sympathetic and grants him until evening to raise the ransom.'),
('Err', 1, 2, 'Antipholus of Syracuse arrives in Ephesus with his slave Dromio of Syracuse and sends Dromio to the inn with their money. Almost immediately, Dromio of Ephesus—the other twin slave, employed by the other Antipholus who lives in Ephesus—arrives and calls Antipholus of Syracuse home to dinner, not recognizing him. Antipholus of Syracuse thinks his Dromio is playing a trick and beats him. Confused, Antipholus goes to look for his own Dromio and their money.'),
('Err', 2, 1, 'Adriana, the wife of Antipholus of Ephesus, argues with her sister Luciana about whether wives should be obedient to husbands. Dromio of Ephesus returns and tells her his master refuses to come home. Adriana is angry, fearing infidelity.'),
('Err', 2, 2, 'Antipholus of Syracuse meets his own Dromio, who denies the earlier conversation about a wife and dinner. Antipholus beats him for the denial. Adriana and Luciana arrive and Adriana passionately accuses Antipholus of Syracuse of infidelity to her as his wife. He is completely baffled but decides to play along and enters the house with them, leaving his Dromio at the door.'),
('Err', 3, 1, 'Antipholus of Ephesus arrives at his own house with Dromio of Ephesus and guests for dinner, only to find his Dromio (actually Dromio of Syracuse) refuses to admit him and his own wife''s voice comes from inside asking him to wait. He is locked out of his own house. Furious, he takes his guests to a nearby courtesan''s house to dine instead and decides to give the courtesan a chain he had made for his wife.'),
('Err', 3, 2, 'Inside the house, Luciana urges Antipholus of Syracuse (whom she thinks is her brother-in-law) to hide his infidelities better if he must have them. He declares he is not married and that he loves Luciana herself. Dromio of Syracuse enters in comic terror: a kitchen-maid named Nell claims him as her husband and he gives a grotesque description of her enormous body. Antipholus sends Dromio to find a ship for their departure. Angelo the goldsmith gives Antipholus of Syracuse the chain meant for Antipholus of Ephesus, who then departs with it.'),
('Err', 4, 1, 'A merchant presses Angelo the goldsmith for money he owes. Angelo expects to collect from Antipholus of Ephesus for the chain, but Antipholus of Ephesus denies receiving it. Angelo has Antipholus of Ephesus arrested. Antipholus of Ephesus sends Dromio of Ephesus home for money to pay bail.'),
('Err', 4, 2, 'Dromio of Ephesus arrives at the house and tells Adriana and Luciana that Antipholus has been arrested. They send him back with bail money. Dromio meets Antipholus of Syracuse instead and gives him the money, causing more confusion.'),
('Err', 4, 3, 'Antipholus of Syracuse is wearing the chain around his neck and is accosted by the merchant who demands payment for it. He denies ordering it. A courtesan arrives and claims Antipholus promised her the chain in exchange for a ring—but this was Antipholus of Ephesus''s arrangement. Antipholus and Dromio of Syracuse flee, thinking the city is full of witches.'),
('Err', 4, 4, 'Dromio of Ephesus brings the money to Adriana, who sends him to bail out Antipholus—but finds Antipholus of Syracuse instead. Antipholus of Ephesus is furious at Dromio for not coming sooner and beats him. Adriana and Luciana arrive; an exorcist named Dr. Pinch is brought in, believing Antipholus of Ephesus is possessed. Both Antipholus and Dromio of Ephesus are bound and taken away. Antipholus of Syracuse and his Dromio enter with drawn swords and everyone flees in terror.'),
('Err', 5, 1, 'Antipholus and Dromio of Syracuse take refuge in a priory, where the Abbess refuses to give them up, diagnosing Antipholus as mad from a jealous wife. Adriana protests. The Duke passes with Egeon on his way to execution. Antipholus of Ephesus arrives, escaped from Pinch, and complains bitterly to the Duke of his mistreatment. Adriana is confused—she thought he was bound in her house. The Abbess brings out Antipholus of Syracuse and his Dromio, and the two pairs of twins stand face to face at last. All the confusions are resolved. The Abbess reveals herself as Egeon''s long-lost wife Emilia. The Duke pardons Egeon. The reunited family and all the characters celebrate together.'),

('LLL', 1, 1, 'Ferdinand, King of Navarre, persuades his three lords—Berowne, Longaville, and Dumaine—to sign an oath to study for three years, avoiding the company of women and living austerely. Berowne signs reluctantly, pointing out that the Princess of France is already on her way on a diplomatic mission. The comic rustic Costard is caught with the wench Jaquenetta, having violated the new decree against consorting with women. Don Adriano de Armado, a fantastical Spanish braggart, reports the offense.'),
('LLL', 1, 2, 'Armado morosely confesses to his page Moth that he is in love with Jaquenetta, the country girl, despite his own superior station. He asks Moth to help him. Costard is in Armado''s custody and is sent to carry a letter from Armado to Jaquenetta.'),
('LLL', 2, 1, 'The Princess of France arrives with her ladies—Rosaline, Maria, and Katherine—and her advisor Boyet. The King cannot allow women inside his court due to his oath, so he meets them in a field. The lords and the ladies meet and are mutually attracted: Berowne to Rosaline, Longaville to Maria, Dumaine to Katherine. The diplomatic business concerns a debt France is owed. The King invites the Princess to lodge in the field.'),
('LLL', 3, 1, 'Armado enlists Costard to deliver a love letter to Jaquenetta. Berowne also gives Costard a letter to carry to Rosaline. Costard muddled the letters and carries both. Alone, Berowne soliloquizes in anguish at finding himself in love despite his oath, particularly with Rosaline, whom he considers a dark-eyed, not obviously beautiful woman.'),
('LLL', 4, 1, 'The Princess hunts deer and jokes with Boyet and her ladies. Costard delivers Armado''s letter to Jaquenetta, mistakenly giving it to the Princess instead of the one from Berowne. Boyet reads it aloud and everyone mocks Armado''s grandiose style.'),
('LLL', 4, 2, 'The pedantic schoolmaster Holofernes and the curate Nathaniel discuss the Princess''s hunt with Dull the constable. Jaquenetta arrives with Costard and shows them the letter she has received—which is actually Berowne''s love letter to Rosaline, misdirected. Holofernes recognizes the letter is addressed to Rosaline and sends Jaquenetta to deliver it to the King.'),
('LLL', 4, 3, 'Berowne hides and watches as the King, then Longaville, then Dumaine each enter in turn reading their own love poems aloud—each hiding from and then catching the next. When all three are exposed, Berowne steps out to mock them, but then Costard and Jaquenetta deliver his own love letter to Rosaline and he is caught too. All four confess they are in love. Berowne delivers a witty speech persuading them that breaking their oath for love is actually fulfilling a higher oath. They plan to woo the ladies with entertainment, costumes, and gifts.'),
('LLL', 5, 1, 'Holofernes plans with Nathaniel, Armado, and Costard to present an entertainment for the King and the ladies—the Nine Worthies, a pageant of famous heroes. Armado will play Hector.'),
('LLL', 5, 2, 'The ladies read and mock the love letters and gifts the lords have sent. The lords arrive disguised as Russian ambassadors and woo the ladies, but the ladies have swapped masks and identities, so each lord woos the wrong lady. The ladies later reveal the trick and mock the lords. The lords try a second approach, this time as themselves, but the ladies have learned the lords swore love to strangers. The Nine Worthies pageant is performed but rudely mocked and disrupted by the audience. A messenger arrives with grave news: the Princess''s father, the King of France, has died. All festivity stops. The Princess prepares to leave. Each lord presses his suit seriously now, but the ladies impose conditions: the men must spend a year in penance—the King in a hermitage, Berowne at a hospital tending the sick—before any marriage. Armado announces he too will spend three years performing an honest vow. The play ends not in weddings but in deferral, with two songs of spring and winter.')
;
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO scene_synopses (work_abbrev, div1, div2, synopsis) VALUES
-- The Tempest
('Tmp', 1, 1, 'A violent storm batters a ship carrying King Alonso of Naples, his son Ferdinand, and their court. The sailors struggle desperately to keep the vessel afloat while the noble passengers panic below deck. The boatswain bluntly tells the lords to stay out of the way, as their rank counts for nothing against the sea. The ship appears to founder, and all aboard seem doomed.'),
('Tmp', 1, 2, 'Prospero, a sorcerer living on a remote island with his daughter Miranda, reveals that he conjured the storm with his magic. He explains that he was once the rightful Duke of Milan, but his brother Antonio conspired with King Alonso to usurp his title and set him and the infant Miranda adrift at sea. Prospero''s spirit servant Ariel reports that he has safely scattered the shipwrecked passengers across the island without harm. Prospero then wakes Miranda, visits the monstrous Caliban who is forced to serve them, and directs Ariel to lead Ferdinand toward Miranda so that the two young people might fall in love.'),
('Tmp', 2, 1, 'King Alonso and his court wander the island, mourning what they believe is Ferdinand''s death by drowning. Gonzalo, an old counselor, tries to comfort the king with optimistic talk, while the cynical lords Antonio and Sebastian mock him. Ariel, invisible, plays music that sends most of the party to sleep. Antonio then tempts Sebastian to murder the sleeping Alonso and Gonzalo so that Sebastian can claim the throne of Naples. Just as they draw their swords, Ariel wakes Gonzalo, foiling the plot, and the group moves on in search of Ferdinand.'),
('Tmp', 2, 2, 'Caliban, carrying firewood, encounters Trinculo, the king''s jester, who takes shelter under Caliban''s cloak during a threatening storm. Stephano, the drunken butler, stumbles upon them and offers Caliban wine. Caliban, having never tasted alcohol, believes Stephano must be a god and eagerly pledges to serve him. The three agree to explore the island together, and Caliban promises to show Stephano all its resources in exchange for more wine, hoping Stephano will help him overthrow Prospero.'),
('Tmp', 3, 1, 'Ferdinand has been set to work by Prospero, hauling logs as a test of his worthiness. Miranda, disobeying her father''s instructions, visits Ferdinand and offers to share his labor. The two confess their love for each other and Ferdinand proposes marriage. Prospero, watching secretly, is pleased that his plan is working and blesses the union to himself while pretending not to approve.'),
('Tmp', 3, 2, 'Caliban, Stephano, and Trinculo continue their drunken plotting. Caliban urges Stephano to kill Prospero and take control of the island, promising him Miranda as a reward. Ariel, invisible, mimics Trinculo''s voice to sow discord between the conspirators. The plotters are distracted by the sound of Ariel''s music and follow it, not realizing Prospero has been warned of their scheme.'),
('Tmp', 3, 3, 'Alonso and his exhausted court give up their search for Ferdinand. Prospero causes a magical banquet to appear before them, and when the lords reach for the food, Ariel descends as a harpy and makes it vanish. Ariel then reminds Alonso, Antonio, and Sebastian of their crime against Prospero, declaring that nature itself is punishing them. Alonso is devastated and, believing Ferdinand is dead as punishment for his sins, rushes off in despair.'),
('Tmp', 4, 1, 'Prospero officially gives his blessing to Ferdinand and Miranda''s engagement, warning Ferdinand sternly to respect Miranda''s chastity until they are married. He then summons Ariel and other spirits to perform a masque celebrating the betrothal, featuring the goddesses Iris, Ceres, and Juno blessing the couple with fertility and abundance. The masque is abruptly halted when Prospero remembers Caliban''s plot against his life. He dismisses the spirits, explains to Ferdinand that all earthly things are insubstantial, and then sets a trap to catch the conspirators.'),
('Tmp', 5, 1, 'Ariel reports that Alonso and his companions are imprisoned and distressed, and expresses sympathy for them. Prospero declares he will forgive his enemies and renounce his magic. He releases the noblemen, reveals himself as the wronged Duke of Milan, and reunites with his old friend Gonzalo. Alonso is overcome with remorse and restores Prospero''s dukedom. Ferdinand and Miranda are revealed playing chess, and Alonso is overjoyed to find his son alive. Caliban and the drunken conspirators are brought in and punished, and Prospero promises to free Ariel once the ship is prepared for the voyage home.'),
('Tmp', 6, 0, 'Prospero delivers the epilogue directly to the audience, noting that his magic powers are gone and he is now just an ordinary man. He asks the audience to free him from the island with their applause, as prayer releases sinners and applause releases him. The speech frames the entire play as a theatrical illusion now concluded.'),
-- The Winter's Tale
('WT', 1, 1, 'Two Sicilian lords, Camillo and Archidamus, discuss the long friendship between King Leontes of Sicilia and King Polixenes of Bohemia, who have been friends since childhood. Archidamus praises the Sicilian court and expresses hope for an enduring alliance. The lords also speak admiringly of Prince Mamillius, Leontes'' young son, whose charm delights everyone at court.'),
('WT', 1, 2, 'Polixenes, who has been visiting Sicilia for nine months, announces he must return home to Bohemia. Leontes asks him to stay longer but Polixenes refuses, and Leontes asks his pregnant wife Hermione to persuade him. Hermione succeeds in convincing Polixenes to stay, and Leontes is suddenly and irrationally seized by jealousy, convincing himself that Polixenes and Hermione are having an affair. Leontes instructs his lord Camillo to poison Polixenes, but Camillo, believing in Hermione''s innocence, warns Polixenes instead and they flee together to Bohemia.'),
('WT', 2, 1, 'Hermione, heavily pregnant, plays happily with young Mamillius, who begins telling her a sad story. Leontes enters, learns of Polixenes and Camillo''s flight, and takes it as proof of his wife''s guilt. He publicly accuses Hermione of adultery and treason, and orders her arrested and imprisoned despite the protests of his lords. Hermione maintains her innocence with calm dignity as she is led away.'),
('WT', 2, 2, 'Paulina, a bold noblewoman and friend to Hermione, visits the prison to show support. She learns that Hermione has given birth to a daughter in captivity. Paulina resolves to bring the infant directly to Leontes, believing that the sight of his newborn child will soften his heart and restore his reason.'),
('WT', 2, 3, 'Leontes, sleepless and tormented, is told that his son Mamillius has fallen gravely ill from grief over his mother''s disgrace. Paulina brings the newborn baby girl to the king, insisting the child is obviously his and urging him to relent. Leontes furiously refuses to acknowledge the infant and orders Antigonus, Paulina''s husband, to take the baby to a remote wilderness and abandon her. Leontes announces he has sent to the oracle at Delphos for divine confirmation of his accusations.'),
('WT', 3, 1, 'Two Sicilian lords, Cleomenes and Dion, return from Delphos carrying the oracle''s sealed message. They remark on the majesty of the sacred island and the speed of their mission, and express hope that the oracle''s verdict will clear Hermione and end the king''s madness.'),
('WT', 3, 2, 'Hermione is brought to public trial and again calmly asserts her innocence. The oracle''s message is read aloud: it declares Hermione chaste, Polixenes blameless, Camillo honest, and Leontes a jealous tyrant, prophesying that he shall have no heir until what is lost is found. Leontes refuses to accept the oracle''s verdict. A servant rushes in to report that Mamillius has died. Hermione faints and is carried out, and Paulina returns moments later to announce that Hermione too has died. Leontes, his delusion shattered by catastrophe, is overcome with remorse.'),
('WT', 3, 3, 'Antigonus arrives on the Bohemian coast with the baby girl. He has had a dream in which Hermione''s ghost appeared and named the child Perdita, indicating she will never see her father again. He leaves the infant with a bundle of gold and a identifying documents. A bear suddenly chases Antigonus offstage and kills him. A Shepherd discovers the baby and calls his son, the Clown, who has just witnessed the bear''s attack and the shipwreck that killed the crew. The Shepherd takes Perdita home, calling it a lucky day for finding gold.'),
('WT', 4, 1, 'Time appears as a personified figure and delivers a chorus, explaining that sixteen years have passed. He announces that Perdita has grown up in Bohemia as a shepherd''s daughter, and that Polixenes has a son named Florizel. Time asks the audience''s patience for the jump forward in years.'),
('WT', 4, 2, 'Polixenes and Camillo discuss the young Prince Florizel, who has been neglecting court duties and visiting a shepherd''s cottage. Polixenes suspects his son is in love with a shepherd''s daughter and decides to investigate in disguise, asking Camillo to accompany him.'),
('WT', 4, 3, 'Autolycus, a cheerful rogue and former servant to Prince Florizel, introduces himself as a peddler and petty thief. He tricks the Clown out of his money by pretending to have been robbed and beaten. As the Clown hurries off to buy supplies for the sheep-shearing feast, Autolycus reveals his plan to attend the festival and pick more pockets.'),
('WT', 4, 4, 'At the sheep-shearing festival, Florizel (disguised as a shepherd named Doricles) and Perdita exchange vows of love. Polixenes and Camillo arrive disguised among the guests and watch the festivities. Autolycus sells ballads and trinkets to the delighted shepherds. When Florizel announces he will marry Perdita, Polixenes reveals himself, furiously forbids the match, threatens the old Shepherd, and storms off. Camillo, who longs to return to Sicilia, advises the young lovers to flee to Sicilia and present themselves to Leontes. Autolycus helps by exchanging clothes with Florizel, and the Shepherd and Clown decide to bring the evidence of Perdita''s origins to the king.'),
('WT', 5, 1, 'In Sicilia, the repentant Leontes is urged by his lords to remarry and produce an heir, but Paulina reminds him of the oracle''s prophecy and keeps him faithful to Hermione''s memory. Florizel and Perdita arrive, claiming to be ambassadors from Polixenes. Word comes that Polixenes has arrived in pursuit of his son. A lord reports that the old Shepherd and the Clown have brought documents proving Perdita''s royal parentage, causing Leontes great emotion.'),
('WT', 5, 2, 'Several gentlemen report the extraordinary events they have just witnessed: Leontes and Perdita''s recognition, the discovery of her true identity, and the revelation of how Antigonus died and the Shepherd found her. The scenes have produced weeping and wonder in all who witnessed them. Autolycus encounters the Shepherd and Clown, who have been made gentlemen by the king; they brush past him without the grudge he feared.'),
('WT', 5, 3, 'Paulina leads Leontes, Perdita, Florizel, and the court to view a newly unveiled statue of Hermione, supposedly carved by the Italian artist Giulio Romano. Leontes is overwhelmed by its lifelike appearance. Paulina announces that the statue can move if those present have faith, and commands it to descend. Hermione, who has been living in secret with Paulina, steps down from the pedestal alive. She embraces Leontes and blesses Perdita. The family is reunited, and Paulina is paired with Camillo.'),
-- Cymbeline
('Cym', 1, 1, 'Two gentlemen at the British court discuss recent events: King Cymbeline''s daughter Imogen has secretly married Posthumus Leonatus, a worthy but low-born man raised at court, against her father''s wishes. Cymbeline has banished Posthumus and intends Imogen to marry Cloten, the arrogant stepson of Cymbeline''s scheming new queen. Posthumus and Imogen bid a sorrowful farewell, exchanging tokens — her diamond bracelet, his ring.'),
('Cym', 1, 2, 'Cloten, the queen''s boorish son, quarrels with a lord who accompanied him in an encounter with the departing Posthumus. Cloten is vain, stupid, and easily flattered. The lord mocks him behind his back, revealing Cloten''s incompetence in the fight.'),
('Cym', 1, 3, 'Imogen''s loyal attendant Pisanio tells her that Posthumus has departed and describes how the ship carrying him gradually disappeared from sight. Imogen laments not having had more time to say farewell, and expresses her deep love and grief at the separation.'),
('Cym', 1, 4, 'In Rome, Posthumus has joined a group of Italian gentlemen. Philario''s guest Iachimo challenges Posthumus''s boasting about Imogen''s virtue and beauty, wagering that he can seduce her. Posthumus, confident in Imogen''s faithfulness, bets his diamond ring against Iachimo''s gold and accepts the wager.'),
('Cym', 1, 5, 'The queen meets with a doctor, Cornelius, requesting potent poisons. Cornelius, suspicious of her motives, gives her drugs that will produce a death-like sleep rather than actual death. Pisanio refuses to help the queen win Imogen over to Cloten''s suit, and the queen gives him the sleeping drug disguised as a restorative medicine, planning to poison Posthumus through him.'),
('Cym', 1, 6, 'Iachimo arrives in Britain and meets Imogen, boldly claiming that Posthumus has been faithless in Rome. Imogen angrily rejects this as a lie and defends her husband''s honor. Iachimo then reverses course, claiming it was a test of her devotion and praising her virtue. He asks Imogen to keep a trunk of valuables safe overnight in her bedchamber, and she innocently agrees.'),
('Cym', 2, 1, 'Cloten and two lords return from a tennis match, with Cloten in a foul temper over a lost wager. The lords discuss Iachimo''s arrival. One lord privately comments on Cloten''s stupidity and Imogen''s misfortune in having such a suitor.'),
('Cym', 2, 2, 'At night, Iachimo emerges from the trunk hidden in Imogen''s bedchamber while she sleeps. He surveys the room, reads the title of the book on her pillow, and removes the bracelet from her wrist. He also notes a mole on her breast. Satisfied with his evidence, he climbs back into the trunk before she wakes.'),
('Cym', 2, 3, 'Cloten has musicians play under Imogen''s window to woo her. Cymbeline and the queen discuss the expected Roman tribute. Cloten tries to speak to Imogen, who coldly tells him that Posthumus''s meanest garment is worth more to her than Cloten himself. Cloten is furious at the insult.'),
('Cym', 2, 4, 'Iachimo returns to Rome and presents Posthumus with Imogen''s bracelet, claiming he seduced her. He describes the furnishings of the bedchamber and the mole on Imogen''s breast. Posthumus, devastated, believes the lie and concedes the wager. Alone, he delivers a furious misogynistic speech, cursing women and vowing revenge on Imogen.'),
('Cym', 2, 5, 'Posthumus delivers a bitter soliloquy railing against women and their supposed deceitfulness, fueled by his belief in Imogen''s infidelity. He resolves to have her killed and writes to his servant Pisanio in Britain instructing him to murder her.'),
('Cym', 3, 1, 'Roman ambassadors arrive at the British court demanding the tribute that Britain owes to Rome. Cymbeline, urged on by the queen and Cloten, refuses to pay. The Romans declare war on Britain.'),
('Cym', 3, 2, 'Pisanio receives two letters from Posthumus: one ordering him to bring Imogen to Milford Haven and kill her there, and one for Imogen falsely telling her that Posthumus is waiting there. Pisanio is horrified by the murder order but shows Imogen the letter summoning her, and she eagerly prepares to travel to Wales to reunite with her husband.'),
('Cym', 3, 3, 'In the Welsh mountains, Belarius (living in exile as Morgan) emerges from a cave with two young men, Guiderius and Arviragus, whom he has raised as his sons. The audience learns that Belarius was falsely accused and banished by Cymbeline, and that the two youths are actually Cymbeline''s own sons, kidnapped in infancy. The young men display noble instincts despite their rough upbringing.'),
('Cym', 3, 4, 'Near Milford Haven, Pisanio reveals the truth to Imogen: Posthumus has ordered her death. He shows her the letter. Imogen is devastated but refuses to believe Posthumus acted freely, suspecting the queen''s poison. Pisanio proposes she disguise herself as a boy and travel to Rome with the Roman ambassador Lucius to be near Posthumus. He gives her the drug the queen gave him, not knowing it is a sleeping potion rather than medicine.'),
('Cym', 3, 5, 'Cymbeline discovers Imogen is missing. Cloten, obsessed and furious over her insult, forces Pisanio to tell him where she went. Pisanio gives Cloten a letter to mislead him and provides him with Posthumus''s clothes, which Cloten intends to wear when he pursues, abducts, and murders Posthumus in front of Imogen. Cloten heads to Wales.'),
('Cym', 3, 6, 'Imogen, exhausted and hungry, discovers the cave in the Welsh mountains and enters, finding food. She helps herself, leaving money in payment. Belarius and the two princes return home and discover her there, disguised as a boy calling herself Fidele. They are immediately charmed by the youth''s appearance and invite the stranger to stay with them.'),
('Cym', 3, 7, 'A Roman senator announces to a tribune that the emperor Lucius has been appointed general of the forces to invade Britain. They discuss the recruitment of soldiers from across the empire.'),
('Cym', 4, 1, 'Cloten, wearing Posthumus''s garments, arrives in the Welsh mountains and delivers a contemptuous soliloquy about his plans: he will cut off Posthumus''s head, rape Imogen before her, and drag her back to court. He hopes to meet Guiderius and his companions.'),
('Cym', 4, 2, 'Imogen falls ill and takes the drug Pisanio gave her; it induces a death-like sleep. Cloten arrives and confronts Guiderius, insulting him; Guiderius kills Cloten and cuts off his head. When the others find Imogen apparently dead, they mourn deeply and lay her out with flowers, singing the famous dirge beginning "Fear no more the heat o'' the sun." Imogen wakes to find beside her a headless body dressed in Posthumus''s clothes and believes it is Posthumus. She is overcome with grief. The Roman general Lucius arrives with his army and takes the weeping Fidele into his service.'),
('Cym', 4, 3, 'Cymbeline is distracted by three crises: Imogen is missing, the queen has fallen into a dangerous illness, and the Roman army is invading. He prepares to face the Roman forces despite the turmoil at court.'),
('Cym', 4, 4, 'Belarius and the two princes learn of the Roman invasion and debate whether to join the British defense. The young men, despite their rough mountain life, feel their noble blood stirring and insist on fighting for Britain. Belarius reluctantly agrees to join them.'),
('Cym', 5, 1, 'Posthumus, with the Roman forces, receives a bloody cloth from Pisanio that is supposed to prove Imogen is dead. He is immediately overcome with guilt and grief, wishing he had not ordered her death. He resolves to disguise himself as a British peasant and fight for Britain, hoping to die in battle as penance.'),
('Cym', 5, 2, 'The battle between the Romans and Britons is depicted. The British forces initially flee, but Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus rally them to fight. Posthumus, fighting as a British peasant, also helps turn the tide. The British win the battle and capture Lucius along with other Romans.'),
('Cym', 5, 3, 'Posthumus describes the miraculous turning point of the battle to a British lord, explaining how an old man and two young men rallied the British in a narrow lane. Afterwards, Posthumus — still alive and frustrated in his desire to die — changes back into Roman clothes so he can be captured as a prisoner and perhaps executed.'),
('Cym', 5, 4, 'Posthumus is imprisoned among the Roman captives. He falls asleep and the ghosts of his dead father, mother, and brothers appear and appeal to the god Jupiter for mercy on his behalf. Jupiter himself descends and promises that Posthumus will be fortunate. Posthumus wakes, finding a tablet with a cryptic prophecy. A jailer takes him to Cymbeline''s court for judgment.'),
('Cym', 5, 5, 'In an extended final scene, all threads are resolved. The dying queen''s deathbed confession reveals her plot against Cymbeline and Imogen. Lucius presents Fidele (Imogen in disguise) to Cymbeline, who grants the youth a boon. Iachimo confesses his deception to Cymbeline, and Posthumus reveals himself and forgives Imogen''s supposed killer before Imogen steps forward to reveal herself alive. She and Posthumus are reunited. Belarius reveals that Guiderius and Arviragus are Cymbeline''s lost sons. Guiderius is pardoned for killing Cloten when the full story is told. The prophecy is fulfilled, and Cymbeline magnanimously agrees to pay Rome''s tribute and make peace, ending the play in universal reconciliation.'),
-- Pericles
('Per', 1, 0, 'The poet Gower appears as Chorus and introduces the story, explaining that he has come to sing an ancient tale of Pericles, Prince of Tyre. He sets the scene in Antioch, where King Antiochus has a beautiful daughter and poses a deadly riddle to her many suitors: those who solve it may marry her, but those who fail are executed. Their heads are displayed on the city walls.'),
('Per', 1, 1, 'Pericles arrives in Antioch to attempt the riddle. He reads the riddle and realizes it reveals that Antiochus is committing incest with his own daughter. Understanding he cannot expose a powerful king, Pericles dissembles and asks for time to consider his answer. Antiochus realizes Pericles has understood and sends an assassin, Thaliard, to kill him. Pericles flees Antioch.'),
('Per', 1, 2, 'Back in Tyre, Pericles is tormented by fear that Antiochus will pursue and destroy his kingdom to silence him. His counselor Helicanus advises him to travel and let matters settle. Pericles decides to voyage to Tarsus, leaving Helicanus in charge of Tyre. Thaliard arrives in Tyre but learns Pericles has already departed.'),
('Per', 1, 3, 'Thaliard has arrived in Tyre only to learn that Pericles has sailed away. Since Pericles is already gone, Thaliard concludes he need not pursue the assassination further.'),
('Per', 1, 4, 'Pericles arrives in Tarsus, a city suffering terrible famine. Governor Cleon and his wife Dionyza describe the desperate suffering of their starving people. Pericles offers the grain ships he has brought, relieving the city. Cleon and Dionyza are deeply grateful and promise to shelter Pericles whenever he needs refuge.'),
('Per', 2, 0, 'Gower returns as Chorus to summarize events: Pericles has been warned by Helicanus that Antiochus seeks his life, so he sets sail again. His ship is wrecked in a storm. Gower directs the audience to the coast of Pentapolis.'),
('Per', 2, 1, 'Pericles is cast ashore on the coast of Pentapolis after the shipwreck, the sole survivor. Fishermen find him and share what they know of the land: King Simonides rules Pentapolis, and the next day is a jousting tournament where knights compete for his daughter Thaisa''s hand. The fishermen drag up a net containing a suit of armor, which Pericles recognizes as his father''s — it was in the ship''s cargo. Pericles decides to enter the tournament and borrows a horse from the fishermen.'),
('Per', 2, 2, 'At the tournament in Pentapolis, several knights present their shields with symbolic devices to Thaisa. Pericles arrives in rusty armor, and the court initially looks down on him. However, Thaisa is drawn to him, and he wins the jousting. King Simonides praises his performance.'),
('Per', 2, 3, 'At the celebratory banquet after the tournament, Pericles sits apart, brooding over his misfortunes. Simonides and Thaisa draw him out, and he reveals himself as a prince of Tyre. Thaisa is clearly falling in love with him, and Simonides is pleased.'),
('Per', 2, 4, 'Back in Tyre, Helicanus informs the Tyrian lords that Antiochus and his daughter have been struck dead by divine fire from heaven. The lords pressure Helicanus to assume the throne since Pericles has been absent so long. Helicanus asks them to wait twelve months more; if Pericles has not returned by then, he will comply.'),
('Per', 2, 5, 'King Simonides tests Pericles by showing him a letter from Thaisa declaring her love and her refusal to marry anyone but him. Simonides initially pretends anger, but seeing Pericles''s honorable reaction, reveals his approval. He blesses their union and they are betrothed. Messengers from Tyre arrive to summon Pericles home, now that Antiochus is dead.'),
('Per', 3, 0, 'Gower appears as Chorus to narrate: Pericles and Thaisa, now married, sail for Tyre. Thaisa is pregnant and near her term. A terrible storm strikes the ship at sea.'),
('Per', 3, 1, 'In the storm at sea, Thaisa goes into labor and apparently dies giving birth to a daughter. The superstitious sailors demand her body be cast overboard, believing a corpse at sea causes storms. Pericles sorrowfully agrees. The baby is placed in a waterproof chest with jewels and a letter, and Thaisa is consigned to the sea. Pericles, grief-stricken, names the baby Marina for her birth at sea and orders the ship to make for Tarsus.'),
('Per', 3, 2, 'In Ephesus, the physician Cerimon opens the chest that has washed ashore and finds Thaisa''s apparently dead body. Using his medical skill, he revives her. Thaisa awakes confused and grieving.'),
('Per', 3, 3, 'Pericles arrives in Tarsus and leaves the infant Marina in the care of Cleon and Dionyza, fearing the sea journey will harm the baby. He vows not to cut his hair until Marina is grown and married. Cleon and Dionyza promise to raise Marina as their own child.'),
('Per', 3, 4, 'Thaisa, recovered in Ephesus but believing her husband and infant have perished, decides to become a priestess in the temple of Diana rather than return to her father in Pentapolis.'),
('Per', 4, 0, 'Gower appears as Chorus to narrate the passage of years. Marina has grown into a beautiful and accomplished young woman in Tarsus, excelling Cleon''s own daughter Philoten in every grace. The jealous Dionyza plots to have Marina killed so Philoten will not be overshadowed.'),
('Per', 4, 1, 'Marina mourns at her nurse Lychorida''s grave on the seashore. Dionyza''s servant Leonine approaches to murder her on his mistress''s orders. Before he can act, pirates rush in and carry Marina off to sea. Leonine reports her dead to Dionyza, though he did not actually kill her.'),
('Per', 4, 2, 'The pirates sell Marina to a brothel in Mytilene. The bawd and her servant Boult plan to advertise her to customers. Marina refuses to yield to prostitution and instead speaks so eloquently of virtue that she confounds all who approach her.'),
('Per', 4, 3, 'Cleon is horrified to learn of Dionyza''s plot to murder Marina. Dionyza justifies herself coldly, pointing out that Marina is gone and thus not a rival to Philoten. She has told Pericles that Marina died of natural causes. Cleon is deeply uneasy with the deception.'),
('Per', 4, 4, 'Gower narrates that Pericles has come to visit Marina''s supposed tomb in Tarsus and been devastated by news of her death. He shows Pericles in dumb show sailing away in grief. Gower directs the audience toward Mytilene, where Marina is alive.'),
('Per', 4, 5, 'Two gentlemen of Mytilene emerge from the brothel marveling: Marina has converted them to virtue with her words and made them ashamed to have come there. They resolve to reform their lives.'),
('Per', 4, 6, 'The governor of Mytilene, Lysimachus, visits the brothel in disguise. Marina speaks to him so movingly of virtue and honor that he is shamed and impressed. He leaves money for her and promises to help her. Marina continues to refuse all customers and begins teaching music and needlework to earn honest money.'),
('Per', 5, 0, 'Gower narrates that Pericles, grief-stricken, has sailed aimlessly for months, refusing to eat, wash, or speak. His ship has drifted to Mytilene.'),
('Per', 5, 1, 'The governor Lysimachus boards Pericles''s ship in Mytilene harbor and learns the prince is in deep despair. He suggests Marina might be able to reach him through her musical gifts. Marina is brought aboard and sings to Pericles. Gradually drawing him into conversation, she tells him fragments of her story, and he slowly realizes she is his lost daughter. Their recognition is joyful and overwhelming. That night Pericles hears the music of the spheres in a vision; Diana appears and commands him to go to her temple in Ephesus and tell his story there.'),
('Per', 5, 2, 'Gower narrates that Lysimachus is now betrothed to Marina and the fleet sails for Ephesus to fulfill Diana''s command.'),
('Per', 5, 3, 'At Diana''s temple in Ephesus, Pericles tells the story of his sufferings before the assembled priests and priestesses. Thaisa, now a priestess, recognizes him by his voice and story and reveals herself. They are reunited in overwhelming joy. Cerimon, who revived Thaisa, confirms the full account. Pericles announces that he will rule Pentapolis while Lysimachus and Marina will govern Tyre. The family long separated is at last whole again.'),
('Per', 6, 0, 'Gower delivers the final epilogue, summarizing the fates of the characters: Antiochus and his daughter received divine punishment, while Cleon and Dionyza were burned alive by their own citizens when the truth about Marina was revealed. The virtuous characters — Pericles, Thaisa, Marina, and their allies — have found peace and happiness. Gower bids the audience farewell.'),
-- The Two Noble Kinsmen
('TNK', 0, 0, 'A prologue speaker addresses the audience, explaining that the play is adapted from a tale by Geoffrey Chaucer — "our famous Chaucer" — and asking the audience to receive it favorably. The speaker expresses hope that the new play will honor its noble source and earn approval from the audience.'),
('TNK', 1, 1, 'Duke Theseus of Athens is about to marry Hippolyta, Queen of the Amazons, in a great ceremony. Three queens interrupt the wedding procession, kneeling in the road. They beg Theseus to avenge them: Creon, the tyrant of Thebes, has killed their husbands and refuses to allow their bodies to be buried. After appeals from Hippolyta and her sister Emilia, Theseus agrees to march on Thebes before celebrating his wedding.'),
('TNK', 1, 2, 'In Thebes, two noble cousins and close friends, Palamon and Arcite, discuss the corruption of Creon''s court and debate whether to leave Thebes or stay loyal despite its moral decay. Before they can resolve the question, news arrives that Theseus''s army is attacking. Despite their misgivings about serving Creon, their sense of honor compels them to fight for their city.'),
('TNK', 1, 3, 'Hippolyta and Emilia discuss the friendship between Theseus and his companion Pirithous, which rivals even the love between husband and wife. Emilia describes her own childhood friendship with a girl named Flavina who died young, and declares she may never love a man as deeply as she loved that friend.'),
('TNK', 1, 4, 'The battle is over. The three queens receive the bodies of their husbands for proper burial, thanking Theseus for his intervention. Palamon and Arcite are brought in wounded and unconscious, having fought valorously. Theseus, respecting their bravery, orders them taken to Athens as prisoners rather than left to die.'),
('TNK', 1, 5, 'The three queens and the mourners conduct the funeral rites for their fallen kings in a solemn ceremonial scene. They pray for the souls of the dead and lament their loss.'),
('TNK', 2, 1, 'Palamon and Arcite are imprisoned together in a tower in Athens. Despite their captivity, they find consolation in each other''s company and their unbreakable friendship. They glimpse Emilia, Hippolyta''s sister, walking in the garden below and both instantly fall in love with her. Their friendship immediately fractures as each claims the right to love Emilia, and they quarrel bitterly even while still chained.'),
('TNK', 2, 2, 'Arcite is released from prison by order of Theseus but banished from Athens, while Palamon remains imprisoned. Arcite mourns that he is free but exiled far from Emilia, while Palamon is jailed but close to her. They part with their friendship severely strained.'),
('TNK', 2, 3, 'Arcite, unwilling to leave Athens and Emilia, disguises himself as a country laborer. He encounters countrymen preparing for athletic games before Theseus and volunteers to participate.'),
('TNK', 2, 4, 'Emilia''s Jailer''s Daughter has fallen desperately in love with Palamon. She resolves to free him from prison, help him escape into the forest, and hope he will love her in return.'),
('TNK', 2, 5, 'Arcite wins the athletic contests before Theseus and is taken into the household as a servant to Emilia, unknown to anyone at court. He is assigned as her personal attendant.'),
('TNK', 2, 6, 'The Jailer''s Daughter has freed Palamon and hidden him in the woods. She has brought him food but has begun to worry he will not love her and that she will face punishment for her treachery.'),
('TNK', 3, 1, 'Arcite brings food to the forest where Palamon is hiding. The cousins are briefly glad to see each other but quickly revert to rivalry over Emilia. They agree to meet again secretly so Arcite can bring Palamon armor and weapons, and they will fight a formal duel to determine who wins the right to love Emilia.'),
('TNK', 3, 2, 'The Jailer''s Daughter has lost track of Palamon in the forest. Wandering alone, distressed and rejected, she begins to show signs of losing her sanity. She soliloquizes about her despair and confusion in increasingly fragmented speech.'),
('TNK', 3, 3, 'Arcite returns to the forest with food, drink, and armor for Palamon. Despite their rivalry, they share a meal with the ease of old friends, temporarily setting aside their quarrel. Their bond is evident even as they prepare to fight to the death.'),
('TNK', 3, 4, 'The Jailer''s Daughter wanders the forest in a more deeply distressed state, singing fragments of songs. She has clearly suffered a mental breakdown from unrequited love and fear.'),
('TNK', 3, 5, 'A schoolmaster has organized a morris dance by a group of countrymen and women to perform before Duke Theseus. One of the female dancers has gone missing, and the Jailer''s Daughter, wandering in the woods, is recruited to fill the spot. Theseus, Hippolyta, and Emilia encounter the dance while out hunting and watch with pleasure, rewarding the performers.'),
('TNK', 3, 6, 'Palamon and Arcite have armed each other and are about to fight their duel when Theseus and his hunting party discover them. Theseus orders both men executed for violating Arcite''s banishment and Palamon''s prison escape. Hippolyta, Emilia, and Pirithous intercede for their lives. Theseus decrees instead that both cousins must leave Athens, gather three knights each, and return in one month to fight a formal tournament; the winner marries Emilia, the loser and his knights are executed.'),
('TNK', 4, 1, 'The Jailer''s Daughter has become fully mad, singing and raving about Palamon and ships and the sea. Her father and the Wooer who loves her try to tend to her. A doctor advises the Wooer to pretend to be Palamon in order to cure her through her delusion.'),
('TNK', 4, 2, 'Emilia studies portrait miniatures of both Palamon and Arcite, trying to decide between them. She finds virtues in each and cannot choose. She is told the tournament is about to begin and the cousins have arrived with their knights.'),
('TNK', 4, 3, 'The Wooer, following the doctor''s advice, has been impersonating Palamon to the Jailer''s Daughter. She seems to respond to the treatment. The doctor instructs the Wooer to go further in the role to cure her completely.'),
('TNK', 5, 1, 'Before the tournament, the rivals make their devotions. Arcite prays to Mars, the god of war, for victory in battle. Palamon prays to Venus, the goddess of love, to grant him Emilia. Emilia prays to Diana to let her remain unmarried or, if she must marry, to grant her the man who loves her most truly. Each receives a divine sign that their prayer has been heard.'),
('TNK', 5, 2, 'The Jailer''s Daughter is much improved through the Wooer''s treatment. The doctor advises the Wooer to continue the pretense and ultimately sleep with her as part of the cure. The Jailer reluctantly consents to the unusual remedy.'),
('TNK', 5, 3, 'The tournament takes place offstage while Emilia waits, unable to watch. She receives reports of the fighting. When the outcome is announced, Arcite has won and Palamon''s knights are condemned to die. Emilia is deeply distressed, though she will marry Arcite.'),
('TNK', 5, 4, 'Palamon and his knights have their heads on the block awaiting execution when a messenger arrives: Arcite has been thrown from his horse in a sudden accident and is dying. A mortally injured Arcite is brought in and, in a final gesture of friendship, yields Emilia to Palamon before dying. Palamon mourns his friend and cousin with deep grief even in the moment of his own salvation. Theseus reflects on the strange workings of the gods and invites everyone to give thanks.'),
('TNK', 6, 0, 'An epilogue speaker addresses the audience, acknowledging that the play has been a mixed experience — some sad, some comical — and asking for the audience''s approval and applause. The speaker gently jokes that the audience must have enjoyed it if they did not fall asleep, and hopes the play has pleased them.'),
-- The Taming of the Shrew
('Shr', -1, 1, 'A lord returning from hunting finds Christopher Sly, a drunken tinker, passed out outside a tavern. The lord decides to play an elaborate practical joke: Sly is carried to the lord''s house, dressed in fine clothes, and surrounded by servants who insist he is a nobleman who has been mad for years. Sly awakes confused and the servants patiently maintain the fiction. A page is dressed as a lady to play Sly''s wife. A traveling company of players arrives and will perform a play for Sly''s entertainment.'),
('Shr', -1, 2, 'Sly, now half-convinced he may indeed be a lord, settles in with his supposed wife (the disguised page) to watch the play. The lord''s servants encourage him to enjoy himself. Sly makes a few comments before gradually fading from the action as the inner play, the main story of Petruchio and Katherine, begins.'),
('Shr', 1, 1, 'Lucentio arrives in Padua to study and immediately falls in love with Bianca, the younger daughter of wealthy Baptista. However, Baptista announces that Bianca cannot marry until his elder daughter Katherine is wed first — and Katherine is notorious for her violent, shrewish temper. Lucentio devises a plan: he will disguise himself as a Latin tutor to gain access to Bianca. His servant Tranio will impersonate him in public. Meanwhile, two other suitors for Bianca, Gremio and Hortensio, agree to cooperate in finding a husband for Katherine so Bianca will be available.'),
('Shr', 1, 2, 'Petruchio, a bold gentleman from Verona, arrives in Padua with his servant Grumio seeking a rich wife. His friend Hortensio tells him about Katherine and her great dowry, glossing over her difficult temperament. Petruchio is not deterred and is eager to woo her. Hortensio plans to disguise himself as a music teacher to court Bianca. Gremio proposes that Lucentio (actually Tranio in disguise) also compete for Bianca''s hand.'),
('Shr', 2, 1, 'Petruchio meets Katherine for the first time and the two engage in a rapid, combative battle of wits and wordplay. Katherine physically attacks Petruchio at one point, but he is completely unfazed. He announces to Baptista that Katherine has agreed to marry him, which she heatedly denies. Nevertheless, Baptista is so relieved to have a suitor that he accepts Petruchio''s account and fixes the wedding for Sunday.'),
('Shr', 3, 1, 'Both Lucentio (disguised as Cambio the Latin tutor) and Hortensio (disguised as Licio the music teacher) compete for Bianca''s attention during their lessons. Lucentio secretly conveys his love through his Latin translations and Bianca responds encouragingly, while Hortensio grows suspicious and jealous.'),
('Shr', 3, 2, 'On the wedding day, Petruchio arrives late and dressed in absurd, mismatched clothes. He behaves outrageously throughout the ceremony, shouts at the priest, and kisses Katherine noisily. Immediately after the wedding he announces they must leave at once for his house in Verona, refusing to let Katherine attend her own wedding feast. He drags her away despite her protests, declaring that she is his property now. Lucentio''s servant Tranio continues his masquerade and continues pursuing Bianca.'),
('Shr', 4, 1, 'Petruchio brings Katherine to his house, where he continues his campaign to tame her by systematically denying her food and sleep under the pretense of caring for her welfare — the food is not good enough, the bed is not made properly enough. He behaves as though everything he does is for her sake while keeping her in a state of exhausted confusion.'),
('Shr', 4, 2, 'Hortensio discovers Lucentio (as Cambio the tutor) and Bianca flirting and gives up his pursuit of her in disgust. He decides to marry a widow who has long loved him. Tranio, still impersonating Lucentio, continues to woo Bianca on behalf of his master and arranges for an elderly pedant to impersonate Lucentio''s father Vincentio.'),
('Shr', 4, 3, 'Petruchio continues Katherine''s "taming." A haberdasher brings a fashionable cap and a tailor brings a new gown, but Petruchio rejects both items as unsuitable, despite Katherine''s protests that she loves them. He tells her they will travel to her father''s house but dressed as they are. Katherine is frustrated but beginning to accommodate his moods.'),
('Shr', 4, 4, 'The false Vincentio (the pedant) convinces Baptista of his identity and a marriage contract between Bianca and Lucentio is agreed upon. Tranio keeps up his impersonation with skill. The real Lucentio arranges a secret marriage with Bianca.'),
('Shr', 4, 5, 'Petruchio and Katherine travel toward Padua. Petruchio capriciously insists the sun is the moon, then reverses himself, and Katherine, worn down, agrees with whatever he says. When they meet the real old Vincentio on the road, Petruchio insists he is a beautiful young woman and Katherine plays along, greeting him as such. Petruchio then corrects everyone cheerfully. Katherine''s resistance has broken and she now accommodates his whims.'),
('Shr', 5, 1, 'The real Vincentio arrives in Padua and is startled to encounter the pedant impersonating him. Lucentio''s deceptions begin to unravel as Vincentio confronts Tranio. The scene grows chaotic until Lucentio and Bianca return from their secret wedding and confess all. Vincentio is angry but gradually mollified. Baptista is astonished by the trick played on him.'),
('Shr', 5, 2, 'At a festive dinner, Petruchio proposes a wager: each husband will send for his wife and the one whose wife comes most readily is the winner. Lucentio and Hortensio''s wives refuse to come immediately. Katherine arrives at once when Petruchio calls. He then sends her to bring the other wives, and when they gather, Katherine delivers a long speech explaining that a wife owes duty and submission to her husband just as a subject owes it to a ruler. Petruchio wins the wager and the other husbands are amazed at Katherine''s transformation.'),
-- The Two Gentlemen of Verona
('TGV', 1, 1, 'Valentine departs Verona for Milan to seek adventure and experience at the court, over the protests of his friend Proteus, who is too much in love with Julia to leave. After Valentine departs, Proteus''s servant Speed arrives and reports that Proteus''s love letter to Julia was delivered. Proteus anxiously awaits a response from Julia.'),
('TGV', 1, 2, 'Julia discusses love with her waiting-woman Lucetta, who has been given Proteus''s letter. Julia pretends to be offended by the letter and tears it up, but after Lucetta leaves, she anxiously collects the pieces and reads them, revealing her deep love for Proteus.'),
('TGV', 1, 3, 'Proteus''s father Antonio has decided to send his son to Milan to join Valentine at the duke''s court, believing Proteus needs worldly experience. Proteus is devastated at having to leave Julia but cannot tell his father why. He receives a letter from Valentine (which he pretends is from the duke) as cover for his distress, and reluctantly prepares to depart.'),
('TGV', 2, 1, 'Valentine arrives in Milan and his servant Speed teases him about being in love — Valentine has fallen for Silvia, the duke''s daughter. Silvia herself enters and asks Valentine to write a love letter on her behalf to a secret admirer, then gives the finished letter back to him. Speed explains to his bewildered master that Silvia has given him a letter addressed to himself as a clever way of declaring her own love.'),
('TGV', 2, 2, 'Proteus and Julia part sorrowfully in Verona. They exchange rings and vow eternal fidelity. Proteus promises to return and Julia promises to wait for him.'),
('TGV', 2, 3, 'Proteus''s comic servant Launce sets off for Milan with his dog Crab. Launce delivers a long, affectionate monologue about leaving home, acting out his family''s tearful farewell himself since his dog showed no emotion whatsoever at the parting.'),
('TGV', 2, 4, 'Proteus arrives in Milan and is reunited with Valentine, who confides his love for Silvia and their plan to elope. Silvia enters and Proteus meets her — and immediately falls in love with her, betraying both his vow to Julia and his friendship to Valentine. He resolves to undermine Valentine''s plans.'),
('TGV', 2, 5, 'Speed and Launce meet and banter comically about the news that their masters are both in love. Launce confides that he too is in love with a milkmaid of dubious character and reads a comic catalogue of her faults and virtues.'),
('TGV', 2, 6, 'Proteus delivers a soliloquy laying out his treacherous plan: he will reveal Valentine''s elopement scheme to the Duke of Milan, getting Valentine banished, and then pursue Silvia himself — betraying both his friend and Julia.'),
('TGV', 2, 7, 'Julia, unable to bear the separation from Proteus, persuades Lucetta to help her travel to Milan in disguise as a boy. Lucetta warns her against the venture, but Julia is resolute.'),
('TGV', 3, 1, 'Proteus warns the Duke of Valentine''s elopement plan and reveals that Valentine has a rope ladder hidden under his cloak. The Duke pretends to seek Valentine''s advice, tricks him into revealing the ladder, and banishes him from Milan. Proteus hypocritically offers consolation. Launce enters with a comic letter about his own love affair.'),
('TGV', 3, 2, 'Thurio, the Duke''s preferred suitor for Silvia, is desperate because Silvia still dotes on the banished Valentine. The Duke and Proteus scheme together: Proteus will slander Valentine to Silvia and help Thurio woo her with music and poetry. Proteus agrees, continuing his treachery.'),
('TGV', 4, 1, 'Valentine and Speed are captured by a band of outlaws in the forest. The outlaws, who are themselves gentlemen banished for various offenses, are impressed by Valentine''s manner and offer him the position of their leader. Valentine accepts, though Silvia remains on his mind.'),
('TGV', 4, 2, 'Proteus and Thurio serenade Silvia under her window. Julia has arrived in Milan disguised as the page Sebastian and witnesses Proteus wooing Silvia on Thurio''s behalf — and on his own. Silvia rejects Proteus and scorns him for abandoning Julia. Julia, watching, is heartbroken but impressed by Silvia''s faithfulness to Valentine. Silvia gives Proteus her portrait.'),
('TGV', 4, 3, 'Sir Eglamour, a loyal and honorable knight, agrees to help Silvia escape from Milan and reach the banished Valentine in the forest. Eglamour had sworn a vow of chastity after his own love died and is willing to help Silvia out of his own sense of honor.'),
('TGV', 4, 4, 'Launce enters carrying Crab the dog, whom he has had to rescue from disgrace multiple times at court. Julia, as Sebastian the page, enters Proteus''s service. Proteus sends Sebastian/Julia to deliver the portrait and a ring to Silvia — the ring is the one Julia herself gave Proteus as a love token. Julia goes, overcome with grief at delivering her own ring to her rival.'),
('TGV', 5, 1, 'Silvia and Sir Eglamour flee Milan into the forest. They move quickly, hoping to avoid pursuit.'),
('TGV', 5, 2, 'The Duke discovers Silvia is gone. He, Thurio, and Proteus organize a pursuit into the forest. Julia, still disguised as Sebastian, accompanies them.'),
('TGV', 5, 3, 'Silvia, fleeing Milan to find Valentine, is captured in the forest by the band of outlaws. Her companion Eglamour escapes by outrunning them, leaving Silvia alone. The outlaws note that their companions Moyses and Valerius are pursuing Eglamour, and they order Silvia taken to the west end of the wood where their captain dwells. A First Outlaw reassures the frightened Silvia that their captain is honorable and will not harm her. Silvia endures her capture with patience, sustained by her love for Valentine — unaware that the outlaws'' captain is Valentine himself.'),
('TGV', 5, 4, 'In the forest, Valentine overhears Proteus attempting to force himself on Silvia, who resists him. Valentine steps out and confronts Proteus''s treachery. Proteus immediately repents. Valentine, in a gesture of excessive male friendship, offers to give Silvia up to Proteus. Julia, still disguised, faints. When revived, she reveals herself, and Proteus realizes he loves Julia after all. The Duke and Thurio arrive; Thurio shows himself a coward when Valentine challenges him, and the Duke, impressed, restores Valentine''s favor and gives him Silvia. The outlaws are pardoned and all return to Milan.'),
-- Troilus and Cressida
('Tro', 0, 0, 'An armed Prologue appears and describes the background: the Greeks have brought a fleet of ships to Troy to recover Helen, who was taken by Paris. The Prologue describes the Trojan gates and the encamped Greek army, setting the tone of the play as martial and ironic. The Prologue notes it does not decide whether the audience''s expectations will be met, offering no guarantees of a satisfying story.'),
('Tro', 1, 1, 'Troilus, a young Trojan prince, is so obsessed with love for Cressida that he cannot bring himself to fight in the Trojan War. He enlists the help of Pandarus, Cressida''s uncle, to act as go-between and advance his suit. Pandarus teases him and procrastinates. Aeneas enters with news of the day''s fighting, which prompts Troilus to put on his armor.'),
('Tro', 1, 2, 'Cressida and Pandarus watch the Trojan heroes return from battle. Pandarus praises each warrior, especially Troilus, while Cressida makes sharp, ironic replies. After Pandarus leaves, Cressida reveals in a soliloquy that she is already in love with Troilus but plans to conceal it, knowing that men prize most what they must work to win.'),
('Tro', 1, 3, 'In the Greek camp, Agamemnon and the Greek leaders discuss why after seven years they have not taken Troy. Ulysses delivers a long, famous speech arguing that the Greek army''s failure comes from a breakdown of hierarchy and order — a theme called "degree." He singles out Achilles and his companion Patroclus, who mock the generals, as the cause of the rot. Ajax''s servant Thersites is mentioned as another source of contemptuous disorder. A Trojan herald, Aeneas, arrives with a challenge from Hector: he will fight any Greek champion in single combat. Ulysses proposes by lottery to select Ajax rather than Achilles, to provoke Achilles''s jealousy.'),
('Tro', 2, 1, 'The foul-mouthed, bitter Thersites rails at Ajax and curses everyone around him. Ajax beats him. Achilles and Patroclus look on with amusement. Thersites''s abusive commentary establishes the satirical tone of the play''s Greek camp scenes.'),
('Tro', 2, 2, 'The Trojan leaders debate whether to return Helen to the Greeks to end the war. Hector argues logically that Helen is not worth the blood and destruction her retention has caused. Troilus and Paris argue that honor and the value they have placed on Helen demand she be kept. Cassandra rushes in prophesying Troy''s destruction. Hector ultimately agrees to continue the war despite his own logical argument, deferring to a notion of honor — a moment of fatal irrationality.'),
('Tro', 2, 3, 'In the Greek camp, Thersites continues his scabrous commentary. Ulysses and the Greek leaders attempt to shame Achilles into fighting by treating Ajax with great honor and ignoring Achilles. Achilles is puzzled and irritated by the sudden cold shoulder but will not reveal the real reason he won''t fight, which is a secret oath to Hecuba and his love for Troilus''s sister Polyxena.'),
('Tro', 3, 1, 'Pandarus visits Paris and Helen and asks Paris to make excuses for Troilus''s absence at supper. The scene shows Helen as charming but shallow and Paris as besotted. The scene''s lightness contrasts with the weight of the war being fought over her.'),
('Tro', 3, 2, 'Pandarus brings Troilus and Cressida together for the first time. Both declare their love passionately. Cressida, despite her earlier resolve to conceal her feelings, yields completely. Pandarus joins their hands and witnesses their vows of fidelity. All three swear oaths: Troilus that his name will be synonymous with faithfulness, Cressida that hers will be synonymous with falseness if she proves false, and Pandarus that all go-betweens will be called pandars if he is a bad one. The irony is heavy since the audience knows these outcomes.'),
('Tro', 3, 3, 'Calchas, the Trojan priest who has defected to the Greeks, asks Agamemnon to exchange a Trojan prisoner for his daughter Cressida in Troy. The exchange is agreed. Ulysses orchestrates further humiliation of Achilles by having the Greek leaders pass him without acknowledgment. Ulysses then delivers a speech to Achilles on the nature of reputation and time — beauty, valor, and worth must be constantly renewed or they are forgotten. Achilles remains unmoved. Ajax grows insufferably proud at being chosen as Hector''s opponent.'),
('Tro', 4, 1, 'Paris and Diomedes escort the Trojan Antenor, the prisoner to be exchanged for Cressida. They meet Aeneas, who delivers the news that Cressida is to be surrendered to the Greeks. Aeneas and Paris exchange some sharp words about the nature of this exchange.'),
('Tro', 4, 2, 'Troilus and Cressida wake together after their first night. They are interrupted by Aeneas who brings the news of Cressida''s exchange. Troilus is devastated. Pandarus breaks the news to Cressida, who is distraught. The sudden external intrusion destroys the private world they have made.'),
('Tro', 4, 3, 'Paris and his company come to take Cressida away. Troilus is given just a brief time with Cressida before she must go.'),
('Tro', 4, 4, 'Troilus and Cressida say a desperate, anguished farewell. They exchange tokens — Cressida gives Troilus her sleeve. Troilus makes Cressida swear fidelity; Cressida acknowledges she does not fully trust herself. Diomedes arrives to take her to the Greek camp. Troilus and Diomedes exchange tense, veiled threats over Cressida''s honor.'),
('Tro', 4, 5, 'Cressida arrives in the Greek camp. The Greek leaders greet her with kisses, which Ulysses refuses; he reads her character as a flirt and is contemptuous of her. Ajax and Hector meet for their combat, which quickly ends without a decisive result because Hector refuses to fight Ajax, who is his cousin, seriously. Hector graciously visits the Greek camp. Achilles surveys Hector''s body with chilling calculation, identifying where he intends to wound and kill him. Troilus asks Ulysses where Calchas''s tent is, knowing Diomedes will be there with Cressida.'),
('Tro', 5, 1, 'Achilles receives a letter from Hecuba, reminding him of his oath and his love for Polyxena. He resolves again not to fight. Thersites delivers scurrilous commentary on all the Greek heroes. Patroclus urges Achilles to fight to restore his reputation.'),
('Tro', 5, 2, 'Troilus and Ulysses secretly watch Cressida meet with Diomedes outside Calchas''s tent. Cressida flirts and finally gives Diomedes the sleeve Troilus gave her, accepting a promise of assignation. Troilus is destroyed, witnessing the proof of her infidelity. He splits the experience in his mind, struggling to reconcile the Cressida he loved with the one he sees. He resolves to find and kill Diomedes in battle.'),
('Tro', 5, 3, 'Hector prepares to go into battle despite the pleading of Andromache, Cassandra, and Priam, who all have terrible premonitions. Cassandra prophesies Hector''s death. Troilus also urges Hector to fight, having resolved on violence against Diomedes. Hector refuses to be dissuaded.'),
('Tro', 5, 4, 'On the battlefield, Thersites observes the fighting with his usual contemptuous commentary. He encounters Margarelon, an illegitimate son of Priam, who challenges him, and Thersites flees, denying he is even a worthy opponent.'),
('Tro', 5, 5, 'The Greeks rally after Patroclus has been killed by Hector. The news inflames Achilles, who immediately vows to return to battle and kill Hector. Diomedes sends Cressida''s sleeve as a trophy from Troilus''s horse.'),
('Tro', 5, 6, 'Troilus fights furiously on the battlefield, driven by grief and rage. He encounters Ajax, and they battle briefly. He looks for Diomedes but they are separated by the fighting.'),
('Tro', 5, 7, 'Achilles has rejoined the battle with his Myrmidons, a company of soldiers. He encounters Hector and finds him unarmed and weary after a long day''s fighting. Achilles has his Myrmidons surround and kill Hector — not in fair combat but by ambush. This is presented as a dishonorable act.'),
('Tro', 5, 8, 'Achilles ties Hector''s body to his horse''s tail and drags it around the battlefield in a deliberate act of desecration, shouting that Troy has lost its glory.'),
('Tro', 5, 9, 'Agamemnon receives word that Achilles has killed Hector. The Greek leaders celebrate. The mood is triumphant for the Greeks.'),
('Tro', 5, 10, 'Troilus brings the news of Hector''s death to the Trojans. He delivers a speech of bitter grief and rage, rejecting the possibility of peace and vowing relentless revenge against Achilles. He predicts the ruin of Troy.'),
('Tro', 5, 11, 'Pandarus tries to speak to Troilus but is bitterly dismissed. The play ends with Pandarus delivering a cynical epilogue addressing the audience directly, claiming fellowship with the diseased and decrepit, and bequeathing his illnesses to them — a deliberately unsatisfying and satirical conclusion.'),
-- The Merry Wives of Windsor
('Wiv', 1, 1, 'Justice Shallow arrives in Windsor complaining that Sir John Falstaff has wronged him and he intends to complain to the Star Chamber. Falstaff brazenly does not deny his various offenses. The scene also introduces Slender, Shallow''s foolish nephew, as a potential suitor for Anne Page, daughter of the respectable Windsor citizen Master Page. Master and Mistress Page welcome everyone to dinner.'),
('Wiv', 1, 2, 'The local curate Sir Hugh Evans speaks to Slender''s servant Simple, sending him to Mistress Quickly with a message supporting Slender''s suit for Anne Page''s hand.'),
('Wiv', 1, 3, 'Falstaff, short of money, devises a scheme: he has noticed that Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, wives of wealthy Windsor citizens, seem to look at him favorably and control their husbands'' purse strings. He writes identical love letters to both women and sends his follower Pistol to deliver them. Pistol refuses on grounds of honor, so Falstaff dismisses both Pistol and Nym. The two men revenge themselves by informing the husbands of Falstaff''s letters.'),
('Wiv', 1, 4, 'Mistress Quickly, who works as housekeeper for the French doctor Caius, meets with Simple, who carries Evans''s letter supporting Slender''s suit for Anne. Doctor Caius discovers the intrusion and is furious that Evans is meddling. He writes a letter challenging Evans to a duel. Fenton, another young suitor for Anne, also visits Quickly seeking her help.'),
('Wiv', 2, 1, 'Mistress Page and Mistress Ford compare their identical letters from Falstaff and are indignant. They resolve to trick and humiliate him together. Ford, warned by Pistol, is furiously jealous and resolves to investigate, disguising himself as a man named Brook to approach Falstaff. Page, warned by Nym, is more sanguine about his wife''s faithfulness.'),
('Wiv', 2, 2, 'Falstaff entertains Pistol and Nym''s disloyal behavior with contempt. Mistress Quickly arrives and brings encouraging messages from both Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, telling Falstaff each woman is secretly interested in him. Ford arrives in his disguise as Brook and pays Falstaff to woo Mistress Ford on his behalf, claiming he himself wants to seduce her after Falstaff has prepared the way. Falstaff, delighted, reveals he already has an assignation with Mistress Ford and will tell Brook everything afterward. Ford is beside himself with jealous fury.'),
('Wiv', 2, 3, 'Doctor Caius waits at the appointed dueling spot for Evans but the Host of the Garter Inn and the others have misdirected both men to different locations, preventing the duel. Caius is furious but the situation is largely defused. Fenton speaks privately with the Host about his pursuit of Anne Page.'),
('Wiv', 3, 1, 'Evans also waits at the wrong dueling location, nervously singing fragments of songs while he waits. The Host and others arrive and broker a peace between Evans and Caius, who are annoyed but reconciled.'),
('Wiv', 3, 2, 'Ford, still disguised as Brook, watches everyone''s movements. He intercepts the merry wives and overhears enough to increase his suspicion. Master Page and the others discuss the merits of the various suitors for Anne: Page favors Slender, Mistress Page favors Caius, and Anne herself secretly loves Fenton.'),
('Wiv', 3, 3, 'Falstaff arrives at the Ford house for his assignation with Mistress Ford. The women have prepared their trick: a large laundry basket is waiting. When Ford arrives in a jealous rage, Falstaff is hidden in the basket under the dirty laundry. Mistress Ford orders the basket of linen to be carried to the laundress and dumped into the Thames. Ford searches the house and finds nothing. When Ford tells the story later, the wives gleefully reveal what they have done, and Falstaff, soaking wet and miserable, is furious.'),
('Wiv', 3, 4, 'Fenton and Anne Page speak privately. Anne is devoted to Fenton and they plan for their future together despite her parents'' opposition. Shallow and Slender arrive and continue their unsuccessful courtship. Page and Mistress Quickly also appear. The scene shows the tangled suitor plot alongside the Falstaff trick.'),
('Wiv', 3, 5, 'Falstaff, recovered and dried off, is visited again by Mistress Quickly who brings another supposed invitation from Mistress Ford. Falstaff agrees to try again. Ford as Brook returns and Falstaff tells him about the laundry basket humiliation while assuring him the affair will be back on track.'),
('Wiv', 4, 1, 'In a brief comic scene, Evans quizzes young William Page in his Latin lessons in front of Mistress Quickly, whose comments on the vocabulary provide bawdy wordplay. The scene is largely a self-contained comic interlude.'),
('Wiv', 4, 2, 'Falstaff comes again to the Ford house. When Ford returns early, the wives disguise Falstaff in the clothes of the old fat woman of Brentford, a figure Ford hates. Ford beats the disguised Falstaff soundly before throwing him out. Searching the house again, Ford finds nothing. The wives tell their husbands the full truth of their tricks and all agree to devise one final, public humiliation for Falstaff.'),
('Wiv', 4, 3, 'The Host of the Garter is alarmed that his horses have been stolen. Caius and Evans have conspired in this revenge for the Host''s meddling in their duel.'),
('Wiv', 4, 4, 'The wives, husbands, and others plan the final trick for Falstaff: he is to come to Windsor Great Park at midnight, disguised as Herne the Hunter, a legendary ghost. Children dressed as fairies will torment and pinch him. Meanwhile, each of the three rival suitors is given instructions for a secret elopement with Anne that will happen during the confusion, but each set of instructions is different and only one — Fenton''s — is real.'),
('Wiv', 4, 5, 'Falstaff is visited by Quickly with the invitation to Windsor Park and agrees to come. Simple arrives to ask about Slender''s affairs. The Host learns more about his stolen horses.'),
('Wiv', 4, 6, 'Fenton secretly arranges with the Host for a proper clergyman to be present that night to marry him and Anne. The Host agrees to help.'),
('Wiv', 5, 1, 'Ford, as Brook, visits Falstaff who confirms he will go to Windsor Park that night as Herne the Hunter. Ford plans to be there.'),
('Wiv', 5, 2, 'Page and the others arrive at Windsor Park early and prepare for the trick. Page reminds Slender to take a boy in white for his elopement; unknown to Page, it is actually a boy dressed as Anne.'),
('Wiv', 5, 3, 'Mistress Page, Doctor Caius, and Mistress Quickly finalize their arrangement. Caius is to take a boy in green — also not actually Anne.'),
('Wiv', 5, 4, 'Evans and a group of children disguised as fairies prepare to ambush Falstaff.'),
('Wiv', 5, 5, 'Falstaff arrives in Windsor Park wearing deer antlers as Herne the Hunter. Mistress Page and Mistress Ford meet him. The disguised children rush out, pinching and burning Falstaff with candles and tormenting him with fairy speeches. Falstaff is exposed and utterly humiliated when the "fairies" reveal themselves as his neighbors. The elopements are revealed: Slender has run off with a boy in boy''s clothes, and Caius with another boy — Anne, with Fenton''s help, has slipped away and married him. Page and Mistress Page are each put out but ultimately accept the marriage. Ford reveals himself as Brook to Falstaff, and everyone laughs. Falstaff is invited to laugh at himself and come to dinner.')
;
